WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:18.900
Tim Ferriss: Arthur, so nice to have you here.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:19.900
Thanks for joining.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
Arthur Brooks: Thank you, Tim.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:21.900
It's great to be with you.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.900
I've been looking forward to this.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:23.900
Tim Ferriss: Me too.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:24.900
Arthur Brooks: I feel like we've been looking
forward to meeting and we've been warming

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:25.900
up for this for a long time.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:26.900
Tim Ferriss: We have.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:27.900
Arthur Brooks: We have so many friends in
common.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:28.900
Tim Ferriss: We have a lot of friends in common.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.251
I have read your prior work, From Strength
to Strength, excellent book recommended to

00:00:32.251 --> 00:00:34.120
me by Peter Attia.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.390
People may know him as Dr. Peter Attia, good
friend, proud Texan also now.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.520
Arthur Brooks: Where all the cool kids live,
we're all of Texas.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.440
Tim Ferriss: Where all the cool kids are.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:54.750
And I have more questions than we will have
time, but I'm hoping to really explore broadly,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:01:00.980
and I wanted to start with something that
I thought would pique the interest of many

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.299
people listening, and that is the reverse
bucket list.

00:01:05.299 --> 00:01:09.090
Is this something you still do and what does
it entail?

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.010
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, the reverse bucket list
is something I do.

00:01:12.010 --> 00:01:16.829
Oh, boy, do I ever do it and it took me too
long to figure this one out.

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:19.810
When I was 50, I'm 59 years old.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.880
When I was 50, I found my bucket list from
when I was 40.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.530
It was a classic thing, and on my birthday,
I would make a list of my desires and my ambitions,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:34.220
and I would imagine myself, visualize myself
consuming or experiencing these things and

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.710
it would fire me up and make me well feel
like a loser, quite frankly, because it was

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:42.590
lowering my sense of satisfaction I subsequently
found out.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:47.939
I looked at that list from when I was 40,
and I checked everything off that list and

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:49.710
I was less happy —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.280
Arthur Brooks: — at 50 than I was at 40.

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.210
And so I thought, I'm a social scientist so
I thought to myself, obviously I'm doing something

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:55.210
wrong.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.369
What am I doing wrong?

00:01:56.369 --> 00:01:59.119
And basically this is the problem.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:03.840
This is a neurophysiological problem and a
psychological problem all rolled into one

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.509
handy package.

00:02:05.509 --> 00:02:11.150
I was making the mistake of thinking that
my satisfaction would come by having more.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.550
And the truth of the matter is that lasting
and stable satisfaction, which doesn't wear

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:22.480
off in a minute, comes when you understand
that your satisfaction is your haves divided

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.530
by your wants.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:25.530
Haves divided by wants.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.530
That's the model.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.180
So it's like I'll slow down because I know
people watching are like, "I'm going to write

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.180
that down."

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:31.180
Haves divided by wants.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:36.879
You can increase your satisfaction temporarily
and inefficiently by having more, or permanently

00:02:36.879 --> 00:02:38.959
and securely by wanting less.

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:42.319
I thought to myself, so what does that mean?

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:44.510
And the answer to that, what does that mean?

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.510
Is I need a reverse bucket list.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.950
Not that I'm not going to get nice things
in my life, but I'm going to be consciously

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:55.840
detached from them by going through the exercise
of writing them down and crossing them out.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.409
So on my birthday now, and my 59th birthday
a couple of months ago, I wrote down all my

00:03:01.409 --> 00:03:09.390
ridiculous ambitions and desires of money
or whatever, power, ambition, admiration,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.080
all these things that I want from other people
that we all want because we're human and Mother

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.970
Nature wires us to accumulate the rewards
that'll help us survive and pass our genes,

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:17.970
I got it.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:22.349
I know how evolutionary psychology works,
and I know that these things are going to

00:03:22.349 --> 00:03:26.379
occur to me as natural goals, but I do not
want to be owned by them.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:27.379
I want to manage them.

00:03:27.379 --> 00:03:32.340
I don't want them to be like phantasms in
my limbic system managing me.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:37.840
I want to move the experience of these ambitions
to my prefrontal cortex, which is my executive

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:41.970
manager, the bumper of tissue right behind
my forehead.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.040
And the way that you do that is by looking
at each one of these ambitions and saying,

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.579
"Maybe I get it and maybe I don't, but I'm
going to cross it out as an attachment."

00:03:51.579 --> 00:03:52.870
And I'm telling you, Tim, I'm free.

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.599
Tim Ferriss: And so you find that to work.

00:03:54.599 --> 00:03:56.480
Arthur Brooks: Oh, it works.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:00.360
It does, not because you don't care about
it, but because you're not attached to it

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.379
in the same way.

00:04:01.379 --> 00:04:06.940
You've made the decision to have it not be
a rootless desire, a ghost in your head.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:10.829
You've made it into something that you will
consciously manage by literally experiencing

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:12.920
that ambition in a different part of your
brain.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.080
Tim Ferriss: So you go through this exercise,
so you write down whatever it is, receive

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:22.440
such and such an honor, sell so many copies
of book X, whatever these are.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.590
Arthur Brooks: All the stuff that guys like
you and me do.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:30.410
Tim Ferriss: And then you have 37-inch arms
in your case possibly.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:31.780
You're incredibly fit.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.431
I'm not hitting on you, I'm just saying I
aspire to —

00:04:34.431 --> 00:04:35.960
Arthur Brooks: You're a beautiful man too.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:41.900
Tim Ferriss: Well, we go to the same stylist
for our haircuts, but I would say we will

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:45.780
maybe get to self-care and physical practice
later.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.650
You go through this exercise, you write those
things down, you cross them out in an idol

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:52.820
worship, sleight-of-hand move.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:53.990
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:05:02.650
Tim Ferriss: And then following that, I'm
curious when is if there is the spark of this

00:05:02.650 --> 00:05:10.190
desire, whatever it might be, do you have
self-talk or something that acts as an interrupt?

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:11.250
Arthur Brooks: I keep the list.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.250
Tim Ferriss: You keep the list.

00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:14.670
Arthur Brooks: I keep the list and I go back
and I look at the list just like I would look

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:18.910
at my bucket list later every six months or
every year, every 10 years, whatever it happened

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:19.910
to be.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.220
I go back and look at my reverse bucket list
and say, "Am I living up to this?

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:23.830
Am I truly conscious?"

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.400
Tim Ferriss: "Am I living up to not being
attached?"

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.810
Arthur Brooks: "Am I practicing the detachment
that I committed myself to?"

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:31.310
And the truth is usually, yeah, kind of.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:33.140
I mean, the phantasm will be back.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:37.740
It will be flying around in your head again,
but you brought it to your prefrontal cortex

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.759
for a reason and your prefrontal cortex is
an adult and will govern the children.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:47.139
But sometimes you have to remind yourself,
"Remember, look, I mean you're an adult here,"

00:05:47.139 --> 00:05:50.030
and you look back on it and you say, and it's
funny.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:54.009
Actually, it's pretty amusing when you look
at it and you say, "Oh, I can't believe that

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:56.229
thing is bugging me again."

00:05:56.229 --> 00:05:58.090
But you're not governed by it in the same
way.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.270
You know what I actually put on my list?

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:00.270
I was reading.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:04.940
Tim Ferriss: Just for clarity, so it's okay
to have the goal and to have it as a target

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:05.940
and plan for it.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.070
Let's say selling X number of copies, but
not to have that hungry ghost attachment to

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.070
it.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.120
Arthur Brooks: It's nothing more than an intention.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:16.210
Intention is fine, but attachment is bad.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:18.509
And this is what the Dalai Lama talks about
too.

00:06:18.509 --> 00:06:20.580
He talks about intention without attachment.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.360
There's a word in sailing, the rhumb line.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:27.460
In Spanish, rumbo, which is a lot more common
in normal everyday speech.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.949
And what it means is it's the intention of
your voyage.

00:06:31.949 --> 00:06:35.960
You have to have that even though you're not
going to be true to it, because if you don't

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:39.449
know you're just going to be going in circles,
you'll just be, "I don't know, I've been out

00:06:39.449 --> 00:06:40.600
to sea for a long time.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.600
Who knows where I am?"

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.990
It'll be like one of your vacations.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:47.639
It's like, "I don't know where I am, I'm just
doing nothing, man," which is, it can be therapeutically

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:52.210
important, but it's actually not the way that
you get from Europe to the Americas.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:54.210
Arthur Brooks: You need a rhumb line.

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.210
Tim Ferriss: You need a location and your
end point on the GPS.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.300
Arthur Brooks: But if you're super attached
to it, then you're going to freak out when

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.720
something throws you off.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.120
And furthermore, you're not going to recognize
the fact that it is the voyage itself that

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:08.300
is the adventure of life, not actually reaching
the particular destination, whether it's the

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:11.990
original one or one that turns out to be better
or worse or wherever you wind up.

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.660
And that's the way you've got to live your
life.

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:15.910
So it's intention without attachment.

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:19.509
Now there's another thing that I was thinking
about this year because Thích Nhất Hạnh

00:07:19.509 --> 00:07:21.460
died, you know —
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:25.800
Arthur Brooks: The great Vietnamese Buddhist
monk, and I wrote his obituary in The Washington

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:26.800
Post actually.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:27.800
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:29.330
Arthur Brooks: It was a really interesting
experience.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.539
I talked about all what he's taught all of
us.

00:07:32.539 --> 00:07:36.270
And Thích Nhất Hạnh said, and I almost
had an impact on my, but I reflected on it

00:07:36.270 --> 00:07:40.720
upon his death that one of the greatest attachments
that people have in modern life is to their

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:43.120
views and opinions.

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:44.599
That's a real attachment.

00:07:44.599 --> 00:07:48.470
It can be as dangerous as your attachment
to money or power.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:49.470
Why?

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:53.759
Because people treat their opinions as if
they were gold, they're jewels.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:57.190
"Get between me and my political opinions,
man, I'll cancel you.

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:58.400
I'll get you fired.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.220
I'll denounce you on social media.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:01.750
Who knows, maybe I'll be violent."

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.419
I mean, life is crazy these days.

00:08:04.419 --> 00:08:08.910
And so I looked at it and I thought, am I
weighed down by attachment to my views, to

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.180
my political views?

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:15.830
So I wrote down five of my strongest political
views and I crossed them out.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.180
Tim Ferriss: This was after writing the obituary?

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:21.020
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it was on my reverse
bucket list when I turned 59.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:26.419
And I said, "Look, it's not that I don't have
these views, I just don't have the attachment

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:27.419
to these views.

00:08:27.419 --> 00:08:32.580
Look, man, I need more friends, and strong
political opinions is not going to get me

00:08:32.580 --> 00:08:33.580
there.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:34.580
It's not going to get me there.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.580
Love is going to get me there, tolerance is
going to get me there.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:42.510
A sense of curiosity about other people is
going to get me there, and strong political

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:45.279
opinions is going to put me in the wrong direction."

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:46.779
So, man, dead.

00:08:46.779 --> 00:08:48.279
The attachment, dead.

00:08:48.279 --> 00:08:52.410
Tim Ferriss: So maybe this is a little too
granular, but I'm curious.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.320
Thích Nhất Hạnh, this is a legendary
figure.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.480
I would be very intimidated by the task of
writing an obituary for such a person.

00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:06.820
I've read numbers of his books, been very
influenced by his thinking.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:11.800
How do you go about deciding what goes into
an obituary for someone like that, or for

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.860
anyone for that matter, but in this particular
case?

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:17.750
Arthur Brooks: The Germans talk about doing
something called a festschrift.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:24.370
It's a word that means this encomium, a celebration
of the work of somebody, and which I always

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:28.670
start when you do one of these things, it's
a typical European intellectual deal is you

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:34.740
look at the things that they said and did
that had the most impact and it was most meaningful

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.010
to them in the same way.

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.560
Now, I didn't know Thích Nhất Hạnh.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.270
I've worked very closely with the Dalai Lama
in the Tibetan Buddhist tradition, but the

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.500
Theravada Buddhist tradition I've never penetrated
personally and so I didn't know Thích Nhất

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:46.500
Hạnh.

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:51.850
But I do know the things that he said and
taught that really changed the way that Westerners

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:52.850
think.

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:56.310
And so that's what I wanted to talk about,
not what did he do that changed Theravada

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:57.310
Buddhism.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.060
I'm not a Theravada Buddhist.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:03.810
I'm not equipped to write that, but I can
talk about the way he changed me as a Catholic.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:09.620
I can talk about how he changed me as a public
policy person because I was reading Thích

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.029
Nhất Hạnh when I was running a think tank
in Washington, DC.

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:16.320
How he influenced me as a happiness professor
at Harvard, that's the stuff that I really

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:17.850
wanted to focus on.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:22.200
And that was the idea of attachment and detachment.

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:25.760
That was the idea of the illusion of individuality.

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:31.370
I mean, these are concepts that people didn't
think about.

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:35.820
He also was really behind the whole mindfulness
revolution in the West.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.970
I mean, he starts off The Miracle of Mindfulness,
that famous book, big bestseller with this

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.639
simple description of washing the dishes.

00:10:43.639 --> 00:10:48.350
And he says, "When you're washing the dishes,
you should be thinking about washing the dishes,

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:51.389
because if you don't think about washing the
dishes, you're not really there washing the

00:10:51.389 --> 00:10:52.839
dishes, are you?"

00:10:52.839 --> 00:10:58.700
And he persuades you with this hypnotic prose
to remember that if you don't think about

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.620
washing the dishes, but you're thinking about
doing something at work tomorrow or you're

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:04.410
thinking about something that happened yesterday,
that you're living in the future of the past

00:11:04.410 --> 00:11:05.410
and you've missed your life.

00:11:05.410 --> 00:11:09.400
Tim Ferriss: Or if you're thinking about eating
the juicy peach, which is a story — may

00:11:09.400 --> 00:11:12.670
not have been in that book — that I remember
him telling, but it was paired with the washing

00:11:12.670 --> 00:11:13.670
of the dishes.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:14.670
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:16.130
Tim Ferriss: He said, "If you're thinking
about the peach while you're washing the dishes,

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:18.570
when you're eating the peach, you'll probably
be thinking of something else as well."

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.570
Arthur Brooks: Exactly right.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:24.269
And then what you're doing is while you're
doing this, you're planning for a future existence

00:11:24.269 --> 00:11:25.490
that's better than now.

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.040
And when you reach it, it will be a phantasm
as well.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:33.690
It will be nothing more than a mythical past
while you're thinking about a new future that

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:35.360
doesn't quite exist and you've missed your
whole life.

00:11:35.360 --> 00:11:39.160
Tim Ferriss: How did he change you or influence
you as a Catholic?

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:44.800
Arthur Brooks: It's an interesting, and the
Dalai Lama too, by the way, I mean a lot of

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:51.620
Buddhist thinking has been incredibly helpful
to me to understand exactly what I'm trying

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.900
to do as a person when I'm centering myself
in prayer.

00:11:53.900 --> 00:12:01.540
So for example, Catholics, traditional Catholics,
they generally pray what's called the rosary.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:05.850
And the rosary is a set of beads that you
pray repetitive prayers.

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.399
It's about a thousand-year-old Catholic meditation.

00:12:08.399 --> 00:12:10.529
I do it every day.

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:13.370
And I noticed that I was having a hard time
focusing.

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:20.670
I was having a hard time understanding what
I was trying to do for myself, for this offering.

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.670
I didn't know how to make it fruitful in its
way.

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.579
I didn't know exactly know what the point
was.

00:12:25.579 --> 00:12:29.820
And studying the work of Thích Nhất Hạnh
and also studying at the Dalai Lama's monastery

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:35.019
in Dharamshala, India, sitting in meditation
and studying meditation techniques with the

00:12:35.019 --> 00:12:39.260
Tibetan Buddhist monks, I actually learned
what I was supposed to be doing as a Catholic

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:45.080
praying my rosary, how I actually could center
my prayer and make it deeply worshipful in

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:51.070
a meaningful way, and perhaps God wanted me
to learn that from my Buddhist sisters and

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:52.070
brothers.

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:57.710
Tim Ferriss: This is going to be adjacent
to this conversation, this topic, but I'm

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:02.870
curious, do you believe that, and we can always
cut this, but I'm very curious, do you believe

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:06.690
that direct transmission in some of these
lines, which is very important, certain schools

00:13:06.690 --> 00:13:14.300
of, we could call it mindfulness, but that
is a real phenomenon, or at least I suppose

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:17.699
it's undeniably important in the tradition,
but the idea that sitting with someone in

00:13:17.699 --> 00:13:25.440
meditation with them, that there are teachings
that are directly transmitted.

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:26.920
What is your thought on that if you have?

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:28.449
Arthur Brooks: I don't know the answer to
that.

00:13:28.449 --> 00:13:32.050
I do know that you can deeply be in communion
with somebody really intimately.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:36.630
And my wife, Ester and I, we actually, we
teach young couples that are engaged about,

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:37.970
she teaches the theological part.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:42.949
I teach the social science because I'm a total
wonk.

00:13:42.949 --> 00:13:48.100
And what we do tell them is that according
to the best science and our experience and

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:52.889
common sense that one of the most intimate
things that you can do with your partner or

00:13:52.889 --> 00:13:55.470
spouse is pray together.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:56.589
It's super intimate.

00:13:56.589 --> 00:14:01.250
I know couples that have been married for
25 years that will have sex together but won't

00:14:01.250 --> 00:14:05.149
pray together because it's too embarrassing
to pray together because praying together

00:14:05.149 --> 00:14:07.180
is just too intimate.

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:10.190
They don't know how to do it, it's embarrassing
to them.

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:14.920
Imagine you'll get naked in front of somebody
else, but you won't pray in front of them?

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:17.310
And that's because it's such a deeply intimate
thing.

00:14:17.310 --> 00:14:22.509
So when you cross the boundary of the greatest
intimacy with another person, there's going

00:14:22.509 --> 00:14:24.190
to be some transmission.

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:28.389
There's going to be something, and it's going
to — the idea of direct transmission rings

00:14:28.389 --> 00:14:32.940
true to me because of the nature and intensity
of the intimacy that comes from that experience.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.040
Tim Ferriss: We're going to take a hard left
for a second and we're going to come back

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:42.011
to a throughline which I am tracking, because
it's just what is top of mind for me.

00:14:42.011 --> 00:14:46.990
And then we're going to come back to possible
mystical/semi-mystical experiences in Mexico.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:48.640
So I'm giving an idea of —
Arthur Brooks: All right, I can't wait.

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:49.640
Tim Ferriss: Where in my head.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.540
Arthur Brooks: I get to go to Mexico and have
mystical experience.

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.410
Tim Ferriss: The invitation is now formal.

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:59.490
But first, because for those who are not watching
this video, I mean you are incredibly physically

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:05.560
fit and I know that you have some very deliberate
consistent practices including weight training

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:06.579
and so on.

00:15:06.579 --> 00:15:09.029
Blood occlusion training, are you still using
cuffs?

00:15:09.029 --> 00:15:10.420
Arthur Brooks: I do, yeah.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.320
And I recommend it for anybody over 40.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:14.910
Tim Ferriss: Could you explain what this is
and why you recommend it?

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:19.699
Arthur Brooks: So occlusion training, what
it effectively does is it's not a tourniquet,

00:15:19.699 --> 00:15:24.850
but what it is, it's a band around your arms
and/or legs so that when you're lifting, you

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:30.990
actually will get a much greater, as in the
vernacular, the burn, from resistance training

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:31.990
with lower weight.

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:35.670
Now, the reason I recommend this very strongly
is because the research suggests that it's

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:40.790
very good for both strength and hypertrophy
at lower weight levels so it saves your joints

00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:42.040
but gives you results.

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:45.000
And that's what you need to do when you're
over 40, save the joints.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:47.899
Tim Ferriss: And I really strongly encourage
people to check this out.

00:15:47.899 --> 00:15:53.420
I've done some of it, and to say that it increases
the burn or the pump is certainly true.

00:15:53.420 --> 00:15:55.759
Arthur Brooks: You've got a lot of pain.

00:15:55.759 --> 00:15:56.759
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, exactly.

00:15:56.759 --> 00:15:57.759
Arthur Brooks: Got a lot of pain.

00:15:57.759 --> 00:16:03.790
Tim Ferriss: I would say start conservative
because you might feel weak dialing back the

00:16:03.790 --> 00:16:04.790
weights, but just wait.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:05.899
It hurts, just give it a bit.

00:16:05.899 --> 00:16:08.970
Arthur Brooks: It really does, but then you
learn to love the pain.

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:11.649
Tim Ferriss: And you can travel with, there
are cuffs you can travel with.

00:16:11.649 --> 00:16:12.750
Arthur Brooks: I have them.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:13.930
I carry them with me all the time.

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.060
I'm on the road 48 weeks a year.

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:16.850
Tim Ferriss: Is there a particular type that
you use?

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:19.791
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, I don't actually remember
the brand, but they're actually, they use

00:16:19.791 --> 00:16:22.300
velcro and they're about two inches thick.

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:26.430
So too thick, and I actually can't get the
kind of occlusion that I really want.

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:31.310
So experiment with what works with the size
and shape of your biceps and triceps and then

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:34.769
learn how to use them and then make them tighter
and then get in touch with the pain.

00:16:34.769 --> 00:16:36.740
Tim Ferriss: And they're easy to travel with.

00:16:36.740 --> 00:16:39.310
Do your homework and start conservative.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:44.089
Kelly Starrett, a very famous performance
coach and PT, is a friend, has been on the

00:16:44.089 --> 00:16:45.850
show many times, introduced me to that.

00:16:45.850 --> 00:16:46.850
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it's amazing, isn't it?

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:50.730
Tim Ferriss: And he had a very similar argument,
which is effectively, he doesn't say this,

00:16:50.730 --> 00:16:54.839
but I would say you are as old as your joints
and connective tissue feel.

00:16:54.839 --> 00:16:56.420
I mean, there's certainly a cognitive component.

00:16:56.420 --> 00:17:02.420
And this is a really elegant way to preserve
or build muscle mass without risking integrity.

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:06.990
Arthur Brooks: I learned this from my friend
Sal Di Stefano, who started Mind Pump with

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:08.250
his partners.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.049
Tim Ferriss: Mind Pump, what is it?

00:17:10.049 --> 00:17:13.740
Arthur Brooks: Mind Pump, it's a fitness and
culture podcast and I've listened to it for

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:16.850
years and years and he taught me how to do
it.

00:17:16.850 --> 00:17:20.959
He actually is, gives me training tips and
good ideas and now it's great.

00:17:20.959 --> 00:17:21.959
It's been very helpful.

00:17:21.959 --> 00:17:26.430
And by the way, when I was a younger man,
when I was in my thirties, I figured, what's

00:17:26.430 --> 00:17:27.770
my goal with fitness?

00:17:27.770 --> 00:17:30.190
And the answer is to still be lifting when
I'm 80.

00:17:30.190 --> 00:17:32.169
To still be able to, why?

00:17:32.169 --> 00:17:34.680
Because we know of all the health benefits
from it.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:40.080
But the truth is that physical fitness, for
me, is a way to manage my negative affect.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:41.820
It's actually a happiness technique for me.

00:17:41.820 --> 00:17:42.820
It doesn't make me happier.

00:17:42.820 --> 00:17:44.650
It makes me less unhappy.

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.130
That's what physical fitness will do — it'll
buy you less unhappiness.

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:50.100
And so I managed my negative affect that way.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:53.640
And I know when I'm 80, I'm going to need
to manage my negative affect so I don't want

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:54.640
to hurt myself.

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:58.970
So I would go to the gym when I was 35 or
40 years old and I would go to these iron

00:17:58.970 --> 00:18:03.840
gyms wherever I was traveling, and I would
find the oldest dude, the old guy who's still

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:05.100
lifting heavy things.

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:06.770
And I would go up to him, them, I say, "Can
I ask your advice?"

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:09.549
And they always want to give you advice.

00:18:09.549 --> 00:18:12.630
The 76-year-old guy, he's like, "Don't bench
press.

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:14.950
It restricts your range of movement and that's
going to hurt.

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.310
You're going to get impingement injuries along
the way."

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:22.210
So that's why as you get older, you should
actually press with dumbbells and then do

00:18:22.210 --> 00:18:26.640
higher rep to get volume, do higher reps with
lower weight, et cetera, and just sensible

00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:27.640
stuff.

00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:32.130
But it's actually been incredibly helpful
for me to not get hurt and stay in the gym.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:34.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, a hundred percent.

00:18:34.560 --> 00:18:36.620
All right, so naturally, from the next place
—

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.420
Arthur Brooks: From the sublime to the ridiculous.

00:18:38.420 --> 00:18:41.120
Tim Ferriss: Well, maybe ridiculous, maybe
not.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.220
Arthur Brooks: Not for me, it's not.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:43.220
That's for sure, yeah.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:45.490
Tim Ferriss: Could you please describe your
experience — I think it was as a teenager.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:47.800
Was it as a teenager, in Mexico?

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:48.800
Arthur Brooks: Oh, yeah, the Mexican.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:55.059
It was not out in the woods with a shaman
on ayahuasca.

00:18:55.059 --> 00:18:58.330
When I was 15, I was on a band trip.

00:18:58.330 --> 00:19:00.610
I used to be a musician and I started as a
kid.

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:05.400
I was a real serious musician, and I wound
up being a professional musician from when

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:06.770
I was 19 until I was 31.

00:19:06.770 --> 00:19:07.770
Tim Ferriss: French horn?

00:19:07.770 --> 00:19:10.409
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, I was a French horn player,
classical French horn player all the way through

00:19:10.409 --> 00:19:11.409
my twenties.

00:19:11.409 --> 00:19:15.549
I didn't go to college until I was 30, but
it was my gap decade.

00:19:15.549 --> 00:19:18.720
And when I was —
Tim Ferriss: I need one of those, never too

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:19.720
late.

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:24.800
Arthur Brooks: And when I was 15 years old,
I was on this trip with a group with, not

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.860
a professional, it was an amateur concert
band, but doing a tour through Mexico and

00:19:28.860 --> 00:19:30.490
we were doing a lot of tourism.

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:34.049
And one of the tourist activities was to go
to the Shrine of Guadalupe in Mexico City.

00:19:34.049 --> 00:19:38.530
Now, this is a famous place for Catholics
because it's one of the great pilgrimage sites

00:19:38.530 --> 00:19:39.530
of the world.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:44.850
In Catholic teaching, this is where when the
Spanish explorers came to Mexico and having

00:19:44.850 --> 00:19:48.560
a horrible time because their marketing was
all goofed up for the Catholic Church.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:54.291
I mean, "Convert or die" is not a very compelling
fit, it turns out, and racist and everything

00:19:54.291 --> 00:19:56.760
that you possibly could want.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:20:00.890
What happens then is this weirdly miraculous
thing for Catholics and fellow travelers.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:02.419
I mean, it was a mystical experience.

00:20:02.419 --> 00:20:08.700
So there was a peasant man by the name of
Juan Diego who's on a hill outside of Mexico

00:20:08.700 --> 00:20:13.450
City and sees an apparition of the Blessed
Virgin Mary.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:21.290
So he on his poncho has imprinted the Blessed
Virgin Mary's image on his tilma, he takes

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.290
it back.

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:23.290
Tim Ferriss: Tilma is a textile?

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:24.290
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it's a poncho.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:25.290
Tim Ferriss: Right.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.820
Arthur Brooks: The tilma is what they would
wear.

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:32.490
And it was based on a fabric made out of cactus
actually.

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:33.760
And it's beautiful imprint, in color.

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:39.070
We've all seen the picture of, almost everybody's
seen the blessed Virgin Mary of Guadalupe

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:40.070
on this.

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:43.610
And if you google it, the Virgin of Guadalupe,
and you see it say, "Oh, I've seen that a

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:46.720
million times," because it's in every Latino
church.

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:51.010
And for the Catholic Church, she's the patroness
of all the Americas, including the United

00:20:51.010 --> 00:20:52.010
States.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:53.260
I mean, this is a big deal for the Church.

00:20:53.260 --> 00:20:58.890
So the bishop sees it, they take the tilma,
they display the tilma in public, and for

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.820
the first time they start getting converts.

00:21:00.820 --> 00:21:05.150
In the next nine years, seven million people
convert to the Catholic Church.

00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:06.150
Now why?

00:21:06.150 --> 00:21:09.090
There's worldly explanation, there's divine
explanations for it.

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:11.420
Here's a worldly explanation that's pretty
compelling.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.730
Our Lady of Guadalupe is not white.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:18.080
She's not Spanish, she's a mestiza.

00:21:18.080 --> 00:21:20.830
She's a woman of mixed race.

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:24.980
Now, we don't realize today how incredibly
subversive that is.

00:21:24.980 --> 00:21:28.340
How unbelievably culturally transgressive
that would've been.

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.100
"The Blessed Virgin Mary is not white?

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:32.120
What are you talking about?"

00:21:32.120 --> 00:21:33.309
"No, no, no.

00:21:33.309 --> 00:21:34.309
She's us."

00:21:34.309 --> 00:21:35.309
"She's us?"

00:21:35.309 --> 00:21:37.140
"She's every person in the world.

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:40.800
She loves you, she loves me, and she's one
of us because she's in all of us," and that

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:45.270
was this weird message that nobody really
would've thought of at the time, or so lore

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:46.630
goes.

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:52.350
And that's why people started saying, "Ah,
oh, this actually is for me because she actually

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:53.500
looks like me."

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:54.500
Crazy.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:59.710
So anyway, so I'm in this church in Mexico
City, this Shrine of Guadalupe, and I'm looking

00:21:59.710 --> 00:22:00.710
at the tilma.

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:03.080
Just sitting up there and I'm thinking, "This
is boring."

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:06.370
But then I noticed, she was looking at me.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:08.159
She was looking at me.

00:22:08.159 --> 00:22:13.000
Now to be sure, I didn't realize that you
can look at Elvis on velvet and the eyes will

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.250
follow you, right?

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:15.809
Okay, fair is fair.

00:22:15.809 --> 00:22:17.970
But I couldn't get it out of my head.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:19.350
I couldn't get it out of my head.

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:25.670
So worldly or divine or mystical or not, reasonable
people can disagree, but I couldn't get the

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:27.790
image out of my head, I couldn't.

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:31.590
And I realized I needed more in my life.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:35.400
I needed a deeper sense of the metaphysical
in my life.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:38.279
I needed the transcendent in my life.

00:22:38.279 --> 00:22:40.690
And every time I thought of it, I thought
of that image.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:45.529
And so I became a Catholic at 16 years old,
and I've been a Catholic man ever since.

00:22:45.529 --> 00:22:49.740
Tim Ferriss: And did you have a family history
of Catholicism?

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:50.740
Arthur Brooks: Zero.

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:51.740
Tim Ferriss: Zero.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:52.740
Arthur Brooks: Zero.

00:22:52.740 --> 00:22:56.279
No, I grew up in a Christian family, so I
had a little bit of wiring, but I literally

00:22:56.279 --> 00:22:57.850
knew no Catholics.

00:22:57.850 --> 00:22:59.880
It's just this thing.

00:22:59.880 --> 00:23:03.220
And my parents are like, "Ah, adolescent rebellion.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:05.070
I guess it's better than drugs."

00:23:05.070 --> 00:23:07.410
Tim Ferriss: It is better than heroin.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:12.940
Arthur Brooks: And the point is, a lot of
people watching us, some are traditionally

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:14.840
religious and some are spiritual and some
are not.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:19.200
But the whole point is we have a sense that
there's something else.

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:21.940
We have a sense that there's something deeper.

00:23:21.940 --> 00:23:26.240
Let something in your life take you to the
deeper place.

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:29.730
Something needs to take you by the hand to
the deeper place.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:32.600
There's a lot of research that suggests this
is the case.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:38.170
Don't try to go to the deeper place to find
it randomly on your own, let someone take

00:23:38.170 --> 00:23:39.170
you.

00:23:39.170 --> 00:23:43.190
Now that insight, it's ambiguous.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:49.549
But once people start to say, "I'm ready to
be taken to a deeper place, a more transcendent

00:23:49.549 --> 00:23:52.480
state," that person, that entity will appear.

00:23:52.480 --> 00:23:56.590
Tim Ferriss: We may come back to that, thank
you.

00:23:56.590 --> 00:24:06.080
And I do think that many different paths touch
corners of the same thing is my impression,

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:10.950
which we may not have time for in this conversation,
but to be continued.

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:14.559
And the reason I'm asking about this experience,
for those people who are wondering how this

00:24:14.559 --> 00:24:21.110
ties into maybe the headline of the podcast
is because I want to better understand the

00:24:21.110 --> 00:24:26.670
influences and experiences that have shaped
you and your perceptual apparatus and your

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:27.670
thinking.

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:31.570
So another would be you've had multiple wake
up calls in your life, and as you described,

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:36.020
you didn't exactly have the linear path to
Harvard prof that people might've envisioned,

00:24:36.020 --> 00:24:37.020
right?

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:38.130
Arthur Brooks: I got rejected from Harvard.

00:24:38.130 --> 00:24:39.590
Tim Ferriss: Exeter, Harvard, McKinsey.

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:40.590
Arthur Brooks: No.

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:41.620
Tim Ferriss: Different path.

00:24:41.620 --> 00:24:44.060
Arthur Brooks: I'd actually applied to Harvard
for graduate school.

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:45.060
And they're like, "No."

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.679
It took two weeks for them to reject.

00:24:47.679 --> 00:24:50.610
Part of it is at the time I was 31, I was
a college dropout.

00:24:50.610 --> 00:24:52.750
I had a degree from a correspondent school.

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:54.160
I was a French horn player.

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:57.860
These are not the core demographics of your
typical Harvard graduate student.

00:24:57.860 --> 00:25:03.830
And so yeah, I got rejected, which suggests,
by the way, Tim, that our standards for faculty

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:05.350
today are lower than for our students.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:06.850
But be that as it may.

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:07.850
Tim Ferriss: Maybe.

00:25:07.850 --> 00:25:09.460
Maybe, I'm skeptical in your case.

00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.500
I'd like to talk about your dad.

00:25:11.500 --> 00:25:12.500
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:25:12.500 --> 00:25:14.110
Tim Ferriss: So who was your father?

00:25:14.110 --> 00:25:18.090
What was he like and how did his life end?

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:25.620
Arthur Brooks: My dad was a mathematician,
he had a PhD in biostatistics.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:30.120
He was a lifelong college professor, most
of it at a small Christian college in Seattle

00:25:30.120 --> 00:25:31.690
called Seattle Pacific University.

00:25:31.690 --> 00:25:36.420
He was born to missionary parents, evangelical
missionary parents.

00:25:36.420 --> 00:25:43.549
He was born in the Navajo Nation in New Mexico
where my grandfather ran a mission school,

00:25:43.549 --> 00:25:44.549
actually.

00:25:44.549 --> 00:25:47.380
And then my grandfather went on to become
the dean of a very famous college outside

00:25:47.380 --> 00:25:52.059
Chicago called Wheaton College where all of
our family members had gone.

00:25:52.059 --> 00:25:55.960
My aunt went there in the '40s and dated Billy
Graham in college, as it turns out.

00:25:55.960 --> 00:25:56.960
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

00:25:56.960 --> 00:26:00.700
Arthur Brooks: And then my dad went on to
another Christian college, to the college

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:01.700
where he ultimately taught.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:07.279
And he was just absolutely in love with the
beauty of mathematics.

00:26:07.279 --> 00:26:09.360
As kids, my mother was an artist.

00:26:09.360 --> 00:26:11.490
My mother was a painter, my dad was a mathematician.

00:26:11.490 --> 00:26:15.799
And around the dinner table, we would talk
about art and we would talk about math.

00:26:15.799 --> 00:26:18.370
Those are the things and my father would pose
math problems to us.

00:26:18.370 --> 00:26:19.950
He would say, "Okay.

00:26:19.950 --> 00:26:25.130
Okay, boys," me and my brother, "Imagine all
of the integers between one and 100, one,

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:26.279
two, three, four.

00:26:26.279 --> 00:26:28.179
Okay, add them all together.

00:26:28.179 --> 00:26:29.179
What's the result?"

00:26:29.179 --> 00:26:30.179
And we'd be like, "I don't know.

00:26:30.179 --> 00:26:31.210
I need a piece of paper."

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:32.390
He'd say, "No, you don't.

00:26:32.390 --> 00:26:33.600
Think it another way.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:35.020
Think of another way."

00:26:35.020 --> 00:26:40.850
The solution by the way, is one plus a hundred
equals 101 and two plus 99 equals 101 and

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:41.850
three plus 98 equals 101.

00:26:41.850 --> 00:26:43.710
And there are 51 of those, which makes what?

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:44.900
It makes 101 combinations.

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:46.100
51 combinations of 101.

00:26:46.100 --> 00:26:48.830
Tim Ferriss: So that's how you —
Arthur Brooks: 5,050.

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:50.580
So that's what we would do.

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:51.580
That's what my father would do.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:53.380
Tim Ferriss: You're like, "Dad, I'm just trying
to eat my macaroni and cheese."

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:55.840
Arthur Brooks: I know, I eat my macaroni and
cheese, and they would say something like,

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:57.510
"See, isn't that beautiful?

00:26:57.510 --> 00:26:58.770
Isn't that beautiful?

00:26:58.770 --> 00:27:00.200
Isn't that evidence of God?"

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:02.730
Tim Ferriss: That's what he or your mom would
say?

00:27:02.730 --> 00:27:03.730
That's what he would say.

00:27:03.730 --> 00:27:04.730
Arthur Brooks: My dad would say.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:05.730
Tim Ferriss: The elegance of that.

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:10.140
Arthur Brooks: My dad believed not that what
we know is what we should pay attention to,

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:14.570
but to marvel at what we can do and still
not know anything.

00:27:14.570 --> 00:27:19.980
So my father would explain, it had a big impact
on me, that there's two kinds of problems

00:27:19.980 --> 00:27:21.020
in life.

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:23.450
There's complicated problems and there's complex
problems.

00:27:23.450 --> 00:27:27.600
It's a mathematical difference, but the technique
doesn't matter.

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:31.299
Complicated problems are all the things that
you can solve with computational horsepower

00:27:31.299 --> 00:27:32.960
and tech.

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:37.410
Complex problems are super easy to understand,
but you can never solve them.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:41.380
Like who's going to win between the Patriots
and the Dolphins?

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:44.559
I know what winning looks like, it's more
points on the scoreboard, but I have no idea

00:27:44.559 --> 00:27:47.452
and that's why it's beautiful to watch the
game and you don't want to simulate it on

00:27:47.452 --> 00:27:50.440
a computer because it'll be inaccurate.

00:27:50.440 --> 00:27:51.830
Love is complex.

00:27:51.830 --> 00:27:53.190
A jet engine is complicated.

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:56.500
A cat is complex, a toaster is complicated.

00:27:56.500 --> 00:27:58.490
And he said two kinds of problems.

00:27:58.490 --> 00:28:03.760
And this he said as a basic math problem,
the reason that we're always unsatisfied in

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:07.809
life is because we have complex problems.

00:28:07.809 --> 00:28:13.159
We want love, and all we have is complicated
solutions that the world is offering us like

00:28:13.159 --> 00:28:17.149
computers and the internet and social media
and blah, blah, blah, blah.

00:28:17.149 --> 00:28:20.750
And you'll never be satisfied because the
wrong kind of solution for the wrong kind

00:28:20.750 --> 00:28:21.750
of problem.

00:28:21.750 --> 00:28:24.980
Tim Ferriss: So that seems like a tremendously
valuable insight.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:25.980
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:28:25.980 --> 00:28:29.320
Tim Ferriss: Would you describe your father
as a happy man?

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:30.409
Arthur Brooks: No.

00:28:30.409 --> 00:28:35.490
My father was a gloomy man, and no doubt that
it had a lot to do with genetics, because

00:28:35.490 --> 00:28:39.110
50 percent of our emotional baseline is genetic.

00:28:39.110 --> 00:28:43.480
We know this from identical twin studies where
twins are born and separated at birth into

00:28:43.480 --> 00:28:44.480
separate families.

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:48.270
This is not an experiment that social scientists
have done because that would be horribly unethical

00:28:48.270 --> 00:28:50.100
but —
Tim Ferriss: Three Identical Strangers show

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:51.100
up, it turns out.

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:52.100
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:53.100
Well, yeah.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:54.100
Tim Ferriss: If you want to watch a brutal
doc.

00:28:54.100 --> 00:28:55.100
Arthur Brooks: Oh, yeah.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:56.100
That's a great documentary.

00:28:56.100 --> 00:28:57.100
Tim Ferriss: It is great.

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:00.570
Arthur Brooks: But 50 percent of your baseline
mood, your tendency toward ebullience or gloominess,

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:08.289
is sitting in your DNA, and the result of
that is you need — this is the reason, Tim,

00:29:08.289 --> 00:29:13.919
that I came to the happiness field, because
I saw my grandfather, who was a wonderful

00:29:13.919 --> 00:29:20.450
man and gloomy, and my father, who was brilliant
and a lovely person and an excellent, ethical,

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.880
kind man who was gloomy, and I said, "No more,
man.

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:24.880
No more.

00:29:24.880 --> 00:29:26.399
I'm going to live on the other 50 percent.

00:29:26.399 --> 00:29:32.530
I am not going to be governed by that 50 percent
that made my father an unhappy man."

00:29:32.530 --> 00:29:34.549
He died young, 66.

00:29:34.549 --> 00:29:42.390
He had cancer, and they gave him 10 or 15
years or more, and he died in two.

00:29:42.390 --> 00:29:43.690
As a statistician, he explained it.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:48.140
He was a biostatistician by his graduate degree,
and I said, "Dad, it's terrible luck."

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:51.899
He said, "Look, there's got to be people on
that side of the curve, too."

00:29:51.899 --> 00:29:54.500
He had a good sense of humor.

00:29:54.500 --> 00:30:03.539
But the whole point was, he wasn't prepared
to fight for life because he didn't have the

00:30:03.539 --> 00:30:07.120
hygiene for his own happiness.

00:30:07.120 --> 00:30:14.179
That example set me on the path to learn about
this and to share these ideas, and it has

00:30:14.179 --> 00:30:15.179
transformed my life.

00:30:15.179 --> 00:30:19.320
I'm grateful to my father, and I will be for
the rest of my life, for the example that

00:30:19.320 --> 00:30:28.470
he gave me and the lesson he taught me, even
through his unhappiness.

00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:43.620
Tim Ferriss: What were some decisions you
made, or things you began doing or stopped

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:46.250
doing, after your father's death?

00:30:46.250 --> 00:30:48.500
Arthur Brooks: I started exercising.

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.389
There is a little vanity to it, but not that
much.

00:30:50.389 --> 00:30:54.440
The whole point of the matter is, this is
one of the single best ways to manage your

00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:57.360
negative affect, is to be in the gym for an
hour a day.

00:30:57.360 --> 00:31:01.010
Tim Ferriss: It's a bit dated probably, but
there's a book called Spark, which also goes

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:07.600
into a lot of BDNF, and a lot of the circuitry
and biochemical reasons for exercise for this

00:31:07.600 --> 00:31:08.600
explicit purpose.

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:12.160
Arthur Brooks: When I'm working with students,
I put them through a battery of tests to look

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:15.620
at whether the bigger challenge in their life
is happiness or unhappiness.

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:18.950
The world is really scarce in the definitions
that it gives us, and there's a big mistake

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:22.680
that almost everybody makes about happiness,
which is, yeah, a lot of mistakes.

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:25.330
Number one, that it's the absence of unhappiness.

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:26.750
Number two, that it's a feeling.

00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:28.279
Both of those are wrong.

00:31:28.279 --> 00:31:32.150
Number three is that we can get it.

00:31:32.150 --> 00:31:37.630
We can approach happiness, but we can't get
there because we actually need unhappiness,

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:40.769
and unhappiness and happiness are not opposites
and can coexist.

00:31:40.769 --> 00:31:43.519
There's a lot to unpack from that.

00:31:43.519 --> 00:31:48.299
To begin with, happiness and unhappiness,
in terms of moods, are largely processed in

00:31:48.299 --> 00:31:49.500
different hemispheres of the brain.

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:54.309
A lot of neuroscientists believe that unhappy
cognitions and emotions are largely dominated

00:31:54.309 --> 00:31:56.330
by activity in the right side of the brain.

00:31:56.330 --> 00:32:01.920
The way that we know this, there's a terrific
neuroscientist at University of Wisconsin,

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:05.779
Richard Davidson, and he's done work that
shows that the left side of the musculature

00:32:05.779 --> 00:32:10.580
on your face is more active when you're unhappy,
when you're experiencing negative emotions,

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:14.510
anger, fear, disgust, sadness, and it's actually
funny.

00:32:14.510 --> 00:32:19.649
When my kids were little, I would notice that
when they fall down, you get a moment where

00:32:19.649 --> 00:32:20.950
you don't know what's going to happen next.

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:24.269
Are they going to laugh and keep playing,
or you're going to get the waterworks, right?

00:32:24.269 --> 00:32:28.260
The way to tell on that, because it's usually
five or six seconds, because the wiring in

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:29.850
their brain is actually not complete.

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:30.929
Tim Ferriss: It's a work in progress.

00:32:30.929 --> 00:32:36.160
Arthur Brooks: One of the tests that I administer
to my students, and I put in my new book as

00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:39.669
a matter of fact, because it's so important
for me and for understanding ourselves — it's

00:32:39.669 --> 00:32:43.850
called the Positive Affect Negative Affect
Series, PANAS, P-A-N-A-S.

00:32:43.850 --> 00:32:46.520
It's on my website, and it's easy to find.

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:47.520
I didn't create it.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.601
It's been psychometrically validated a bunch
of times.

00:32:49.601 --> 00:32:50.710
It's a great test.

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:56.690
What it does is it helps you figure out if
you're high-positive, high-negative, or low-positive,

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:58.900
low-negative, and there's four combinations.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:00.429
It's a two-by-two matrix.

00:33:00.429 --> 00:33:04.820
You can be a high-positive-affect person and
a high-negative-affect person.

00:33:04.820 --> 00:33:08.580
That's a high-affect person who's got a lot
of strong moods about everything.

00:33:08.580 --> 00:33:10.110
That's the mad scientist.

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:12.159
That's the mad scientist profile.

00:33:12.159 --> 00:33:13.190
That's me.

00:33:13.190 --> 00:33:18.170
I'm at the 85th percentile in happiness, happy
affect, and I'm at the 85th percentile in

00:33:18.170 --> 00:33:19.170
negative affect.

00:33:19.170 --> 00:33:20.170
Tim Ferriss: The highs are high and the lows
are low?

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:21.519
Arthur Brooks: Right, so I don't have a happiness
problem.

00:33:21.519 --> 00:33:23.470
I have an unhappiness problem, is what it
comes down to.

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:26.120
It's not a problem, because unhappiness is
really important.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.159
You won't be happy if you get rid of your
unhappiness, because you've got to be fully

00:33:29.159 --> 00:33:33.990
alive to get happier, but you do need to manage
yourself.

00:33:33.990 --> 00:33:37.519
Some people are really high-positive and really
low-negative.

00:33:37.519 --> 00:33:39.030
Those are the cheerleaders.

00:33:39.030 --> 00:33:41.000
Those people can't stay in the gym.

00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:42.000
Why?

00:33:42.000 --> 00:33:44.950
Because they don't feel better when they go
to the gym, because their negative affect

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:46.149
is already in the cellar.

00:33:46.149 --> 00:33:48.950
The third is high-negative and low-positive.

00:33:48.950 --> 00:33:49.950
That's the poet.

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:53.935
Nobody wants to be a poet, but the truth is
that the part of the brain called the ventral

00:33:53.935 --> 00:33:57.769
lateral prefrontal cortex is highly active
for people who are sad and people who are

00:33:57.769 --> 00:33:58.769
creative.

00:33:58.769 --> 00:34:01.630
That's the reason when I say poet, you don't
think about somebody who's skipping down the

00:34:01.630 --> 00:34:02.630
street, right?

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:04.340
You think of a creative person.

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:06.720
Tim Ferriss: “Life is shit,” with a cigarette
and a beret.

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:07.720
Arthur Brooks: Exactly right.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:12.179
You're in a French cafe, thinking nihilistic
thoughts and writing poetry.

00:34:12.179 --> 00:34:16.119
That's the poetic disposition, and the last
is low-low.

00:34:16.119 --> 00:34:20.310
You could be perfectly happy or unhappy, but
you have low affect levels.

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:22.859
That's called the judge, the sober judge.

00:34:22.859 --> 00:34:27.281
Tim Ferriss: I wish it were otherwise, but
I put high confidence that I am right down

00:34:27.281 --> 00:34:29.250
the beret-wearing way.

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:33.139
How would you advise someone — and I know
this won't apply to everybody listening, but

00:34:33.139 --> 00:34:37.869
just by way of a working example.

00:34:37.869 --> 00:34:41.400
What does someone do with — what are their
options for how to act upon that?

00:34:41.400 --> 00:34:42.490
Arthur Brooks: Okay.

00:34:42.490 --> 00:34:45.089
If you know that you're a poet, this is a
very good — this is an affect level.

00:34:45.089 --> 00:34:48.060
It doesn't mean that you're cosmically happy
or unhappy.

00:34:48.060 --> 00:34:50.290
It just means that this is your natural disposition.

00:34:50.290 --> 00:34:51.290
Tim Ferriss: Oh, right.

00:34:51.290 --> 00:34:53.399
Arthur Brooks: So this is important, right?

00:34:53.399 --> 00:34:56.919
It also means that you need to manage two
levers.

00:34:56.919 --> 00:35:01.700
You need to manage your happiness levels up,
and you need to manage your unhappiness levels,

00:35:01.700 --> 00:35:02.700
not to zero.

00:35:02.700 --> 00:35:05.800
There's nothing wrong with unhappiness, but
you need to mute them.

00:35:05.800 --> 00:35:07.900
You don't need to numb them.

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:09.310
You need to manage them.

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:14.231
Tim Ferriss: I may have already missed the
plot, but just define those two, because I

00:35:14.231 --> 00:35:19.010
think a lot of people, myself maybe included,
would think, "If you remove unhappiness, by

00:35:19.010 --> 00:35:21.940
default, since it's unhappiness — they seem
to be antonyms.

00:35:21.940 --> 00:35:23.680
So you remove one, and you have more of the
other."

00:35:23.680 --> 00:35:24.680
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:25.680
Tim Ferriss: So how should we think about
these two?

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:26.680
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, yeah.

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:27.680
That's a very good question.

00:35:27.680 --> 00:35:29.430
By the way, the answer is not obvious.

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:33.630
As a matter of fact, psychologists, until
about 50 years ago, really believed that unhappiness

00:35:33.630 --> 00:35:36.380
was the absence of happiness.

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:40.520
But it's not true, because we actually find
that the basic negative and positive emotions

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:43.349
are coming from different parts of the limbic
system and can coexist.

00:35:43.349 --> 00:35:44.349
Tim Ferriss: I see.

00:35:44.349 --> 00:35:47.950
So if you're thinking about it, you can think
about it then sort of neuro-anatomically,

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:50.030
as opposed to semantically with the words.

00:35:50.030 --> 00:35:51.030
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, yeah.

00:35:51.030 --> 00:35:52.740
And as a matter of fact —
Tim Ferriss: It's kind of like, not to dumb

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:58.260
it down too hard, but it's like if your left
hand performed X types of tasks and your right

00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:02.970
hand performed Y type of tasks, it's like,
okay, you can sort of decide what ratio you're

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:03.970
aiming for.

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:04.970
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:06.890
And your negative emotions are much more intense
than your positive emotions.

00:36:06.890 --> 00:36:09.589
This has evolved to keep you alive.

00:36:09.589 --> 00:36:11.010
Positive emotions are kind of nice to have.

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:14.530
Negative emotions are signals or alarms that
something might kill you.

00:36:14.530 --> 00:36:19.440
A sweet smile from somebody across the room
is very pleasant, but a frowning face might

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:21.089
be somebody who wants to murder you.

00:36:21.089 --> 00:36:23.270
Therefore, we've evolved to pay attention.

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:25.579
This is called the negativity bias that we
have.

00:36:25.579 --> 00:36:27.800
We also have mixed states that we go through
all day long.

00:36:27.800 --> 00:36:31.640
If you ask people in surveys what they're
feeling at any given moment, so you'll say

00:36:31.640 --> 00:36:33.980
in five-minute increments, "What are you feeling,
positive or negative?"

00:36:33.980 --> 00:36:37.600
you'll find that about 90 percent of the time
people can tell you how they're feeling.

00:36:37.600 --> 00:36:41.300
In 40 percent of the time for the average
person, it's really pure positive emotions

00:36:41.300 --> 00:36:42.300
at a low level.

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:44.150
It's your idle, is most of the time.

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:45.150
It's good.

00:36:45.150 --> 00:36:46.150
Life's okay.

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:47.150
It's kind of sunny today, whatever, whatever.

00:36:47.150 --> 00:36:48.150
All good.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:52.710
About 16 percent of the time is pure negative
emotion, and that usually means something's

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:53.950
happening that you don't like.

00:36:53.950 --> 00:36:57.470
Now, 33 percent of the time, you've got both.

00:36:57.470 --> 00:37:01.829
And usually, the way that this looks in people's
lives is that you're in your positive idle,

00:37:01.829 --> 00:37:03.730
but something's intruding.

00:37:03.730 --> 00:37:06.380
So there's something bugging you that you
don't always remember.

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:10.360
And we go through life, and on a certain day
it's like, yeah, it's all good.

00:37:10.360 --> 00:37:11.480
Oh, yeah, that thing.

00:37:11.480 --> 00:37:14.400
And then you go back to kind of going back
to your positive idle, and oh, yeah, that

00:37:14.400 --> 00:37:15.990
thing, that thing.

00:37:15.990 --> 00:37:17.150
So that's the mixed state.

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:18.150
Tim Ferriss: Like Twitter.

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:19.520
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, totally.

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:22.390
And by the way, I mean, it's an unhappiness
machine.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:23.510
You mean X?

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:24.640
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:37:24.640 --> 00:37:25.640
Sorry.

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:26.640
Exactly, X.

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:28.540
Arthur Brooks: A big black X.

00:37:28.540 --> 00:37:30.530
You mean it's like X.

00:37:30.530 --> 00:37:32.450
It's so appropriate.

00:37:32.450 --> 00:37:35.410
So this is important because we understand
that these are separable phenomena.

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:36.410
We need both.

00:37:36.410 --> 00:37:40.960
We need both these emotions, but we need to
be able to manage our emotions like pros,

00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:41.980
and that's a lot of what I do.

00:37:41.980 --> 00:37:43.780
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, so what are some options?

00:37:43.780 --> 00:37:46.960
So, I go through the PANAS, end up a “Poet.”

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:49.920
There are these two levers.

00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:53.950
What might be the process of thinking through
how to pull these levers in different ways?

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:58.520
Arthur Brooks: The happiness side is what
I spend a lot more time on because it's just

00:37:58.520 --> 00:38:01.690
so interesting on how we can pull the happiness
lever.

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:05.329
It starts by actually a good definition, which
is not feelings.

00:38:05.329 --> 00:38:06.510
Feelings are evidence of happiness.

00:38:06.510 --> 00:38:08.610
And thank God happiness isn't a feeling.

00:38:08.610 --> 00:38:11.069
Can you imagine going through life chasing
a feeling?

00:38:11.069 --> 00:38:12.069
That's awful.

00:38:12.069 --> 00:38:16.800
It's like if it feels good, do it or some
— or if it feels bad, make it stop.

00:38:16.800 --> 00:38:19.250
It's a terrible life strategy.

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:24.430
Happiness and its feelings are associated
with three tangible phenomena in our lives

00:38:24.430 --> 00:38:30.530
that we can actually understand and manage:
enjoyment, satisfaction, and meaning.

00:38:30.530 --> 00:38:32.700
Those are the three things that we need in
balance and abundance.

00:38:32.700 --> 00:38:38.760
Think of these as the macronutrients of happiness,
the protein, carbohydrates, and fat of happiness.

00:38:38.760 --> 00:38:42.840
If you don't have protein and carbohydrates
and fat in balance and abundance, all kinds

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:44.360
of weird things are going to happen to you.

00:38:44.360 --> 00:38:46.351
And you're not going to be as healthy as you
should be, and you won't feel good.

00:38:46.351 --> 00:38:47.859
The same thing is true.

00:38:47.859 --> 00:38:48.970
You need to enjoy your life.

00:38:48.970 --> 00:38:52.940
You need to get satisfaction from your accomplishments,
and you need to have a deep sense of meaning.

00:38:52.940 --> 00:38:55.280
And each one of these is a big challenge.

00:38:55.280 --> 00:39:00.079
When I meet somebody, through a series of
structured questions, I can figure out if

00:39:00.079 --> 00:39:03.530
there's a problem along one of these macronutrient
dimensions.

00:39:03.530 --> 00:39:08.480
And it turns out that there's some very easy
interventions that we can make across all

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:10.230
three, and I'll give you an example.

00:39:10.230 --> 00:39:11.450
The first one is enjoyment.

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:13.040
So I say, "Do you enjoy your life?"

00:39:13.040 --> 00:39:14.730
And they'll think about their little pleasures.

00:39:14.730 --> 00:39:17.270
I say, "That's not it."

00:39:17.270 --> 00:39:19.420
Pleasure is not a secret to happiness.

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:25.589
Pleasure is the fast road to obsessive-compulsive
activity and addiction.

00:39:25.589 --> 00:39:26.810
Why?

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:31.270
Because all pleasure is is a phenomenon from
the limbic system of your brain saying, "Do

00:39:31.270 --> 00:39:34.460
more of that thing because you're more likely
to survive and pass on your genes if you get

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:37.869
those calories, if you have that sex," whatever
it happens to be.

00:39:37.869 --> 00:39:41.369
The way that you can actually turn that into
a source of happiness is by mixing in two

00:39:41.369 --> 00:39:45.850
ingredients with your pleasure: people and
memory.

00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:48.580
Never do something that gives you pleasure
alone.

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:49.830
That's the rule of thumb.

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:51.160
If you're doing it alone, it's a problem.

00:39:51.160 --> 00:39:52.760
So Anheuser-Busch does a beer ad.

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:55.760
They never have in the beer ad —
Tim Ferriss: A guy drinking in his closet

00:39:55.760 --> 00:39:57.619
—
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, a guy pounding a 12-pack

00:39:57.619 --> 00:39:58.619
in his apartment alone.

00:39:58.619 --> 00:39:59.930
Tim Ferriss: — listening to Pink Floyd.

00:39:59.930 --> 00:40:00.930
Arthur Brooks: Why?

00:40:00.930 --> 00:40:04.270
Because that's pleasure, and that actually
leads to addiction.

00:40:04.270 --> 00:40:05.270
It's problematic.

00:40:05.270 --> 00:40:10.440
They always have an ad of a guy cracking a
beer with his buddies, people, and doing something

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:11.770
he's going to remember.

00:40:11.770 --> 00:40:12.770
Memory.

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:14.079
That's the secret to it.

00:40:14.079 --> 00:40:16.440
So I'll take a survey of people's habits.

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:20.450
Now, this is the reason that pornography is
a big problem, right?

00:40:20.450 --> 00:40:23.560
You're not consuming it in public with friends
to make a memory, for God's sake.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:24.560
That's just sort of weird.

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:25.560
Tim Ferriss: Very rarely.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:27.700
Arthur Brooks: That's a pretty weird thing
to do, right?

00:40:27.700 --> 00:40:28.730
And that's one of the reasons.

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:30.330
I mean, it hits the dopamine lever.

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:33.710
I mean, they all are neurophysiologically
similar phenomena.

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:37.440
They hit dopamine, dopamine, dopamine, dopamine
over and over and over again, and they ruin

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.520
your life because they become addictive.

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:43.140
They become monomaniacal, and they're not
the secret to happiness.

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:46.930
If you're at 3:00 in the morning in Vegas
pulling the lever on the slot machine by yourself,

00:40:46.930 --> 00:40:51.250
that's not the secret of happiness to anybody
I know.

00:40:51.250 --> 00:40:55.650
And I've never heard somebody say, "You know
what gives my life happiness?

00:40:55.650 --> 00:40:56.910
Methamphetamine."

00:40:56.910 --> 00:40:58.349
Never been said, right?

00:40:58.349 --> 00:41:01.420
And the reason for that is because these are
drugs.

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:02.420
People say drugs of abuse.

00:41:02.420 --> 00:41:03.420
No, no, no.

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:04.420
These are drugs of pleasure.

00:41:04.420 --> 00:41:05.420
Let's not lie about it.

00:41:05.420 --> 00:41:06.940
They give you pleasure.

00:41:06.940 --> 00:41:11.090
And pleasure alone, without memory, is a problem.

00:41:11.090 --> 00:41:15.940
This is a very practical example of how we
can use the science and turn it into a set

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:17.310
of guideposts for our life.

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:18.360
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm.

00:41:18.360 --> 00:41:21.770
So you have enjoyment, which you clarify.

00:41:21.770 --> 00:41:24.420
Arthur Brooks: Satisfaction and meaning are
next.

00:41:24.420 --> 00:41:25.420
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:41:25.420 --> 00:41:29.820
How would you make satisfaction granular?

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:34.620
Because I think about satisfaction, and I'm
like, hmm, I might conflate being satisfied

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:38.180
with doing, say, a large project with some
form of meaning.

00:41:38.180 --> 00:41:40.330
I can see how I might get my wires crossed.

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:42.650
So how should we think about these two in
your framework?

00:41:42.650 --> 00:41:45.470
Arthur Brooks: Satisfaction is achieving something
with struggle.

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:50.070
It really is that project that you're talking
about, which it will give you meaning as well.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:53.430
The project per se can actually cross the
boundaries across the macronutrients, just

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:58.069
like something that you eat has more than
one macronutrient.

00:41:58.069 --> 00:42:03.930
But satisfaction per se is doing something
that takes effort and expending the effort.

00:42:03.930 --> 00:42:09.990
So if my graduate students, they cheat to
get an A on an exam in my class, they'll get

00:42:09.990 --> 00:42:12.280
the grade, but they won't get the satisfaction.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:14.599
The satisfaction really comes from the pain.

00:42:14.599 --> 00:42:16.819
This is one of the paradoxes of life.

00:42:16.819 --> 00:42:19.240
You've got to suffer to get the satisfaction.

00:42:19.240 --> 00:42:21.329
You've got to defer the gratification.

00:42:21.329 --> 00:42:24.340
When somebody is on the pleasure mill, by
the way, they also don't get satisfaction

00:42:24.340 --> 00:42:27.290
from anything because they can't defer their
gratification anymore.

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:29.760
These problems bleed into each other.

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:36.140
So one of the things that you find is that
people who are very accomplished, the people

00:42:36.140 --> 00:42:41.069
— you are good at satisfaction because you
can defer your gratification and do long-term

00:42:41.069 --> 00:42:42.069
projects.

00:42:42.069 --> 00:42:43.160
You're a satisfaction guy.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:48.650
The problem for you and me and a lot of people
who are going to watch your show because they

00:42:48.650 --> 00:42:51.560
want to be better — I mean, nobody's watching
this show just for giggles.

00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:54.220
They're watching this show because —
Tim Ferriss: We're not funny enough.

00:42:54.220 --> 00:42:57.020
Arthur Brooks: They're watching your show
because they want to be better in their lives,

00:42:57.020 --> 00:43:00.000
and so they're going to be good at deferring
their gratification and doing hard things.

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:01.570
Tim Ferriss: Or at least aspire to do that.

00:43:01.570 --> 00:43:02.600
Arthur Brooks: Totally.

00:43:02.600 --> 00:43:04.630
I mean, I've read your books.

00:43:04.630 --> 00:43:08.140
It's not anything — it's interestingly deceptive.

00:43:08.140 --> 00:43:12.360
The 4-Hour Fill-in-the-Blank, the early books,
it makes it sound like it's a hack and it's

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:13.360
easy.

00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:14.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:15.360
Arthur Brooks: No.

00:43:15.360 --> 00:43:16.360
Tim Ferriss: No, it's not.

00:43:16.360 --> 00:43:17.360
Arthur Brooks: No, it's not.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:18.490
The four hours is hard, is the point.

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:19.490
Those four hours are going to hurt.

00:43:19.490 --> 00:43:21.170
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, and there's a lot of front-loading,
right?

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:25.220
There's also hard work without forethought
and planning.

00:43:25.220 --> 00:43:32.460
So there is built into any of those books
a strategic deferral.

00:43:32.460 --> 00:43:33.859
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, for sure.

00:43:33.859 --> 00:43:35.360
So that's what brings satisfaction.

00:43:35.360 --> 00:43:41.180
Here's the problem with satisfaction, and
this goes back to where we started the conversation.

00:43:41.180 --> 00:43:43.960
Mick Jagger sang, "I can't get no satisfaction."

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:45.390
That's wrong.

00:43:45.390 --> 00:43:47.780
You can't keep no satisfaction.

00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:51.579
That's why you try and you try and you try,
and there's a reason for that.

00:43:51.579 --> 00:43:57.240
Neuroscientists talk about homeostasis, which
is a phenomenon of always going back physically

00:43:57.240 --> 00:43:59.710
and emotionally to your baseline.

00:43:59.710 --> 00:44:02.520
And there's a reason for that, because you
need to go back to the baseline in your life

00:44:02.520 --> 00:44:06.050
so you can be ready to react to the next set
of circumstances.

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:10.100
If emotionally you were going to be bummed
out for the rest of your life, you'd become

00:44:10.100 --> 00:44:11.100
immobilized.

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:13.869
And if you felt super happy when something
good happened, you wouldn't be in the hunt

00:44:13.869 --> 00:44:15.040
anymore.

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:19.461
Mother Nature wants you to think that you're
going to feel this emotion for the rest of

00:44:19.461 --> 00:44:24.190
your life so that you will either avoid one
thing or pursue something, but she wants to

00:44:24.190 --> 00:44:28.359
fool you and send you back again and again
and again and have you never figure it out.

00:44:28.359 --> 00:44:30.420
Man, if I get that car, I'm going to love
it.

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:33.970
If I move to California, I'm going to enjoy
the sunshine for the rest of my life.

00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:39.670
It turns out you get six months, by the way,
of enjoying weather, but the taxes are forever.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:43.750
And that experience of never learning is called
the hedonic treadmill, and it's unbelievably

00:44:43.750 --> 00:44:44.750
painful.

00:44:44.750 --> 00:44:48.450
The way to solve that problem is haves divided
by wants.

00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:52.700
The way to get real satisfaction is not having
more but wanting less.

00:44:52.700 --> 00:44:54.780
And that's the reverse bucket list, et cetera.

00:44:54.780 --> 00:45:00.780
That's the set of habits that helps you dominate
that particular science and short circuit

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:04.660
the loop that you're on because of Mother
Nature's plans for you, which are not really

00:45:04.660 --> 00:45:05.960
in your happiness interests.

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:07.330
She doesn't care if you're happy, by the way.

00:45:07.330 --> 00:45:08.340
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:45:08.340 --> 00:45:09.350
Arthur Brooks: No.

00:45:09.350 --> 00:45:12.170
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, producing progeny is not
dependent on big smiles.

00:45:12.170 --> 00:45:13.680
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it's like DNA, man.

00:45:13.680 --> 00:45:18.510
It's all survival to pass on your genes, and
that's great for the propagation of the species.

00:45:18.510 --> 00:45:21.579
It's not good for, I don't know, for pursuing
the divine path.

00:45:21.579 --> 00:45:23.440
Tim Ferriss: We are going to come back to
meaning.

00:45:23.440 --> 00:45:28.870
I want to just take a sidebar on reducing
wants for a second —

00:45:28.870 --> 00:45:29.870
Arthur Brooks: Sure.

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:39.750
Tim Ferriss: — and talk about exercises
that you've had your students do in your classes.

00:45:39.750 --> 00:45:44.790
And I'm pulling this from memory, so I may
not get the wording spot on, but identifying

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:45.790
their idols.

00:45:45.790 --> 00:45:46.790
Am I getting this right?

00:45:46.790 --> 00:45:47.790
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:45:47.790 --> 00:45:50.050
Tim Ferriss: So if you could expand on what
that looks like.

00:45:50.050 --> 00:45:51.070
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

00:45:51.070 --> 00:45:52.070
We have a game.

00:45:52.070 --> 00:45:53.380
I have a game show in my class at Harvard.

00:45:53.380 --> 00:45:55.309
It's called What's My Idol?

00:45:55.309 --> 00:45:57.390
Now, you can tell how old I am.

00:45:57.390 --> 00:46:02.010
When I was a really little kid, there was
a game show on TV called What's My Line?

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:03.030
Your viewers can Google it.

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:07.171
I'm sure there's some black-and-white grainy
footage of it or something from when I was

00:46:07.171 --> 00:46:10.190
in the '70s or back.

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:15.369
What's My Idol is actually based on the insight
of a medieval philosopher, St. Thomas Aquinas,

00:46:15.369 --> 00:46:20.170
a theologian, but really a philosopher in
the Neoplatonic tradition.

00:46:20.170 --> 00:46:25.150
Aquinas is responsible for introducing Aristotle
to the modern world.

00:46:25.150 --> 00:46:28.300
Up until that point, nobody read Aristotle.

00:46:28.300 --> 00:46:29.700
It was all Plato, Aristotle's teacher.

00:46:29.700 --> 00:46:32.970
St. Thomas Aquinas, he said, "No, no, no,
no, no.

00:46:32.970 --> 00:46:34.280
Guys, read this one.

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:35.609
This is the one."

00:46:35.609 --> 00:46:36.609
Right?

00:46:36.609 --> 00:46:38.780
And he interpreted Aristotle for the modern
world.

00:46:38.780 --> 00:46:43.550
It was the same thing more or less that Averroes
was doing for the Muslim world and that Maimonides

00:46:43.550 --> 00:46:47.710
was doing for the Jewish world, because they
were coexisting in southern Spain in this

00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:49.270
intellectual soup.

00:46:49.270 --> 00:46:52.359
That's hard for us to understand today, it
was so deep, what was going on.

00:46:52.359 --> 00:46:57.109
St. Thomas Aquinas was an unbelievably adroit
social scientist.

00:46:57.109 --> 00:47:00.569
He was also, by the way —
Tim Ferriss: A phenomenally impressive figure.

00:47:00.569 --> 00:47:05.819
I mean, for people who aren't familiar, just
go read the Wikipedia entry and a few other

00:47:05.819 --> 00:47:06.819
things.

00:47:06.819 --> 00:47:07.819
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, yeah.

00:47:07.819 --> 00:47:08.819
Yeah, Right.

00:47:08.819 --> 00:47:09.819
Tim Ferriss: It will blow your mind.

00:47:09.819 --> 00:47:13.270
Arthur Brooks: And his magisterial work was
the Summa Theologiae, which is just — and

00:47:13.270 --> 00:47:16.960
the way that he does it, by the way, it's
a master class in the way that we should be

00:47:16.960 --> 00:47:23.859
thinking about big topics today, which is
by starting the topic with the best objection.

00:47:23.859 --> 00:47:26.130
"Here's the question.

00:47:26.130 --> 00:47:27.130
Here's the supposition.

00:47:27.130 --> 00:47:28.130
What's the best objection?

00:47:28.130 --> 00:47:29.130
Here's how I meet that objection.

00:47:29.130 --> 00:47:30.310
What's the second-best objection?

00:47:30.310 --> 00:47:31.550
Here's how I meet the objection."

00:47:31.550 --> 00:47:32.930
The whole book is written this way.

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:33.930
It's unbelievable.

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:38.540
And in the section on human happiness, which
he pulls from Aristotle, who claims without

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:44.050
any need for proof — this is what we all
want, whether we act that way or not.

00:47:44.050 --> 00:47:47.200
That there's four things that we do that distract
us from happiness.

00:47:47.200 --> 00:47:51.329
Now, his definition of happiness is seeking
the divine.

00:47:51.329 --> 00:47:52.329
This is what we all want.

00:47:52.329 --> 00:47:53.329
What do you want?

00:47:53.329 --> 00:47:54.329
The divine.

00:47:54.329 --> 00:47:55.329
Do you know that?

00:47:55.329 --> 00:47:56.329
Not necessarily.

00:47:56.329 --> 00:47:57.329
Is it hard?

00:47:57.329 --> 00:48:00.490
Yeah, and that's the reason we don't actually
act like we're pursuing the divine, because

00:48:00.490 --> 00:48:07.000
pursuing God or the divine or the metaphysical
singularity or exterior consciousness or what

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:12.270
your thing is has a lot of one-sided conversations
and a lot of inconvenient morality attached

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:13.270
to it.

00:48:13.270 --> 00:48:16.470
So we take these divinity substitutes.

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:18.700
These are the idols.

00:48:18.700 --> 00:48:23.320
Biblically and in mythology, what idols all
have in common is that they're God-like, but

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:24.320
they're not God.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:26.430
They're convenient substitutes for God.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:27.500
That's all an idol is.

00:48:27.500 --> 00:48:31.500
And St. Thomas Aquinas says, "There's four
substitutes for God," which we can think of

00:48:31.500 --> 00:48:34.080
as the four substitutes for the real secrets
to happiness.

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:37.210
They are money, power, pleasure, and fame.

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:39.480
Tim Ferriss: How little things change!

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:40.480
Arthur Brooks: Ah.

00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:42.630
And when he said fame, he actually didn't
say fame.

00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:45.020
He said honor, but that has a different connotation
in English.

00:48:45.020 --> 00:48:49.930
I have a son who's a Special Forces in the
military, and he serves with honor.

00:48:49.930 --> 00:48:51.160
That's not what they mean.

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:53.030
Honor means to be honored.

00:48:53.030 --> 00:48:54.030
It's fame.

00:48:54.030 --> 00:48:55.060
It's reputation.

00:48:55.060 --> 00:48:57.710
It's the admiration of other people.

00:48:57.710 --> 00:49:02.079
Maybe it's Instagram followers or something
ridiculous, but it is something that we want

00:49:02.079 --> 00:49:04.849
in the opinion of other people, and we thirst
after it.

00:49:04.849 --> 00:49:07.830
And he says, "Everybody is motivated by one
thing."

00:49:07.830 --> 00:49:11.640
And he'll even throw away comments, like,
"Communities, they coalesce around an idol."

00:49:11.640 --> 00:49:12.640
It's so true.

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:14.780
We're recording this podcast in New York.

00:49:14.780 --> 00:49:16.430
It's money, baby!

00:49:16.430 --> 00:49:19.340
You go to a party in New York, and everybody's
like, "How's your fund this year?

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:20.340
How are you doing this year?

00:49:20.340 --> 00:49:21.340
And how much does this apartment cost?"

00:49:21.340 --> 00:49:23.200
It's all about money.

00:49:23.200 --> 00:49:25.040
You go to L.A. What's the idol?

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:28.790
Tim Ferriss: Well, I'm not going to even call
it honor.

00:49:28.790 --> 00:49:29.790
Fame.

00:49:29.790 --> 00:49:30.790
Arthur Brooks: Fame, fame.

00:49:30.790 --> 00:49:32.650
Okay, okay, let's keep with the quiz.

00:49:32.650 --> 00:49:33.760
DC, what's the idol?

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:34.760
Tim Ferriss: Power.

00:49:34.760 --> 00:49:35.760
Arthur Brooks: Power, man!

00:49:35.760 --> 00:49:37.470
It's how close are you to the President?

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:38.839
What senator do you know?

00:49:38.839 --> 00:49:39.839
Vegas?

00:49:39.839 --> 00:49:40.839
Tim Ferriss: Pleasure.

00:49:40.839 --> 00:49:41.839
Yeah.

00:49:41.839 --> 00:49:42.839
Arthur Brooks: Right?

00:49:42.839 --> 00:49:45.680
I mean communities will coalesce around this,
but each one of us has our idol too.

00:49:45.680 --> 00:49:46.680
So here's the game.

00:49:46.680 --> 00:49:47.680
You want to play?

00:49:47.680 --> 00:49:48.900
Tim Ferriss: Sure, let's play the game.

00:49:48.900 --> 00:49:49.900
Arthur Brooks: Okay.

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:53.300
So the truth is, none of us has all four.

00:49:53.300 --> 00:49:54.470
None of us is so vice-ridden.

00:49:54.470 --> 00:49:57.380
We don't have the energy or time to be serving
all four of these idols.

00:49:57.380 --> 00:49:58.380
I mean, come on, life is short.

00:49:58.380 --> 00:50:00.520
Tim Ferriss: You underestimate me, sir.

00:50:00.520 --> 00:50:06.500
Arthur Brooks: But we all have one or two
that really animate us, and we don't always

00:50:06.500 --> 00:50:08.720
know it because we're not paying attention
to it.

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:14.570
Now, Aquinas's point, and modern psychology
bears all this out, that as a normal adult

00:50:14.570 --> 00:50:19.210
with a complicated life, if you know your
idol, you'll recognize the thing that always

00:50:19.210 --> 00:50:24.060
leads you astray and leads you to do the things
that you later regret because you were following

00:50:24.060 --> 00:50:25.060
that idol.

00:50:25.060 --> 00:50:28.650
So it's very important to figure out which
idol that is so that you can manage it.

00:50:28.650 --> 00:50:31.660
Not that you'll make it go away, because you're
human, but you can manage your idol.

00:50:31.660 --> 00:50:35.250
Okay, so I start by saying, "What's not your
idol?"

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:38.280
Because of the four, there's something that
you could ditch.

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:39.280
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:41.170
Power is not — yeah, power immediately.

00:50:41.170 --> 00:50:42.760
I'll name one to start with.

00:50:42.760 --> 00:50:44.530
So that's —
Arthur Brooks: That's why you're not a CEO.

00:50:44.530 --> 00:50:48.440
I mean, you've got a company, but you're not
trying to run a corporation.

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:49.809
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:49.809 --> 00:50:54.740
There's a lot of things that are — and I
know a lot of people who are power focused

00:50:54.740 --> 00:50:57.700
in a sense, in one form or another.

00:50:57.700 --> 00:50:59.200
It just doesn't —
Arthur Brooks: Do you dislike it?

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:00.760
Do you actively dislike power?

00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:03.440
Like, people having power over you and you
having power over others?

00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:06.060
Tim Ferriss: I don't dislike it necessarily.

00:51:06.060 --> 00:51:12.900
I mean, I think that it's an inherent dynamic
in particularly chimpanzee politics, but just

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:14.000
in nature in general.

00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:17.980
Arthur Brooks: Speaking of which, let me — react
to this phrase: Tim Feriss for Congress.

00:51:17.980 --> 00:51:19.010
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:51:19.010 --> 00:51:20.430
Arthur Brooks: There you go.

00:51:20.430 --> 00:51:21.560
Tim Ferriss: Absolutely not.

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:23.050
Arthur Brooks: Your answer is correct.

00:51:23.050 --> 00:51:24.720
You are not motivated by power.

00:51:24.720 --> 00:51:25.920
It's the first one to get rid of.

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:29.589
Okay, you've got three left: money, pleasure,
fame.

00:51:29.589 --> 00:51:31.390
You've got to kick one out.

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:32.660
You've got to get rid of one.

00:51:32.660 --> 00:51:33.660
What's the next one to go?

00:51:33.660 --> 00:51:34.700
Tim Ferriss: I can get rid of fame easily.

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:40.020
I think that there was a point in my life
where I sought a lot of social validation,

00:51:40.020 --> 00:51:45.030
but having seen the flip side and the shadow
elements of that, it holds no —

00:51:45.030 --> 00:51:48.710
Arthur Brooks: Well, you got pretty famous
young, and so you saw the dark side of that,

00:51:48.710 --> 00:51:54.280
which is what social scientists and even neuroscientists
will tell you with their research is that

00:51:54.280 --> 00:51:59.250
fame is really the only of the idols that
you can ever be happy in spite of.

00:51:59.250 --> 00:52:04.950
When Lady Gaga tweeted, "Fame is prison,"
and everybody derided that, I'm thinking,

00:52:04.950 --> 00:52:06.240
"That's deeply adept.

00:52:06.240 --> 00:52:08.900
That is a deeply clarifying comment."

00:52:08.900 --> 00:52:15.630
And by the way, I mean, John Milton wrote
that in the 17th century, that it is the idol

00:52:15.630 --> 00:52:19.640
that we thirst after and sacrifice the happiness
of our days for that.

00:52:19.640 --> 00:52:24.630
And you learn that by experience, not because
of disposition, because you did hunger after

00:52:24.630 --> 00:52:28.450
it and then experienced it, and you realize
that it's a very, very, very bitter fruit.

00:52:28.450 --> 00:52:29.450
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:34.359
There are a lot of footnotes and a lot of
fine print in that Faustian bargain.

00:52:34.359 --> 00:52:37.010
Arthur Brooks: Okay.

00:52:37.010 --> 00:52:38.619
For 46-year-old Tim Feriss, that's number
two.

00:52:38.619 --> 00:52:39.770
That goes out the door.

00:52:39.770 --> 00:52:40.770
Tim Ferriss: Don't care.

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:45.410
In fact, if I could put the toothpaste back
in the toothpaste tube, in numerous ways,

00:52:45.410 --> 00:52:46.410
I would do that.

00:52:46.410 --> 00:52:47.410
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:52:47.410 --> 00:52:48.410
Okay.

00:52:48.410 --> 00:52:51.980
You've got two left, and it's getting hot
in here, right?

00:52:51.980 --> 00:52:53.069
Money and pleasure, wealth and pleasure.

00:52:53.069 --> 00:52:54.440
Which one do you get rid of?

00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:58.540
Tim Ferriss: I would say money, in the hierarchy,
I can get rid of.

00:52:58.540 --> 00:53:04.490
The marginal utility of each additional dollar
just makes no difference in the things that

00:53:04.490 --> 00:53:05.490
I care about.

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:07.390
Arthur Brooks: And a middle-class life wouldn't
freak you out?

00:53:07.390 --> 00:53:08.470
Tim Ferriss: No.

00:53:08.470 --> 00:53:10.320
At this point, I —
Arthur Brooks: I mean, I'm not talking about

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:11.320
poverty.

00:53:11.320 --> 00:53:12.800
I'm not talking about not getting three squares.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:17.500
I'm talking about not having the money for
the nice stuff that people think is really

00:53:17.500 --> 00:53:19.069
wonderful to have: the houses, the boats,
the cars.

00:53:19.069 --> 00:53:20.780
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I don't really buy that
stuff.

00:53:20.780 --> 00:53:21.830
Arthur Brooks: You don't care?

00:53:21.830 --> 00:53:22.830
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I don't really care.

00:53:22.830 --> 00:53:28.020
And I care less and less, because also the
more financial success you have, the more

00:53:28.020 --> 00:53:31.150
time you spend with people who have even greater
financial success.

00:53:31.150 --> 00:53:39.450
And I've seen no indication, in fact, it might
even be a contraindication, that wealth produces

00:53:39.450 --> 00:53:40.990
the things I'm after.

00:53:40.990 --> 00:53:45.190
And I think there are significant adverse
side effects.

00:53:45.190 --> 00:53:48.829
Arthur Brooks: Well, there's a lot of research
that shows that there is a way to buy happiness,

00:53:48.829 --> 00:53:50.160
but it's not by buying stuff.

00:53:50.160 --> 00:53:51.750
It's by buying experiences.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:55.110
It's by buying time, or by giving it away.

00:53:55.110 --> 00:53:56.130
And you already figured that one out.

00:53:56.130 --> 00:53:57.130
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:53:57.130 --> 00:53:58.130
Yeah.

00:53:58.130 --> 00:53:59.130
I mean, quick example of that.

00:53:59.130 --> 00:54:02.630
For me, in the last handful of years, I owe
Tim Urban a huge thank you for his article

00:54:02.630 --> 00:54:03.690
"The Tail End."

00:54:03.690 --> 00:54:10.109
I ended up booking most years, depending on
my family's health and so on, a family trip

00:54:10.109 --> 00:54:13.970
once a year that we can look forward to for
six months and have group chats about and

00:54:13.970 --> 00:54:14.970
so on.

00:54:14.970 --> 00:54:19.450
And then even today, I was discussing a boys'
trip with some of my closest friends, probably

00:54:19.450 --> 00:54:20.450
like six months from now.

00:54:20.450 --> 00:54:21.450
Arthur Brooks: Great.

00:54:21.450 --> 00:54:24.100
Tim Ferriss: And the ROI on those is huge.

00:54:24.100 --> 00:54:25.660
But I would say money, I can get rid of money.

00:54:25.660 --> 00:54:26.710
Arthur Brooks: So we found your idol.

00:54:26.710 --> 00:54:27.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, pleasure.

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:29.010
Arthur Brooks: Tim Ferriss likes to feel good.

00:54:29.010 --> 00:54:30.010
Tim Ferriss: I do.

00:54:30.010 --> 00:54:31.010
Yeah.

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:32.010
Arthur Brooks: Yep.

00:54:32.010 --> 00:54:33.069
And again, there's nothing wrong with feeling
good.

00:54:33.069 --> 00:54:38.070
But for you, we need to watch to make sure
that you're getting enjoyment and not pleasure.

00:54:38.070 --> 00:54:39.070
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:54:39.070 --> 00:54:41.630
Arthur Brooks: And we need to be very disciplined
about enjoyment and not pleasure.

00:54:41.630 --> 00:54:47.030
Tim Ferriss: And I would also add to this
that I do think I'm a pleasure junkie for

00:54:47.030 --> 00:54:51.740
certain things, the sensual, the sexual being
very high on the list.

00:54:51.740 --> 00:54:55.460
There's also, I think, a wrinkle to this.

00:54:55.460 --> 00:55:00.990
The sort of Mara sitting on my shoulder, if
anybody gets the reference, would be given

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:05.569
a history of depression, I fear its opposite.

00:55:05.569 --> 00:55:11.690
So I chase pleasure as hopefully some inoculation
against darkness.

00:55:11.690 --> 00:55:13.250
So there are many things contributing to that.

00:55:13.250 --> 00:55:15.569
Arthur Brooks: Well, pleasure tends to be
numbing.

00:55:15.569 --> 00:55:19.510
It does tend to be numbing of negative emotion,
so people will pursue pleasure because they're

00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:22.520
actually trying to — it's fentanyl.

00:55:22.520 --> 00:55:25.970
There's a reason that fentanyl — there's
nobody who enjoys fentanyl, because it's not

00:55:25.970 --> 00:55:30.210
something you — like, "I'm going to go hang
out with my buddies and take fentanyl and

00:55:30.210 --> 00:55:31.210
make a memory."

00:55:31.210 --> 00:55:32.900
On the contrary, you're trying to eviscerate
memory.

00:55:32.900 --> 00:55:36.380
You're trying to get rid of memory with these
types of things.

00:55:36.380 --> 00:55:38.990
So numbing things tend to work that way, and
I understand.

00:55:38.990 --> 00:55:43.099
I mean, you have a history of mood disorders
in your life, and you don't want them.

00:55:43.099 --> 00:55:44.470
You don't want them back.

00:55:44.470 --> 00:55:48.109
You don't want to invite them back, and so
you do something that feels like an inoculation

00:55:48.109 --> 00:55:49.680
from those depths.

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:55.530
The key thing is that the real insurance policy
is enjoyment, satisfaction, and purpose.

00:55:55.530 --> 00:55:56.530
That's the real insurance policy.

00:55:56.530 --> 00:55:57.530
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:55:57.530 --> 00:56:02.390
Arthur Brooks: And again, because it doesn't
eradicate the unhappiness, the darkness.

00:56:02.390 --> 00:56:07.520
But it puts it into context such that it's
part of the bigger — the quilt of your life.

00:56:07.520 --> 00:56:11.230
So it's just one square in the quilt of your
life and not the whole blanket.

00:56:11.230 --> 00:56:12.349
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:56:12.349 --> 00:56:16.880
I want to expand on this because this is a
thread selfishly that I'd like to pull on.

00:56:16.880 --> 00:56:17.880
Arthur Brooks: No, you've got it.

00:56:17.880 --> 00:56:22.220
Tim Ferriss: And I will add maybe just one
thought, which is much of the pleasure that

00:56:22.220 --> 00:56:25.099
I pursue is not numbing.

00:56:25.099 --> 00:56:33.090
It's actually a — it's a volume competition
in the sense that if I start to feel like

00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:41.150
there are whispers of the potential of an
onset of a depressive episode, or maybe it's

00:56:41.150 --> 00:56:45.150
the ruminative thoughts and anxiety that comes
with that — but let's just say those are

00:56:45.150 --> 00:56:48.290
at a two out of 10 volume.

00:56:48.290 --> 00:56:53.770
There's fear because I worry that that is
going to spiral, and so I seek out something

00:56:53.770 --> 00:56:57.289
on the positive side of the ledger, or let
me just say the pleasure side of the ledger,

00:56:57.289 --> 00:56:58.289
that is higher volume.

00:56:58.289 --> 00:57:01.950
Arthur Brooks: You're trying to knock yourself
out of a groove is what it comes down to,

00:57:01.950 --> 00:57:02.950
right?

00:57:02.950 --> 00:57:06.710
Tim Ferriss: It's like overcoming a dread
of deadness with greater aliveness.

00:57:06.710 --> 00:57:07.710
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:57:07.710 --> 00:57:13.650
Now, what's one possible strategy that could
be a substitute strategy for that?

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:17.730
Tim Ferriss: Well, that's why I'm here, Doctor.

00:57:17.730 --> 00:57:20.010
Arthur Brooks: And there are, right?

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:21.010
There are.

00:57:21.010 --> 00:57:25.950
Meditation and prayer, seeking for the divine,
a serious spiritual practice, these are the

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:29.200
things that will actually do much the same
things, but without the numbing and without

00:57:29.200 --> 00:57:31.300
the danger, without the — because it's tricky,
right?

00:57:31.300 --> 00:57:34.950
I mean, hitting the dopamine lever has consequences.

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:39.450
Hitting it hard and often has desperate consequences
for it, and none of us needs problems in our

00:57:39.450 --> 00:57:40.450
lives.

00:57:40.450 --> 00:57:41.450
We have plenty of those already.

00:57:41.450 --> 00:57:42.680
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:46.170
So yes, and let's just say spare, meditation
— prayer.

00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:47.170
Spare!

00:57:47.170 --> 00:57:50.180
Spare prayer, the name of my forthcoming book.

00:57:50.180 --> 00:57:51.180
Arthur Brooks: I like it.

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:54.799
Tim Ferriss: So prayer and —
Arthur Brooks: I like the sound of it.

00:57:54.799 --> 00:57:55.799
Tim Ferriss: — and meditation.

00:57:55.799 --> 00:57:57.030
With meditation, I can see the difference.

00:57:57.030 --> 00:58:00.650
It's easy for, say, a secular listener to
consider.

00:58:00.650 --> 00:58:02.089
Arthur Brooks: Right.

00:58:02.089 --> 00:58:08.329
Tim Ferriss: What would you say to people
who are perhaps secular, not necessarily militant

00:58:08.329 --> 00:58:15.010
atheists, who I think recognize, as I do — in
some ways, I am deeply, just to layer on my

00:58:15.010 --> 00:58:20.049
sins here, envious of people who have strong
religious conviction.

00:58:20.049 --> 00:58:22.250
I recognize the value.

00:58:22.250 --> 00:58:28.820
And from at least in the sort of monotheistic
Judeo-Christian traditions, I don't think

00:58:28.820 --> 00:58:33.359
I'm likely to join one of the bands.

00:58:33.359 --> 00:58:36.160
Arthur Brooks: I'll work on you.

00:58:36.160 --> 00:58:41.900
Tim Ferriss: So I recognize we can go do some
blood occlusion training and you can just

00:58:41.900 --> 00:58:44.530
—
Arthur Brooks: And then we'll say a rosary

00:58:44.530 --> 00:58:45.530
together.

00:58:45.530 --> 00:58:49.290
Tim Ferriss: In between reps, lay the prayer
on thick.

00:58:49.290 --> 00:58:53.630
So what would you suggest to me or someone
who recognize the value?

00:58:53.630 --> 00:58:57.260
I do not have a counterargument, but I'm not
sure how to embrace that.

00:58:57.260 --> 00:58:59.120
Arthur Brooks: There are alternatives.

00:58:59.120 --> 00:59:01.299
I can tell you, as a Catholic, that that's
my thing.

00:59:01.299 --> 00:59:04.430
But I can tell you as a social scientist that
it's not the only thing that will actually

00:59:04.430 --> 00:59:08.450
bring the same neurophysiological and psychological
benefits.

00:59:08.450 --> 00:59:12.690
What we need is a sense of the transcendent
that makes us small.

00:59:12.690 --> 00:59:13.690
That's what we need.

00:59:13.690 --> 00:59:14.690
Why?

00:59:14.690 --> 00:59:15.690
Because we need perspective and peace.

00:59:15.690 --> 00:59:19.809
I mean, Tim, you're going to go through all
day; it's like my podcast and it's my commute

00:59:19.809 --> 00:59:20.809
and —
Tim Ferriss: Me, me, my, my, I, I.

00:59:20.809 --> 00:59:25.109
Arthur Brooks: — my lunch, my money, and
my friends, and my mom, and my me, me.

00:59:25.109 --> 00:59:29.289
Man, it's just so — it's like watching the
same episode every single night of Better

00:59:29.289 --> 00:59:31.210
Call Saul for the rest of your life.

00:59:31.210 --> 00:59:33.890
Tim Ferriss: It's like having the seagulls
from Finding Nemo in your head.

00:59:33.890 --> 00:59:35.140
Mine, mine, mine, mine, mine.

00:59:35.140 --> 00:59:36.890
Arthur Brooks: Me, me, mine, mine, mine.

00:59:36.890 --> 00:59:40.339
I mean, it's the worst, and so you have to
find a way to get peace.

00:59:40.339 --> 00:59:43.130
But what we really need is to put ourselves
in perspective.

00:59:43.130 --> 00:59:49.060
Now, most people are afraid to be little,
are really afraid to be insignificant.

00:59:49.060 --> 00:59:56.740
Perspective requires that we see reality,
and we get smaller, and we stand in awe of

00:59:56.740 --> 00:59:58.690
the universe and what it can bring.

00:59:58.690 --> 01:00:01.950
There's lots of ways to do that.

01:00:01.950 --> 01:00:04.030
Meditation practices are very good for that.

01:00:04.030 --> 01:00:05.589
Prayer is very good for that.

01:00:05.589 --> 01:00:08.630
Religion, under most traditional circumstances,
is very good for that.

01:00:08.630 --> 01:00:12.980
But walking in nature without devices before
dawn for an hour is incredibly good for that,

01:00:12.980 --> 01:00:14.089
for all sorts of reasons.

01:00:14.089 --> 01:00:17.260
And this is extremely well-validated in the
social science literature.

01:00:17.260 --> 01:00:19.270
Tim Ferriss: How important is the timing on
that?

01:00:19.270 --> 01:00:22.790
Arthur Brooks: Well, it's good because you
start the day that way, and it's the programming

01:00:22.790 --> 01:00:23.790
of it.

01:00:23.790 --> 01:00:27.160
And by the way, the experience of seeing the
sunrise is incredibly awe-inspiring.

01:00:27.160 --> 01:00:28.910
Plus, it's quiet and cool.

01:00:28.910 --> 01:00:30.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, all pleasant.

01:00:30.160 --> 01:00:31.369
Arthur Brooks: Quiet and cool, for sure.

01:00:31.369 --> 01:00:35.369
And therefore, you get just a bigger bolus
of the benefits that you actually get from

01:00:35.369 --> 01:00:39.670
being in contact with the Earth and being
in contact with nature, et cetera.

01:00:39.670 --> 01:00:43.410
Another way to do this is to stand in awe
of human genius.

01:00:43.410 --> 01:00:44.910
That's way outside the realm of your experience.

01:00:44.910 --> 01:00:48.119
So learn about the fugues of Johann Sebastian
Bach.

01:00:48.119 --> 01:00:49.119
Read about his life.

01:00:49.119 --> 01:00:50.119
Tim Ferriss: Your favorite.

01:00:50.119 --> 01:00:51.180
Arthur Brooks: Oh, yeah.

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:55.200
And listen to a hundred of his cantatas and
learn how to analyze them.

01:00:55.200 --> 01:00:56.950
I mean, your life will never be the same.

01:00:56.950 --> 01:01:02.940
Or your neighbor and our mutual friend, Ryan
Holiday, read the Stoics.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:03.940
Read the Stoics.

01:01:03.940 --> 01:01:05.730
I mean, it's a quasi-religious experience.

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:11.720
You will feel a deep satisfaction at your
littleness probably for the first time in

01:01:11.720 --> 01:01:13.170
years, as a matter of fact.

01:01:13.170 --> 01:01:17.570
There are many ways to get this, but you need
to get small in front of Bach, in front of

01:01:17.570 --> 01:01:21.390
Epictetus, in front of God.

01:01:21.390 --> 01:01:23.280
You need to get small, is the whole point.

01:01:23.280 --> 01:01:24.339
Tim Ferriss: I love that.

01:01:24.339 --> 01:01:25.400
It's easy to remember, right?

01:01:25.400 --> 01:01:26.770
Get small.

01:01:26.770 --> 01:01:31.900
And just to underscore that for myself, I
mean, a few things that have been helpful.

01:01:31.900 --> 01:01:32.900
Nature, yes.

01:01:32.900 --> 01:01:34.599
Arthur Brooks: Yes, for sure.

01:01:34.599 --> 01:01:39.130
Tim Ferriss: Studying or it could take the
form of listening to, say, Hardcore History

01:01:39.130 --> 01:01:45.730
and listening to "Kings of Kings," the Assyrians,
and really widening the aperture of your historic

01:01:45.730 --> 01:01:51.050
lens, just to put the problems of this week
in perspective.

01:01:51.050 --> 01:01:54.620
And also the impermanence of empires may be
relevant these days.

01:01:54.620 --> 01:01:56.819
Arthur Brooks: This is a moment in time and
— yeah.

01:01:56.819 --> 01:01:57.819
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:01:57.819 --> 01:02:01.890
So going back and realizing some of the most
powerful figures in the history of humankind

01:02:01.890 --> 01:02:07.530
you will not recognize, which also, I think
for me, has been very relieving and also has

01:02:07.530 --> 01:02:14.410
taken a lot of the earlier fixation on money
away because I realize it's just ashes to

01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:16.279
ashes, dust to dust, right?

01:02:16.279 --> 01:02:18.260
Arthur Brooks: To quote the Ecclesiastes.

01:02:18.260 --> 01:02:19.260
Tim Ferriss: That's right.

01:02:19.260 --> 01:02:20.590
Give me the full name of Alexander the Great.

01:02:20.590 --> 01:02:21.799
Most people can't.

01:02:21.799 --> 01:02:28.220
So rather than get fixed on some vague notion
of legacy, let's actually focus on other things.

01:02:28.220 --> 01:02:31.380
The getting small: looking at the stars, honestly.

01:02:31.380 --> 01:02:38.410
I did so much as a kid, and I lost it and
have sort of reclaimed as this thing that

01:02:38.410 --> 01:02:41.320
can be used as such a tool for zooming out.

01:02:41.320 --> 01:02:43.299
And I have two friends.

01:02:43.299 --> 01:02:49.670
Ed Cook is one notable example, who when he
thinks of his problems, and I'm going to paraphrase

01:02:49.670 --> 01:02:56.970
this, but he will look at the stars and sort
of zoom out from his neighborhood, himself,

01:02:56.970 --> 01:03:01.010
to the planet, to the solar system.

01:03:01.010 --> 01:03:05.650
And when he then returns back to laying on
the ground, looking at the stars, the problems

01:03:05.650 --> 01:03:08.180
that were plaguing you just do not seem —
Arthur Brooks: That's the point.

01:03:08.180 --> 01:03:09.180
Tim Ferriss: — so significant.

01:03:09.180 --> 01:03:10.180
Arthur Brooks: That's really the point.

01:03:10.180 --> 01:03:13.500
It's not just a question of minimizing your
problems.

01:03:13.500 --> 01:03:19.200
It's also minimizing the scale of your hopes
and dreams and opportunities and recognizing

01:03:19.200 --> 01:03:26.660
that what really matters, you're in that,
to be sure, but you're one part of that.

01:03:26.660 --> 01:03:27.980
It's okay.

01:03:27.980 --> 01:03:29.160
It's all okay.

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:31.819
The great is just okay, and the bad is okay
too.

01:03:31.819 --> 01:03:36.930
And that's so deeply comforting, and it leads
to so many improvements in mental health.

01:03:36.930 --> 01:03:40.569
I just don't know how you can put one foot
in front of the other without doing something

01:03:40.569 --> 01:03:41.680
like this every day.

01:03:41.680 --> 01:03:44.240
Now, people often ask, "Okay, how do I get
started?

01:03:44.240 --> 01:03:45.490
How do I get started?"

01:03:45.490 --> 01:03:47.180
Read 15 minutes a day.

01:03:47.180 --> 01:03:49.289
Pick up The Brothers K.
Tim Ferriss: The Brothers Karamazov?

01:03:49.289 --> 01:03:50.289
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:03:50.289 --> 01:03:51.430
Tim Ferriss: Am I saying that — probably
not.

01:03:51.430 --> 01:03:52.430
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, you are.

01:03:52.430 --> 01:03:54.990
The Brothers Karamazov by Fyodor Dostoevsky.

01:03:54.990 --> 01:03:58.670
If you like fiction, stop wasting your time
on trivialities.

01:03:58.670 --> 01:04:01.060
Go get The Brothers K. Why?

01:04:01.060 --> 01:04:06.060
Because it's a deeply awe-inspiring experience
about the human condition and the absurdity

01:04:06.060 --> 01:04:07.060
of it.

01:04:07.060 --> 01:04:08.060
It's beautiful.

01:04:08.060 --> 01:04:11.819
Get the Meditations of Marcus Aurelius, who,
by the way, a world historical figure because

01:04:11.819 --> 01:04:13.140
he was a Roman emperor.

01:04:13.140 --> 01:04:16.910
Nobody remembers practically a single thing
that happened as Roman Emperor.

01:04:16.910 --> 01:04:18.849
We remember what was written in his private
diary.

01:04:18.849 --> 01:04:19.849
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, it's wild.

01:04:19.849 --> 01:04:20.849
Arthur Brooks: It's craziness.

01:04:20.849 --> 01:04:21.849
Tim Ferriss: Last of the great emperors.

01:04:21.849 --> 01:04:24.960
Arthur Brooks: It's mostly because he was
dumb enough to leave his son in charge.

01:04:24.960 --> 01:04:25.960
Tim Ferriss: Oops.

01:04:25.960 --> 01:04:26.970
Arthur Brooks: His wastrel.

01:04:26.970 --> 01:04:30.000
Tim Ferriss: That's like the story of humankind
also.

01:04:30.000 --> 01:04:33.630
Arthur Brooks: Speaking of which, I think
I want to leave my business to my kids.

01:04:33.630 --> 01:04:34.630
What do you think, Tim?

01:04:34.630 --> 01:04:37.260
Tim Ferriss: There's a long conversation,
knowing how kids are —

01:04:37.260 --> 01:04:38.650
Arthur Brooks: My kids are awesome.

01:04:38.650 --> 01:04:41.260
I wonder if I can get them into the happiness
business.

01:04:41.260 --> 01:04:46.930
Tim Ferriss: "Dad, I know you're always beating
us over the head on this happiness business.

01:04:46.930 --> 01:04:47.930
It's not my path.

01:04:47.930 --> 01:04:48.930
It's your path."

01:04:48.930 --> 01:04:50.880
Arthur Brooks: Eye roll.

01:04:50.880 --> 01:04:57.359
Tim Ferriss: So I am curious for you personally,
having thought about this deeply, having tracked

01:04:57.359 --> 01:05:05.970
a lot of things also, what are some of the
best uses of money that you have found personally?

01:05:05.970 --> 01:05:06.970
What types of things?

01:05:06.970 --> 01:05:09.029
Are there any concrete examples that you can
give?

01:05:09.029 --> 01:05:12.220
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, so we rolled through
that fast just a second ago, but it turns

01:05:12.220 --> 01:05:13.809
out there's a ton of research on this.

01:05:13.809 --> 01:05:18.520
My colleagues, Mike Norton and Ashley Whillans
at the Harvard Business School, they've done

01:05:18.520 --> 01:05:23.880
this exhaustive research on how to buy happiness,
how to buy happiness, right, okay?

01:05:23.880 --> 01:05:26.200
And Mother Nature says, "Get more stuff."

01:05:26.200 --> 01:05:27.200
"Why?"

01:05:27.200 --> 01:05:28.200
"Because satisfaction comes from having more."

01:05:28.200 --> 01:05:29.490
"What's the strategy for life?"

01:05:29.490 --> 01:05:30.490
"More."

01:05:30.490 --> 01:05:31.490
Mother Nature tells you that.

01:05:31.490 --> 01:05:32.539
She lies, she lies.

01:05:32.539 --> 01:05:34.589
Lies and lies and laughs at you.

01:05:34.589 --> 01:05:35.640
That's not the strategy.

01:05:35.640 --> 01:05:41.309
You need to buy enough, but no possession
will do anything beyond bring you out of misery.

01:05:41.309 --> 01:05:46.779
That's the reason that the famous Kahneman
and Deaton study from Princeton shows that

01:05:46.779 --> 01:05:49.870
happiness flattens out after $75,000 a year.

01:05:49.870 --> 01:05:53.291
And Matt Killingsworth at Penn actually reran
the data and finds that it's higher, but it

01:05:53.291 --> 01:05:54.291
still flattens out.

01:05:54.291 --> 01:05:55.770
Tim Ferriss: Inflation-adjusted, but still.

01:05:55.770 --> 01:05:58.910
Arthur Brooks: Inflation adjusted or your
results may vary or whatever it is, but the

01:05:58.910 --> 01:06:02.170
whole point is you just don't get happier
and happier and happier is millions.

01:06:02.170 --> 01:06:06.480
The reason, by the way that we think that
is an illusion that comes from our experience,

01:06:06.480 --> 01:06:10.470
most people have less than they perceive that
they need when they're young.

01:06:10.470 --> 01:06:14.760
And the result of it is that a lot of people
and a lot of people listening to us, they

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:18.260
suffer a lack of meeting some basic needs.

01:06:18.260 --> 01:06:25.029
When I was 19 to 25, I was too poor for six
years to go to the dentist, for six years.

01:06:25.029 --> 01:06:28.240
Now, of course, I don't think I ever went
a day without cigarettes, so I guess it was

01:06:28.240 --> 01:06:30.130
priorities.

01:06:30.130 --> 01:06:32.990
But the point is that when I was —
Tim Ferriss: Breakfast of champions.

01:06:32.990 --> 01:06:34.980
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, beer, cigarettes, pizza.

01:06:34.980 --> 01:06:37.380
I was living in Washington Heights in those
days.

01:06:37.380 --> 01:06:42.190
And when I was 25, I took a job in the Barcelona
Orchestra and I moved to Barcelona, had benefits.

01:06:42.190 --> 01:06:50.760
I had bennies, man, and I went to the dentist
and he filled 10 cavities, and I felt a lot

01:06:50.760 --> 01:06:51.760
better.

01:06:51.760 --> 01:06:53.420
And I thought, money does buy happiness.

01:06:53.420 --> 01:06:59.180
No, no, no, money lowers unhappiness when
you move out of deprivation.

01:06:59.180 --> 01:07:00.180
That's it.

01:07:00.180 --> 01:07:04.970
And so what happens is I made the mental link
between getting money and feeling better.

01:07:04.970 --> 01:07:08.279
And people do that, and they chase that feeling
for the rest of their life.

01:07:08.279 --> 01:07:12.480
Because you're doing sums in your head of
well-being is happiness and unhappiness and

01:07:12.480 --> 01:07:13.480
all that.

01:07:13.480 --> 01:07:17.410
You can't tell the difference when you're
just rolling through your life and early on

01:07:17.410 --> 01:07:18.529
you felt a lot better.

01:07:18.529 --> 01:07:20.380
That's all you know when you had more money.

01:07:20.380 --> 01:07:23.650
And so you want that feeling and you chase
the feeling.

01:07:23.650 --> 01:07:25.810
You chase the high, you chase the early hits.

01:07:25.810 --> 01:07:28.050
It's like drugs, like any other drugs in this
way.

01:07:28.050 --> 01:07:29.280
So that doesn't work.

01:07:29.280 --> 01:07:33.280
There are three ways you can buy happiness
according to Whillans and Norton, my colleagues

01:07:33.280 --> 01:07:38.059
at HBS, you can buy experiences.

01:07:38.059 --> 01:07:41.660
This is critically important because what
do experiences that where you buy happiness

01:07:41.660 --> 01:07:42.660
really have in common?

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:45.230
You add money, which is sort of like pleasure.

01:07:45.230 --> 01:07:49.870
But the really important parts are the people
in the memory.

01:07:49.870 --> 01:07:52.319
People in memory, people in memory are always
part of experiences.

01:07:52.319 --> 01:07:57.609
Okay, now, sometimes you want to do things
alone, but generally speaking, the greatest

01:07:57.609 --> 01:08:01.100
happiness comes because it enhances love in
your life.

01:08:01.100 --> 01:08:07.119
The best way to improve love in your life
if you want your love life with your partner

01:08:07.119 --> 01:08:09.420
to get better, go away together.

01:08:09.420 --> 01:08:10.420
Go away together.

01:08:10.420 --> 01:08:11.501
That's always the best way to do it.

01:08:11.501 --> 01:08:14.349
Or stay home together, but turn off the phone.

01:08:14.349 --> 01:08:16.730
In other words, get an experience with the
person that you love.

01:08:16.730 --> 01:08:20.279
That's a great way to spend money because
it will reliably, unless you waste it, do

01:08:20.279 --> 01:08:21.279
something stupid.

01:08:21.279 --> 01:08:26.120
Like I'm going to have a bender in Vegas and
then get blackout drunk and sit in front of

01:08:26.120 --> 01:08:27.120
the slot machine.

01:08:27.120 --> 01:08:28.460
That's not the secret.

01:08:28.460 --> 01:08:33.359
But being prudent, you can actually buy happiness
through experiences.

01:08:33.359 --> 01:08:34.359
That's number one.

01:08:34.359 --> 01:08:35.359
Number two —
Tim Ferriss: I'm going to ask you for personal

01:08:35.359 --> 01:08:37.589
examples, but yes.

01:08:37.589 --> 01:08:38.589
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:08:38.589 --> 01:08:39.589
Tim Ferriss: I'm serious.

01:08:39.589 --> 01:08:41.000
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, for sure.

01:08:41.000 --> 01:08:42.000
Buy time.

01:08:42.000 --> 01:08:43.000
Buy time.

01:08:43.000 --> 01:08:44.280
But use the time correctly.

01:08:44.280 --> 01:08:50.420
If you have the money and you're cutting your
lawn and you don't like cutting your lawn,

01:08:50.420 --> 01:08:52.520
improve GDP, hire a guy.

01:08:52.520 --> 01:08:55.630
Now, a lot of Americans don't like to do that
because they have this kind of this weird

01:08:55.630 --> 01:08:57.100
reverse-classist sense.

01:08:57.100 --> 01:09:00.030
Well, let me tell you, there's a lot of people
who are making more money than you cutting

01:09:00.030 --> 01:09:02.470
lawns and running a gardening service.

01:09:02.470 --> 01:09:07.100
Spread the love, give somebody a job, and
then use the time correctly.

01:09:07.100 --> 01:09:11.560
Don't fritter away your time scrolling social
media in the house, in the air conditioning.

01:09:11.560 --> 01:09:17.569
No, no, no, use the time specifically for
something, ideally with somebody doing something

01:09:17.569 --> 01:09:19.719
that you enjoy and that will truly buy happiness.

01:09:19.719 --> 01:09:24.969
And last but not least, give your money away
to somebody who needs it, who deserves it,

01:09:24.969 --> 01:09:28.240
and a cause that truly inspires you, something
you really believe in.

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:32.270
I've studied philanthropy long before I studied
happiness.

01:09:32.270 --> 01:09:36.790
I was an academic beavering away in obscurity,
writing books on charity and philanthropy

01:09:36.790 --> 01:09:38.250
and nonprofit organizations.

01:09:38.250 --> 01:09:43.089
And the reason I initially got into happiness
in the first place, besides wanting to be

01:09:43.089 --> 01:09:46.659
happy, was that I found that the more people
gave, the happier they got.

01:09:46.659 --> 01:09:50.429
Tim Ferriss: So we don't have to tread into
these waters, but I think it would be very

01:09:50.429 --> 01:09:54.029
helpful for people, and I'm very curious,
I'm happy to reciprocate also, where have

01:09:54.029 --> 01:09:56.500
you landed for where to give your own money?

01:09:56.500 --> 01:10:00.380
Arthur Brooks: I give money to things that
I really care about that I think have an impact

01:10:00.380 --> 01:10:01.980
on people's lives in a big way.

01:10:01.980 --> 01:10:06.290
And a lot of what I do is I give to education,
to people who are at the margins of society.

01:10:06.290 --> 01:10:11.909
So I give a lot to primary and secondary education,
specifically to Catholic primary and secondary

01:10:11.909 --> 01:10:16.659
education because I think it's really well
done, and it's an option that a lot of people

01:10:16.659 --> 01:10:18.719
don't have access to because it costs money.

01:10:18.719 --> 01:10:23.350
So a lot of what I do, probably 75 percent
of what I give away, and I give away 10 percent

01:10:23.350 --> 01:10:26.159
of my income so —
Tim Ferriss: Good for you.

01:10:26.159 --> 01:10:28.500
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, because it's not that
hard to do.

01:10:28.500 --> 01:10:30.060
You just have to pay attention to it.

01:10:30.060 --> 01:10:31.060
And then there's structures.

01:10:31.060 --> 01:10:32.239
So you get a charitable giving account.

01:10:32.239 --> 01:10:33.490
Tim Ferriss: Do you think of that as a tithe
or —

01:10:33.490 --> 01:10:37.680
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, I do think of it as a
tithe, but I really think of it as a privilege.

01:10:37.680 --> 01:10:41.090
I think of it a lot less as a duty and a lot
more as a privilege.

01:10:41.090 --> 01:10:43.690
My wife and I, we just look forward to it.

01:10:43.690 --> 01:10:47.070
And then we have a process in our family of
should we give money to this?

01:10:47.070 --> 01:10:48.070
Should we give money to that?

01:10:48.070 --> 01:10:51.880
Usually we give something kind of a big bolus
one year, and then smaller gifts to things

01:10:51.880 --> 01:10:54.320
that are ongoing sources of support that we
really believe in.

01:10:54.320 --> 01:10:56.280
Tim Ferriss: How do you decide what to give
to?

01:10:56.280 --> 01:10:59.630
Arthur Brooks: Well, we know the people and
we do the work, right?

01:10:59.630 --> 01:11:03.060
So it's not just anybody, we get a letter
in the mail and we respond with a big check.

01:11:03.060 --> 01:11:04.880
It's like, huh, that seems worthy.

01:11:04.880 --> 01:11:05.880
No, we actually do the work.

01:11:05.880 --> 01:11:10.850
And part of it's, one of the classes I teach
at Harvard is on nonprofit management.

01:11:10.850 --> 01:11:14.960
And so I have kind of a strong background
and whether it's a reputable cause and what

01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:18.280
to look for into measuring the effectiveness
of an organization.

01:11:18.280 --> 01:11:20.390
So I do the work, I do the background work.

01:11:20.390 --> 01:11:24.000
I see what kind of overhead rates that they're
using, how they're using their money.

01:11:24.000 --> 01:11:26.900
A lot has to do with, if I like the mission.

01:11:26.900 --> 01:11:30.190
Sometimes if it's going to be a substantial
amount of money, I get to know the people

01:11:30.190 --> 01:11:32.170
and the organization.

01:11:32.170 --> 01:11:36.620
And it has to be something that I think is
going to change people's lives for the better.

01:11:36.620 --> 01:11:41.820
Now, I've actually changed my view on this
a little bit because early on we'd write a

01:11:41.820 --> 01:11:46.090
lot of little checks, lots and lots and lots
of little checks to great organizations.

01:11:46.090 --> 01:11:50.880
And then I found that you can have a lot more
effect on your own well-being.

01:11:50.880 --> 01:11:55.590
And again, this is kind of selfish, but in
your own well-being, by concentrating on turning

01:11:55.590 --> 01:12:01.560
the whole dial in one person's life that you
can really — so really.

01:12:01.560 --> 01:12:08.540
And there's an old Talmudic phrase from the
book of Sanhedrin that says, "In every man

01:12:08.540 --> 01:12:10.210
is the whole world," right?

01:12:10.210 --> 01:12:13.890
In other words, turn the dial for one person,
you've turned the dial for all of humanity

01:12:13.890 --> 01:12:14.910
because it's really had an impact.

01:12:14.910 --> 01:12:16.320
I remember telling my wife about that.

01:12:16.320 --> 01:12:20.720
It's, "Yeah, I've got these data that when
you really help one person instead of a little

01:12:20.720 --> 01:12:24.639
bit for a lot really anonymously, you can
get all these benefits."

01:12:24.639 --> 01:12:25.639
This is years ago.

01:12:25.639 --> 01:12:27.850
This is maybe 20 years ago now.

01:12:27.850 --> 01:12:31.850
And she's, "Why don't we adopt a baby?"

01:12:31.850 --> 01:12:35.350
And I'm, "It's only a book, man."

01:12:35.350 --> 01:12:36.840
But we did.

01:12:36.840 --> 01:12:39.190
We actually did.

01:12:39.190 --> 01:12:41.670
It was totally life-changing.

01:12:41.670 --> 01:12:42.910
Totally life-changing.

01:12:42.910 --> 01:12:45.159
Tim Ferriss: So I think I missed a hop.

01:12:45.159 --> 01:12:46.870
Arthur Brooks: So that's how you give.

01:12:46.870 --> 01:12:50.710
You've got to do the work and think about
it and put your heart into it.

01:12:50.710 --> 01:12:51.710
And philosophical —
Tim Ferriss: Could you say more about that,

01:12:51.710 --> 01:12:53.139
how did she come to the adoption?

01:12:53.139 --> 01:12:54.500
Arthur Brooks: My wife?

01:12:54.500 --> 01:12:56.260
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, how did she —
Arthur Brooks: She came to it because I was

01:12:56.260 --> 01:12:59.780
saying, you get a lot more benefits when you
turn the whole dial on one person's life as

01:12:59.780 --> 01:13:01.429
opposed to just throwing dollar bills at a
helicopter.

01:13:01.429 --> 01:13:03.850
Tim Ferriss: So she was, Spinal Tap, "Let's
turn this to 11."

01:13:03.850 --> 01:13:09.120
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, she's basically, "Okay,
buddy, you really, really…"

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:12.850
this was, "...I know you've got this interesting
research, fancy social scientist guy, let's

01:13:12.850 --> 01:13:13.850
see.

01:13:13.850 --> 01:13:15.420
Should we put our money where our mouth is?"

01:13:15.420 --> 01:13:23.060
And by the way, she had also been having dreams
about a little girl who was abandoned.

01:13:23.060 --> 01:13:24.060
Dreams and dreams and dreams at night.

01:13:24.060 --> 01:13:26.139
Tim Ferriss: Can you tell me more about your
wife and how you guys met?

01:13:26.139 --> 01:13:30.909
Because I know a little bit of the background,
but how did that happen?

01:13:30.909 --> 01:13:32.630
Since I'm back on the playing field.

01:13:32.630 --> 01:13:34.230
Arthur Brooks: Back on the playing field time.

01:13:34.230 --> 01:13:36.260
Tim Ferriss: Mm-hmm —
Arthur Brooks: One of the things that I teach

01:13:36.260 --> 01:13:44.520
my students at HBS is that I would never invest
in the firm of an entrepreneur who's unwilling

01:13:44.520 --> 01:13:47.000
to give her or his heart away.

01:13:47.000 --> 01:13:51.909
Because it's the single most risky entrepreneurial
thing that you can do, putting every bit of

01:13:51.909 --> 01:13:53.510
capital at risk.

01:13:53.510 --> 01:13:57.160
If you're not willing to give your heart away,
I'm not going to put my money in your fund.

01:13:57.160 --> 01:14:00.800
Now —
Tim Ferriss: And that takes the form of —

01:14:00.800 --> 01:14:02.060
Arthur Brooks: Risk, man.

01:14:02.060 --> 01:14:03.429
Tim Ferriss: Loving someone wholeheartedly.

01:14:03.429 --> 01:14:04.650
Arthur Brooks: Giving your heart away.

01:14:04.650 --> 01:14:05.650
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:14:05.650 --> 01:14:06.780
Arthur Brooks: Giving your heart away.

01:14:06.780 --> 01:14:10.980
And the reason I believe this is because I've
always had this sense that part of the journey

01:14:10.980 --> 01:14:14.030
of life is just getting in it, getting in
it.

01:14:14.030 --> 01:14:18.250
And entrepreneurs talk a lot about putting
capital at risk and talking about money, how

01:14:18.250 --> 01:14:19.250
boring.

01:14:19.250 --> 01:14:22.889
The real capital of the enterprise, the startup
of Tim Ferriss's life because this is the

01:14:22.889 --> 01:14:27.139
ultimate enterprise — the enterprise is
not the podcast and the books and the company,

01:14:27.139 --> 01:14:28.389
and that's not it.

01:14:28.389 --> 01:14:31.949
Those were manifestations of Tim Ferriss Inc.,
of you.

01:14:31.949 --> 01:14:35.889
The enterprise is you, and the currency of
you is not money, it's love and happiness.

01:14:35.889 --> 01:14:36.950
That's your currency.

01:14:36.950 --> 01:14:42.060
So how are you going to put that at risk for
explosive, tectonic, inflecting returns?

01:14:42.060 --> 01:14:43.700
What are you going to do to put it at risk?

01:14:43.700 --> 01:14:45.469
That's the question.

01:14:45.469 --> 01:14:49.980
And the answers are really tricky, but the
one answer that's not right is don't put it

01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:51.330
at risk.

01:14:51.330 --> 01:14:52.330
Don't put it at risk.

01:14:52.330 --> 01:14:53.330
Tim Ferriss: Right, so I'm with you.

01:14:53.330 --> 01:15:01.320
Now, there are myriad ways that if you were
to sort of take off your flak jacket and just

01:15:01.320 --> 01:15:07.010
walk into oncoming traffic with emotional
oncoming traffic with someone who you've been

01:15:07.010 --> 01:15:10.409
on a dating app who you meet within five minutes
and they're cuckoo bananas, that could turn

01:15:10.409 --> 01:15:11.409
out poorly.

01:15:11.409 --> 01:15:12.580
Arthur Brooks: Of course, that's just Vegas.

01:15:12.580 --> 01:15:14.630
That's not entrepreneurship, that's just gambling.

01:15:14.630 --> 01:15:16.460
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:15:16.460 --> 01:15:17.850
Well, first I want to hear your story.

01:15:17.850 --> 01:15:20.520
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, so this gets back to
thees issue at hand.

01:15:20.520 --> 01:15:25.650
And the reason I did that little prelude is
because I don't want anybody to think that

01:15:25.650 --> 01:15:27.340
my screws are too loose, right?

01:15:27.340 --> 01:15:29.699
Tim Ferriss: That's not my goal.

01:15:29.699 --> 01:15:31.780
And finally —
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it's insanity.

01:15:31.780 --> 01:15:32.780
So okay.

01:15:32.780 --> 01:15:37.540
Tim Ferriss: I rest my case, insane man on
the podcast.

01:15:37.540 --> 01:15:42.290
Arthur Brooks: Deeply unbalanced guest joins
Tim Ferriss.

01:15:42.290 --> 01:15:45.630
When I was 24 years old, I was on a concert
tour and I was making my living as a classical

01:15:45.630 --> 01:15:46.630
French horn player.

01:15:46.630 --> 01:15:52.949
I was on a concert tour in the Burgundy region
of France, ended up going from town to town,

01:15:52.949 --> 01:15:54.030
playing concerts.

01:15:54.030 --> 01:15:57.230
And I was staying at this school.

01:15:57.230 --> 01:16:00.440
I don't know, it's just where we were housed
during this.

01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:01.440
We were going out from there.

01:16:01.440 --> 01:16:05.410
And at the same time, there was a music festival
going on, and there were musicians from all

01:16:05.410 --> 01:16:07.790
over Europe that were studying at this music
festival.

01:16:07.790 --> 01:16:12.580
And I was at this concert playing at this
very school and playing my horn, looking out

01:16:12.580 --> 01:16:13.580
in the audience.

01:16:13.580 --> 01:16:17.710
And there was this girl smiling at me in the
front row, beautiful girl smiling at me, just

01:16:17.710 --> 01:16:18.710
gorgeous.

01:16:18.710 --> 01:16:23.230
And I'm a red-blooded 24-year-old dude, and
obviously, I'm going to make a mental note

01:16:23.230 --> 01:16:25.140
to go talk to her.

01:16:25.140 --> 01:16:26.910
So I go to talk to her later, and I find out
two things, two things.

01:16:26.910 --> 01:16:29.340
Number one, she's not French, even though
I was in France.

01:16:29.340 --> 01:16:31.890
And number two, that she doesn't speak a single
word of English.

01:16:31.890 --> 01:16:37.530
And it was hard because I was trying to talk
to her in monosyllabic grunts and the fruitless

01:16:37.530 --> 01:16:39.110
search for cognates.

01:16:39.110 --> 01:16:41.949
And I said, "Are you single?"

01:16:41.949 --> 01:16:45.090
This is like this basic, "Are you single?"

01:16:45.090 --> 01:16:46.710
And she says, "Yes, I'm divorced."

01:16:46.710 --> 01:16:48.060
I'm not down with that.

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:51.190
But what she meant was "I just broke up with
my boyfriend," but she couldn't come up with

01:16:51.190 --> 01:16:57.330
any Latin-based words besides divorce, which
actually comes from Latin to try to get me

01:16:57.330 --> 01:16:58.330
to understand this point.

01:16:58.330 --> 01:17:00.380
Anyway, so it was a comedy of errors.

01:17:00.380 --> 01:17:04.610
And we went out to dinner and we went on some
dates, and I left after a week, and I went

01:17:04.610 --> 01:17:07.510
home and I called my dad in Seattle.

01:17:07.510 --> 01:17:09.100
I grew up in Seattle, and I was living in
New York at the time.

01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:11.220
I said, "Dad, I think I met the girl I'm going
to marry."

01:17:11.220 --> 01:17:13.020
Tim Ferriss: And where was she from?

01:17:13.020 --> 01:17:14.690
Arthur Brooks: Barcelona.

01:17:14.690 --> 01:17:15.690
Barcelona.

01:17:15.690 --> 01:17:16.690
And he said, "Great, let's meet her."

01:17:16.690 --> 01:17:18.770
And I said, "I've got problems.

01:17:18.770 --> 01:17:21.460
Problem number one is that she doesn't live
in the United States.

01:17:21.460 --> 01:17:23.230
Problem number two is she doesn't speak a
word of English.

01:17:23.230 --> 01:17:28.030
Problem number three is that she made me aware
that she doesn't believe in marriage because

01:17:28.030 --> 01:17:31.380
she thinks it's an anachronistic institution
and she's never going to get married."

01:17:31.380 --> 01:17:32.380
Tim Ferriss: It's a hell of a —
Arthur Brooks: Yeah —

01:17:32.380 --> 01:17:34.580
Tim Ferriss: It's a concept to communicate
in Tarzanese.

01:17:34.580 --> 01:17:36.770
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, no, for sure.

01:17:36.770 --> 01:17:40.020
We were together for a week and we —
Tim Ferriss: You got there.

01:17:40.020 --> 01:17:41.360
Arthur Brooks: We got there.

01:17:41.360 --> 01:17:42.370
And I couldn't get out of my head.

01:17:42.370 --> 01:17:44.320
It was like our Lady of Guadalupe kind of.

01:17:44.320 --> 01:17:45.440
I thought about it.

01:17:45.440 --> 01:17:46.440
I thought about it.

01:17:46.440 --> 01:17:48.870
And we exchanged letters and weirdly somehow
talked on the phone.

01:17:48.870 --> 01:17:51.800
She started taking English classes, I started
studying Spanish.

01:17:51.800 --> 01:17:52.860
Who knows when I'll see her?

01:17:52.860 --> 01:17:55.872
I traveled to Barcelona, saw her, she came
to New York and visited me, just a little

01:17:55.872 --> 01:17:56.940
bit here and there.

01:17:56.940 --> 01:18:01.030
And what I didn't tell her is that I had quit
my job in New York and I'd won an audition

01:18:01.030 --> 01:18:05.530
to be a member of the symphony in Barcelona,
because I wanted to go see if I could close

01:18:05.530 --> 01:18:06.530
that deal.

01:18:06.530 --> 01:18:09.560
If I could change her philosophy of marriage.

01:18:09.560 --> 01:18:12.340
It took me two years, but I closed the deal.

01:18:12.340 --> 01:18:16.860
And 32 years later, we have three adult kids
and one grandson, and I'm still in love.

01:18:16.860 --> 01:18:20.570
Tim Ferriss: So how did you end up debate
teaming that?

01:18:20.570 --> 01:18:21.680
I'm just curious.

01:18:21.680 --> 01:18:22.960
How did —
Arthur Brooks: I just wore her down?

01:18:22.960 --> 01:18:24.170
Tim Ferriss: You just wore her down.

01:18:24.170 --> 01:18:25.380
Arthur Brooks: I just wore her down.

01:18:25.380 --> 01:18:26.870
So living in Barcelona, she was in love with
me.

01:18:26.870 --> 01:18:30.909
And so she's not like, "Get out," it's not
like I was some weird stalker.

01:18:30.909 --> 01:18:35.989
She really was in love with me and she wanted
to be with me, but nobody in her family was

01:18:35.989 --> 01:18:36.989
married.

01:18:36.989 --> 01:18:37.989
This is not what you do in Barcelona.

01:18:37.989 --> 01:18:38.989
Tim Ferriss: I didn't realize that.

01:18:38.989 --> 01:18:41.139
Arthur Brooks: Because it sounds like a big
Catholic country.

01:18:41.139 --> 01:18:43.199
It's a post-Christian country.

01:18:43.199 --> 01:18:45.969
Three percent of the population of Barcelona
ever goes to Mass.

01:18:45.969 --> 01:18:46.969
Three percent.

01:18:46.969 --> 01:18:48.620
Tim Ferriss: I never would've guessed that.

01:18:48.620 --> 01:18:50.210
Arthur Brooks: It's Denmark in the Mediterranean.

01:18:50.210 --> 01:18:51.210
Tim Ferriss: That's incredible.

01:18:51.210 --> 01:18:52.210
I had no idea.

01:18:52.210 --> 01:18:53.210
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, nobody does for sure.

01:18:53.210 --> 01:18:54.889
But it has a long tradition.

01:18:54.889 --> 01:18:59.880
And she comes from a hard, red, communist
atheist family.

01:18:59.880 --> 01:19:02.800
So suffice it to say that our backgrounds
are a little bit different.

01:19:02.800 --> 01:19:06.890
Again, she was beautiful and smart, funny,
and I was in love.

01:19:06.890 --> 01:19:11.190
And just love will out, love will out.

01:19:11.190 --> 01:19:17.512
And finally, at the end of two years, finally
I said — I got down on one knee and I said,

01:19:17.512 --> 01:19:19.350
"Are you ready to change your mind?"

01:19:19.350 --> 01:19:21.090
She said, "Yes."

01:19:21.090 --> 01:19:29.300
Tim Ferriss: All right, so once again, envy
has showed its ugly head.

01:19:29.300 --> 01:19:32.989
And I have to admit my French horn game, very
weak.

01:19:32.989 --> 01:19:33.989
Very weak.

01:19:33.989 --> 01:19:38.220
Arthur Brooks: Turns out the French horn is
the least of it.

01:19:38.220 --> 01:19:40.360
Tim Ferriss: Tight pants, big guns.

01:19:40.360 --> 01:19:42.949
Arthur Brooks: In those days, 6'2 and 142
pounds.

01:19:42.949 --> 01:19:48.190
My wife said that it was like hugging a tight
sack of rocks.

01:19:48.190 --> 01:19:53.050
Now she says it's like a loose leather bag
of ropes.

01:19:53.050 --> 01:19:54.050
So I've made some progress.

01:19:54.050 --> 01:19:55.050
Tim Ferriss: Hey, yeah.

01:19:55.050 --> 01:19:56.050
Arthur Brooks: Is that progress?

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:57.050
Tim Ferriss: It sounds like progress, I don't
know.

01:19:57.050 --> 01:19:58.750
Arthur Brooks: Leather bag of ropes.

01:19:58.750 --> 01:20:01.650
Tim Ferriss: Maybe that should be on my dating
profile.

01:20:01.650 --> 01:20:04.960
If you hug me, I feel like — maybe not.

01:20:04.960 --> 01:20:07.420
That's a little too Silence of the Lambs.

01:20:07.420 --> 01:20:09.910
Arthur Brooks: Babies fighting under a blanket.

01:20:09.910 --> 01:20:10.910
I don't know.

01:20:10.910 --> 01:20:16.850
Tim Ferriss: And maybe you're so far removed
that this doesn't make sense to ask, but what

01:20:16.850 --> 01:20:23.950
advice — also, you're coming from a different
orientation with the Catholicism, and I assume

01:20:23.950 --> 01:20:26.110
maybe wrongly, but I imagine your wife —
Arthur Brooks: It was mine but not hers.

01:20:26.110 --> 01:20:28.340
Tim Ferriss: Right, now, is she fully aboard
the Catholic train?

01:20:28.340 --> 01:20:29.340
Arthur Brooks: Fully aboard.

01:20:29.340 --> 01:20:31.780
But that took a long time.

01:20:31.780 --> 01:20:34.120
Tim Ferriss: Wore her down.

01:20:34.120 --> 01:20:35.890
Arthur Brooks: A lot of prayer.

01:20:35.890 --> 01:20:38.860
As they say, this is one of those problems
that requires prayer and fasting.

01:20:38.860 --> 01:20:40.500
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:20:40.500 --> 01:20:43.880
So you prayed and she fasted, and you're like,
no food.

01:20:43.880 --> 01:20:46.430
“It will put the rosaries on its wrist.”

01:20:46.430 --> 01:20:49.680
All right.

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:52.429
Thoughts on how to find a partner?

01:20:52.429 --> 01:20:56.310
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, no, I think about this
an awful lot because this is the number one

01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:59.710
topic in my Science of Happiness class at
HBS that they want to know about.

01:20:59.710 --> 01:21:00.960
Tim Ferriss: It's got to be.

01:21:00.960 --> 01:21:02.250
Arthur Brooks: Oh, for sure.

01:21:02.250 --> 01:21:03.250
Absolutely.

01:21:03.250 --> 01:21:06.090
Because not only is this something that we
all want, that everybody wants, it's something

01:21:06.090 --> 01:21:07.090
that's getting harder.

01:21:07.090 --> 01:21:10.980
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, all the traditional scaffolding
is gone unless perhaps you're part of, say,

01:21:10.980 --> 01:21:17.370
a Jewish community where that type of Yenta-like
matchmaking is sort of a very esteemed role.

01:21:17.370 --> 01:21:21.080
Arthur Brooks: But even then, the modern world
is encroaching on that.

01:21:21.080 --> 01:21:24.780
And if you look at the comparison between
in the '80s when I was in my twenties or the

01:21:24.780 --> 01:21:27.710
'90s when you were in your twenties — sorry,
I didn't mean to shock you with that.

01:21:27.710 --> 01:21:28.710
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's okay.

01:21:28.710 --> 01:21:30.760
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, I know.

01:21:30.760 --> 01:21:31.760
It's much harder.

01:21:31.760 --> 01:21:35.659
Tim Ferriss: I have the ring of Frodo, so
I'm not planning on aging anymore from this

01:21:35.659 --> 01:21:36.659
point forward.

01:21:36.659 --> 01:21:39.950
Side note, you should look at photos of him
now and 20 years ago, they're the same, the

01:21:39.950 --> 01:21:40.950
actor, anyway.

01:21:40.950 --> 01:21:41.950
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:21:41.950 --> 01:21:42.950
Not me, though.

01:21:42.950 --> 01:21:43.950
Tim Ferriss: For those conspiracy theorists
out there.

01:21:43.950 --> 01:21:45.080
Arthur Brooks: Back when I was in the Barcelona
Symphony, I had this hair.

01:21:45.080 --> 01:21:47.290
It was like a great civilization.

01:21:47.290 --> 01:21:48.690
Tim Ferriss: It's like the locks of Samson.

01:21:48.690 --> 01:21:49.890
Arthur Brooks: That was unbelievable.

01:21:49.890 --> 01:21:51.510
And then I started going bald.

01:21:51.510 --> 01:21:52.570
I'm getting more and more bald.

01:21:52.570 --> 01:21:56.060
And my brother, my older brother, he is very
judgmental about me, he's funny, he loves

01:21:56.060 --> 01:21:58.600
me, but he says, "You deserve it because of
the life you've led."

01:21:58.600 --> 01:22:01.520
Tim Ferriss: "Et tu, Brute."

01:22:01.520 --> 01:22:06.760
Arthur Brooks: It's just a lot of DHT, man.

01:22:06.760 --> 01:22:08.590
Anyway, so what are the guardrails?

01:22:08.590 --> 01:22:10.560
What are the mistakes that we make?

01:22:10.560 --> 01:22:12.100
What's actually making it harder?

01:22:12.100 --> 01:22:14.719
And there's a lot of things that go into it.

01:22:14.719 --> 01:22:19.370
The fact that we're using tech where we would've
used humans is deeply problematic.

01:22:19.370 --> 01:22:22.550
The fact that we have so much deal flow is
making it harder.

01:22:22.550 --> 01:22:24.780
The paradox of choice is a real thing.

01:22:24.780 --> 01:22:26.290
Better, better, better, better.

01:22:26.290 --> 01:22:28.422
There's a ton of research on this, by the
way, Tim.

01:22:28.422 --> 01:22:33.550
You've seen some of this research, for example,
on car purchases where you give two groups.

01:22:33.550 --> 01:22:38.080
There's the treatment and control social science
experiments, two groups, different car buying

01:22:38.080 --> 01:22:39.080
experiences.

01:22:39.080 --> 01:22:42.090
For the first, buys their car and there's
no refund and there's no returns.

01:22:42.090 --> 01:22:45.000
And the other side can return it for any reason
in the next six months.

01:22:45.000 --> 01:22:48.739
And the first group is much happier with their
car because they're not thinking about it

01:22:48.739 --> 01:22:49.739
again and again and again.

01:22:49.739 --> 01:22:52.940
There's no more swiping on their car purchase.

01:22:52.940 --> 01:22:57.409
And so the same thing is when it's very easy
to have a lot of selection, it gets much harder

01:22:57.409 --> 01:22:59.280
to attain satisfaction.

01:22:59.280 --> 01:23:01.670
Tim Ferriss: You're also exhausted more easily
because of the decision fatigue.

01:23:01.670 --> 01:23:02.670
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:23:02.670 --> 01:23:05.620
I'll have to take your word for it, but that's
certainly true.

01:23:05.620 --> 01:23:06.620
That's in the data.

01:23:06.620 --> 01:23:09.170
Tim Ferriss: Well, I'm saying not outside
of dating, just in general.

01:23:09.170 --> 01:23:10.170
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:23:10.170 --> 01:23:11.170
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:23:11.170 --> 01:23:13.889
Arthur Brooks: But the bigger problem actually
comes because we're looking for the wrong

01:23:13.889 --> 01:23:15.550
thing in a partner.

01:23:15.550 --> 01:23:20.250
The data suggests that what everybody wants
in a partner that they can curate carefully

01:23:20.250 --> 01:23:24.760
because of the online presence, because of
the platforms, is they want compatibility

01:23:24.760 --> 01:23:27.090
and they look for compatibility with sameness
to them.

01:23:27.090 --> 01:23:28.350
So, "I like this kind of food.

01:23:28.350 --> 01:23:29.350
You do too?

01:23:29.350 --> 01:23:30.350
Great.

01:23:30.350 --> 01:23:31.350
I vote like this.

01:23:31.350 --> 01:23:32.350
You do too?

01:23:32.350 --> 01:23:33.350
Great.

01:23:33.350 --> 01:23:34.350
I'm this religion, or I'm no religion.

01:23:34.350 --> 01:23:35.350
I grew up here.

01:23:35.350 --> 01:23:36.350
I went to college.

01:23:36.350 --> 01:23:37.350
Where did you go to college?"

01:23:37.350 --> 01:23:38.350
Et cetera.

01:23:38.350 --> 01:23:39.350
Tim Ferriss: They're not looking for someone
who doesn't speak their language, who is —

01:23:39.350 --> 01:23:42.030
Arthur Brooks: They're looking for a sibling.

01:23:42.030 --> 01:23:44.760
And as my adult kids would say, "That's not
hot."

01:23:44.760 --> 01:23:45.760
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:23:45.760 --> 01:23:50.060
Arthur Brooks: The truth is we're way too
compatible and we're sorting on compatibility

01:23:50.060 --> 01:23:52.660
with these technological means.

01:23:52.660 --> 01:23:57.700
And the biggest problem with dating today
is we're less and less attracted to people.

01:23:57.700 --> 01:24:00.540
We're less and less attracted because they're
too much like us.

01:24:00.540 --> 01:24:03.210
We need more complementarity and less compatibility.

01:24:03.210 --> 01:24:08.640
Back in the old days, you used to say opposites
attract, not true, but you need a baseline

01:24:08.640 --> 01:24:09.929
of compatibility.

01:24:09.929 --> 01:24:12.230
But on top of that, you need difference.

01:24:12.230 --> 01:24:13.530
Difference is hot.

01:24:13.530 --> 01:24:14.739
Difference is fun.

01:24:14.739 --> 01:24:17.630
Difference is an adventure, and you're just
not going to find it because nobody's going

01:24:17.630 --> 01:24:21.620
to swipe on you, quite frankly, if you're
the other party.

01:24:21.620 --> 01:24:25.750
And a lot of people curate on language and
culture and race, it's insanity.

01:24:25.750 --> 01:24:32.150
That's the reason that good old-fashioned
human people who say they could fall in love,

01:24:32.150 --> 01:24:37.260
when you talk about people who fix you up
on a blind date or the old-fashioned matchmakers,

01:24:37.260 --> 01:24:41.760
they're always looking about complementarity.

01:24:41.760 --> 01:24:46.720
This introvert and this extrovert can fill
in the gaps in each other in a kind of divine

01:24:46.720 --> 01:24:48.520
and cosmic way.

01:24:48.520 --> 01:24:50.570
And that's kind of how I always felt.

01:24:50.570 --> 01:24:51.580
That's how I always kind of felt.

01:24:51.580 --> 01:24:55.949
I felt like my wife was — I don't believe
in magical thinking on this.

01:24:55.949 --> 01:24:59.750
Magical thinking is a big problem because
soulmates don't exist and there's no such

01:24:59.750 --> 01:25:01.410
thing as love at first sight.

01:25:01.410 --> 01:25:06.580
But I always do feel like my wife, Ester,
she was picked for me.

01:25:06.580 --> 01:25:11.260
She was really picked for me and because of
the difference as much as anything else, She

01:25:11.260 --> 01:25:12.520
completes me.

01:25:12.520 --> 01:25:14.310
She makes me a better man.

01:25:14.310 --> 01:25:19.300
She knows when I'm going after the idol, she
can see it going after the lore.

01:25:19.300 --> 01:25:20.320
She can see it.

01:25:20.320 --> 01:25:21.340
She's like, "Mmm, right."

01:25:21.340 --> 01:25:23.129
And that's 32 years of experience together.

01:25:23.129 --> 01:25:26.560
But it's also the deep complementarity that
came through because I was born at a particular

01:25:26.560 --> 01:25:32.080
time and I accidentally had this kind of experience,
and that's what we need.

01:25:32.080 --> 01:25:33.080
That's number one.

01:25:33.080 --> 01:25:34.080
This is clear.

01:25:34.080 --> 01:25:35.860
The second thing is goals.

01:25:35.860 --> 01:25:36.860
Goals.

01:25:36.860 --> 01:25:42.949
And I ask people, "You're going to meet the
person of your dreams, or the good-enough

01:25:42.949 --> 01:25:46.570
person, or whatever; what do you want to have
after five years?"

01:25:46.570 --> 01:25:49.560
Tim Ferriss: The good-enough person.

01:25:49.560 --> 01:25:53.320
Tim Ferriss’s Guide —
Arthur Brooks: The good-enough wife.

01:25:53.320 --> 01:25:55.630
Tim Ferriss: — to Passable Relationships.

01:25:55.630 --> 01:25:59.900
Arthur Brooks: Wife me up with someone good
enough, man.

01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:03.900
Anyway, so yeah, I know.

01:26:03.900 --> 01:26:05.480
Because by the way —
Tim Ferriss: Step one, their criminal record.

01:26:05.480 --> 01:26:06.480
Just kidding.

01:26:06.480 --> 01:26:07.480
Arthur Brooks: I don't know, man, complementarity.

01:26:07.480 --> 01:26:08.550
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, maybe.

01:26:08.550 --> 01:26:11.219
Arthur Brooks: It's like, "You've been in
prison, I haven't.

01:26:11.219 --> 01:26:12.370
We complete each other."

01:26:12.370 --> 01:26:14.960
Tim Ferriss: Clearly whatever I've been trying
isn't working.

01:26:14.960 --> 01:26:17.199
Maybe I'll skip Barcelona and do the prison
circuit.

01:26:17.199 --> 01:26:18.199
All right.

01:26:18.199 --> 01:26:19.199
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:26:19.199 --> 01:26:22.230
But when you ask people "What do you want
your relationship to look like in five years?"

01:26:22.230 --> 01:26:26.159
They have this magical notion of an ongoing
passion.

01:26:26.159 --> 01:26:28.590
And that's the wrong goal.

01:26:28.590 --> 01:26:32.070
The right goal is what is called companionate
love.

01:26:32.070 --> 01:26:34.510
You want to be best friends in five years.

01:26:34.510 --> 01:26:35.880
Five years to best friends, that's the goal.

01:26:35.880 --> 01:26:36.880
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I like that.

01:26:36.880 --> 01:26:39.869
Arthur Brooks: And if you write it out and
if you actually make it the goal and then

01:26:39.869 --> 01:26:44.070
you have interim steps, then a strategic plan,
you might just get there.

01:26:44.070 --> 01:26:47.820
But if you're like, yeah, no, it's like if
the magic feels like the magic's gone, obviously

01:26:47.820 --> 01:26:49.770
there's something wrong with our love.

01:26:49.770 --> 01:26:53.409
No, best friends who are married, they're
going to have plenty of passion, but you can't

01:26:53.409 --> 01:26:54.610
live like that.

01:26:54.610 --> 01:26:59.989
Look, the neurophysiological cascade of the
experience of falling in love is unbelievably

01:26:59.989 --> 01:27:00.989
intense.

01:27:00.989 --> 01:27:01.989
It's 4th of July in your head.

01:27:01.989 --> 01:27:07.480
The bolus of testosterone and estrogen at
the very beginning of the relationship, that

01:27:07.480 --> 01:27:14.989
massive increases in norepinephrine and dopamine
that give you euphoria and concentration and

01:27:14.989 --> 01:27:19.320
focus on the other person, the deep dip in
serotonin, your serotonin dips.

01:27:19.320 --> 01:27:20.320
Why?

01:27:20.320 --> 01:27:24.560
Because you want to ruminate on that person
using the good old ventral lateral prefrontal

01:27:24.560 --> 01:27:25.560
cortex.

01:27:25.560 --> 01:27:29.119
That's the reason that falling in love is
an awful lot like being clinically depressed.

01:27:29.119 --> 01:27:32.900
So you're both addicted to meth and depressed
at the same time.

01:27:32.900 --> 01:27:34.000
That's what falling in love is like.

01:27:34.000 --> 01:27:40.179
And then you're going to get that big, big,
big dose of oxytocin, which is the neuropeptide

01:27:40.179 --> 01:27:44.430
that links you to the other person in this
almost magical, confusing way.

01:27:44.430 --> 01:27:45.430
Man, that's just too much.

01:27:45.430 --> 01:27:47.640
You don't want that for the rest of your life.

01:27:47.640 --> 01:27:48.640
Tim Ferriss: Sounds like a lot.

01:27:48.640 --> 01:27:50.220
Arthur Brooks: Well, you'll be institutionalized.

01:27:50.220 --> 01:27:55.350
Tim Ferriss: All right, so let's pause there
for a second.

01:27:55.350 --> 01:27:56.510
Arthur Brooks: That was a lot.

01:27:56.510 --> 01:27:57.810
Tim Ferriss: It was a lot.

01:27:57.810 --> 01:27:58.969
Arthur Brooks: Are you overstimulated?

01:27:58.969 --> 01:28:06.040
Tim Ferriss: I want to go from falling in
love as meth and depression to the lightness

01:28:06.040 --> 01:28:07.040
of death.

01:28:07.040 --> 01:28:09.980
Arthur Brooks: And we haven't even talked
about meaning yet, man.

01:28:09.980 --> 01:28:11.160
Tim Ferriss: Oh, we're going to get there.

01:28:11.160 --> 01:28:12.160
Arthur Brooks: We'll get there by the ninth
hour.

01:28:12.160 --> 01:28:13.160
Tim Ferriss: I'm keeping track.

01:28:13.160 --> 01:28:14.160
Arthur Brooks: Hour nine.

01:28:14.160 --> 01:28:15.160
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm keeping track.

01:28:15.160 --> 01:28:17.090
Hour nine, I have it bookmarked.

01:28:17.090 --> 01:28:21.670
Also, I have a promise I made to listeners
that I have not fulfilled, which is your personal

01:28:21.670 --> 01:28:27.170
examples for using money for experiences,
time, whatever comes to mind.

01:28:27.170 --> 01:28:28.170
So we'll get to that.

01:28:28.170 --> 01:28:31.679
But before that, death meditation.

01:28:31.679 --> 01:28:38.150
Could you please describe your death meditation
and why you have a death meditation?

01:28:38.150 --> 01:28:42.580
Arthur Brooks: Most people watching us are
not afraid of death, not pathologically afraid

01:28:42.580 --> 01:28:43.580
of death.

01:28:43.580 --> 01:28:45.920
Only about 20 percent of the population is
pathologically afraid of death.

01:28:45.920 --> 01:28:52.159
And I bet you that it's less than one percent
of Tim Ferriss's followers because people

01:28:52.159 --> 01:28:55.200
watching this are in control of their lives
and they understand the contours of their

01:28:55.200 --> 01:29:01.139
lives and they're looking at the truth of
their lives, but they have a death fear.

01:29:01.139 --> 01:29:05.590
The death fear isn't physical death, and this
is a problem.

01:29:05.590 --> 01:29:06.900
For some people, it's irrelevance.

01:29:06.900 --> 01:29:09.179
For some people, it's being forgotten.

01:29:09.179 --> 01:29:15.210
For a lot of your listeners, it's failure,
just straight on failure because they're strivers,

01:29:15.210 --> 01:29:17.010
they're achievers.

01:29:17.010 --> 01:29:18.010
Everybody has a death fear.

01:29:18.010 --> 01:29:19.010
And what is it?

01:29:19.010 --> 01:29:20.010
It's an ego threat.

01:29:20.010 --> 01:29:22.570
It's a threat to who you see as yourself.

01:29:22.570 --> 01:29:29.639
If Tim Ferriss's Tim Ferrissness is threatened,
that will provoke a panic in you because that's

01:29:29.639 --> 01:29:32.739
the ultimate death of who you see yourself.

01:29:32.739 --> 01:29:35.800
The way to get over that — and by the way,
you have to get over that, we all have to

01:29:35.800 --> 01:29:39.139
get over that, or we won't be fully alive
until we actually face the death that really

01:29:39.139 --> 01:29:40.510
matters to us.

01:29:40.510 --> 01:29:44.369
This is an insight that actually comes from
the Theravada Buddhist across the southern

01:29:44.369 --> 01:29:52.750
tier of Asia, from Thailand and Myanmar to
Vietnam and parts of Malaysia and the Sri

01:29:52.750 --> 01:29:54.050
Lanka in particular.

01:29:54.050 --> 01:29:59.869
So the Theravada Buddhist monasteries, often
you'll go in and you'll see photos of cadavers

01:29:59.869 --> 01:30:03.719
in different states of decomposition, super
macabre.

01:30:03.719 --> 01:30:07.420
You're like, what are these dudes all about
here?

01:30:07.420 --> 01:30:14.949
And what they do is that usually there will
be nine states of decomposition; photos, bodies

01:30:14.949 --> 01:30:21.239
decomposing, falling apart, bones, bloated
corpses, and they stand in front of each one

01:30:21.239 --> 01:30:24.500
and they say, that is me.

01:30:24.500 --> 01:30:28.270
And then they move to the next one after contemplating
and saying, and that is me.

01:30:28.270 --> 01:30:29.400
And what are they doing?

01:30:29.400 --> 01:30:34.600
They're familiarizing themselves with the
truth of their future such that they can be

01:30:34.600 --> 01:30:38.760
liberated from any fear of physical death.

01:30:38.760 --> 01:30:40.989
Only then can they be fully alive.

01:30:40.989 --> 01:30:44.330
That's an important insight from Theravada
Buddhism.

01:30:44.330 --> 01:30:46.290
That's a meditation called Maranasati.

01:30:46.290 --> 01:30:47.290
Tim Ferriss: Maranasati.

01:30:47.290 --> 01:30:50.219
Arthur Brooks: The Maranasati death meditation.

01:30:50.219 --> 01:30:56.440
Now, when I read that, I thought it was interesting,
but I thought it's a good thing to do because

01:30:56.440 --> 01:31:00.929
I shouldn't be making all of these decisions
about when I'm old and when I'm retired and

01:31:00.929 --> 01:31:03.631
when I die and what's going to happen and
call the lawyer and all that.

01:31:03.631 --> 01:31:09.989
I want to be free from all that stuff by understanding
that life and death are in a very real way

01:31:09.989 --> 01:31:16.040
an illusion, particularly because I believe
in eternal life, but I have a death fear.

01:31:16.040 --> 01:31:18.369
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah.

01:31:18.369 --> 01:31:20.124
Arthur Brooks: You know what I'm afraid of?

01:31:20.124 --> 01:31:21.124
You know my death fear?

01:31:21.124 --> 01:31:24.710
Tim Ferriss: Who's going to take care of your
cats?

01:31:24.710 --> 01:31:25.870
No, I don't know.

01:31:25.870 --> 01:31:27.750
Arthur Brooks: That's good, man.

01:31:27.750 --> 01:31:31.070
Who's going to take care of my dog, [Chu-cho].

01:31:31.070 --> 01:31:32.070
Yeah.

01:31:32.070 --> 01:31:33.980
It's losing my mind.

01:31:33.980 --> 01:31:35.320
Tim Ferriss: On the way there.

01:31:35.320 --> 01:31:36.320
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:31:36.320 --> 01:31:37.770
Because you know what Arthur Brooks is?

01:31:37.770 --> 01:31:38.770
Gray matter.

01:31:38.770 --> 01:31:41.550
Look, I'm not the smartest guy in the world,
I'm not the cleverest guy in the world, but

01:31:41.550 --> 01:31:46.750
I make my living and I support my family,
and I understand me in terms of my ideas.

01:31:46.750 --> 01:31:50.400
Literally, my company is called ACB Ideas,
right?

01:31:50.400 --> 01:31:54.890
It was very revealing to me when I contemplated
the fact that I —

01:31:54.890 --> 01:31:55.890
Tim Ferriss: Did you say ACB?

01:31:55.890 --> 01:31:57.550
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, it's Arthur Charles Brooks.

01:31:57.550 --> 01:32:00.330
Tim Ferriss: Oh, okay, it took me a second.

01:32:00.330 --> 01:32:02.900
Arthur Brooks: I just can't remember the alphabet.

01:32:02.900 --> 01:32:03.900
Little dyslexic.

01:32:03.900 --> 01:32:06.869
It's like X, Z, Y. Anyway.

01:32:06.869 --> 01:32:09.850
Tim Ferriss: I'm from Long Island.

01:32:09.850 --> 01:32:10.850
Sorry, guys.

01:32:10.850 --> 01:32:12.330
Arthur Brooks: Yes.

01:32:12.330 --> 01:32:21.090
And my mother was showing signs of dementia
in her early fifties, and she was quite demented

01:32:21.090 --> 01:32:22.340
by the time she was my age.

01:32:22.340 --> 01:32:24.050
And it runs in families.

01:32:24.050 --> 01:32:25.050
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:32:25.050 --> 01:32:26.050
Arthur Brooks: It's genetic.

01:32:26.050 --> 01:32:27.640
It doesn't mean I'm going to get the bullet.

01:32:27.640 --> 01:32:31.640
I'm not being morbid about it, I'm not being
fatalistic about it.

01:32:31.640 --> 01:32:35.530
Probably the odds are in my favor, but I'm
just terrified of that.

01:32:35.530 --> 01:32:40.540
And when I recognized that, I realized I need
a Maranasati meditation on that.

01:32:40.540 --> 01:32:42.280
And that's what I contemplate.

01:32:42.280 --> 01:32:47.150
I'll take two minutes and I'll think my memory's
failing and I don't know why.

01:32:47.150 --> 01:32:52.900
And then I imagine going to the neurologist,
the neurologist saying, well, I think you

01:32:52.900 --> 01:32:55.800
need to come into my office.

01:32:55.800 --> 01:33:00.810
And then I imagine myself telling my kids,
the future's going to be rough.

01:33:00.810 --> 01:33:07.139
And then I imagine my work slipping away,
my inability to have this kind of conversation,

01:33:07.139 --> 01:33:11.500
to write a book, to share an idea, to help
somebody with these ideas.

01:33:11.500 --> 01:33:15.520
And then I imagine myself not remembering
what I don't remember.

01:33:15.520 --> 01:33:23.030
And then I imagine being a beloved son of
God with no memory at all.

01:33:23.030 --> 01:33:24.030
Now it's heavy, right?

01:33:24.030 --> 01:33:26.840
Tim Ferriss: It seems like that ending point
is important.

01:33:26.840 --> 01:33:31.429
Arthur Brooks: It's the most important because
you end on the truth.

01:33:31.429 --> 01:33:36.060
Tim Ferriss: So if you're not religious, you
just end up to use the parlance of the kids

01:33:36.060 --> 01:33:38.660
these days, just depressed AF.

01:33:38.660 --> 01:33:40.060
Are you just —
Arthur Brooks: No.

01:33:40.060 --> 01:33:41.210
Tim Ferriss: Shit out of luck?

01:33:41.210 --> 01:33:42.210
Arthur Brooks: No.

01:33:42.210 --> 01:33:43.210
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

01:33:43.210 --> 01:33:46.179
Arthur Brooks: Because in the end of the day,
in the Maranasati meditation, you recognize

01:33:46.179 --> 01:33:51.580
the illusion of the tragedy that was your
death in the first place.

01:33:51.580 --> 01:33:55.070
Ashes to ashes, dust to dust.

01:33:55.070 --> 01:33:57.070
New life will emerge.

01:33:57.070 --> 01:33:58.070
Will it be you?

01:33:58.070 --> 01:33:59.250
Will it be somebody else?

01:33:59.250 --> 01:34:01.040
Do you care?

01:34:01.040 --> 01:34:04.620
See, when you look into the abyss, you can
cope with it.

01:34:04.620 --> 01:34:06.020
You're stronger than that.

01:34:06.020 --> 01:34:11.110
My students, by the way, at Harvard, graduate
students, Harvard Business School, best in

01:34:11.110 --> 01:34:16.850
the world, so we like to think, they're afraid
of failure because they've never failed.

01:34:16.850 --> 01:34:17.900
They've never failed.

01:34:17.900 --> 01:34:24.600
You and I have struggled and we're older and
I've failed a lot, but my students are deeply

01:34:24.600 --> 01:34:25.600
afraid of failure.

01:34:25.600 --> 01:34:28.030
I basically got kicked out of college when
I was 19.

01:34:28.030 --> 01:34:31.409
It turns out you're not supposed to drop all
your required classes and take nothing but

01:34:31.409 --> 01:34:33.660
Indonesian dance and North Indian classical
drumming.

01:34:33.660 --> 01:34:36.590
Turns out that's not the secret to academic
success.

01:34:36.590 --> 01:34:39.630
Kids, make a note of it.

01:34:39.630 --> 01:34:42.140
So I failed, right, but a lot of my students
haven't.

01:34:42.140 --> 01:34:47.780
And so I asked them to do the Maranasati meditation
on their own academic and professional failure.

01:34:47.780 --> 01:34:51.280
Number one, two minutes.

01:34:51.280 --> 01:34:55.739
"I think I'm falling behind academically,
and the people around me are getting better

01:34:55.739 --> 01:34:56.739
grades."

01:34:56.739 --> 01:35:00.200
Two, "I just got put on academic probation.

01:35:00.200 --> 01:35:03.600
This was my dream to come into this school
and I'm not succeeding."

01:35:03.600 --> 01:35:09.409
Three, "The job mark is looking bad compared
to other people, I can't believe it."

01:35:09.409 --> 01:35:14.170
Four, "I think I need to move home for a while."

01:35:14.170 --> 01:35:17.670
Five, "My parents feel sorry for me."

01:35:17.670 --> 01:35:22.080
That's when they cry, right?

01:35:22.080 --> 01:35:25.710
Because all you want is for Mom to be proud
of you.

01:35:25.710 --> 01:35:30.500
That's all you want when you're a success
addict, that's all you want.

01:35:30.500 --> 01:35:31.500
And imagine that.

01:35:31.500 --> 01:35:32.600
Look into that.

01:35:32.600 --> 01:35:34.449
Stare into it.

01:35:34.449 --> 01:35:35.719
Stare into it.

01:35:35.719 --> 01:35:36.719
And they do.

01:35:36.719 --> 01:35:39.690
And they get over it and they can master it.

01:35:39.690 --> 01:35:44.070
That's the Maranasati death meditation on
the self-objectification of the success addict.

01:35:44.070 --> 01:35:46.540
Tim Ferriss: See, what do you see in those
students?

01:35:46.540 --> 01:35:52.340
Because they come from all different backgrounds
and different orientations, religious or not,

01:35:52.340 --> 01:35:53.340
or somewhere in between.

01:35:53.340 --> 01:35:54.340
Arthur Brooks: Some are, some aren't.

01:35:54.340 --> 01:35:55.340
Yeah.

01:35:55.340 --> 01:35:56.340
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:35:56.340 --> 01:35:59.199
What types of effects and how frequently do
you personally do this type of death meditation?

01:35:59.199 --> 01:36:00.500
Arthur Brooks: A lot.

01:36:00.500 --> 01:36:02.020
Because I have my own death meditation.

01:36:02.020 --> 01:36:05.530
I contemplate failure sometimes because I'm
afraid of it, but I'm not deathly afraid of

01:36:05.530 --> 01:36:06.530
it.

01:36:06.530 --> 01:36:11.170
The truth of the matter is the great thing
about being 46 as opposed to 26 for you, the

01:36:11.170 --> 01:36:16.760
great thing about being 59 instead 29 for
me is that bad stuff is going to happen and

01:36:16.760 --> 01:36:18.120
it's going to be okay.

01:36:18.120 --> 01:36:19.989
Bad stuff's just going to happen, right?

01:36:19.989 --> 01:36:23.890
The truth is that we realize, you and I both
realize that the worst thing that's ever happened

01:36:23.890 --> 01:36:28.619
in life probably hasn't happened yet.

01:36:28.619 --> 01:36:31.260
And okay, that's heavy, but it's not that
heavy.

01:36:31.260 --> 01:36:32.260
We're going to survive.

01:36:32.260 --> 01:36:33.820
You've survived a lot and it's actually going
to be okay.

01:36:33.820 --> 01:36:36.520
I don't want to fail.

01:36:36.520 --> 01:36:37.520
We have a book coming out.

01:36:37.520 --> 01:36:43.500
I have a big book coming out a few weeks from
when we're taping this episode, and I deeply

01:36:43.500 --> 01:36:47.260
wanted to be successful, but I can't control
that.

01:36:47.260 --> 01:36:51.950
And so I don't have to do much more than a
couple of Maranasati meditations on the market

01:36:51.950 --> 01:36:53.480
failure of a book.

01:36:53.480 --> 01:36:57.199
But I have to do it about losing my mind because
that feels deeply existential to me.

01:36:57.199 --> 01:36:58.639
I have to do it a lot.

01:36:58.639 --> 01:37:02.720
I do it at least once a week actually, because
I want to remember.

01:37:02.720 --> 01:37:03.720
I need to remember.

01:37:03.720 --> 01:37:08.469
And if I don't, I'm going to be walking kind
of on eggshells and just sort of wondering

01:37:08.469 --> 01:37:10.929
and having that kind of minor sense of dread.

01:37:10.929 --> 01:37:11.929
I don't want to live that way.

01:37:11.929 --> 01:37:12.929
I don't need to live that way.

01:37:12.929 --> 01:37:14.780
It's not important for me to live that way.

01:37:14.780 --> 01:37:17.480
On the contrary, it's important for me not
to live that way because I won't be fully

01:37:17.480 --> 01:37:18.480
alive now.

01:37:18.480 --> 01:37:23.030
I'll be living prospectively in a future,
and that future is dominated by fear.

01:37:23.030 --> 01:37:25.680
And then I'm really not washing the dishes.

01:37:25.680 --> 01:37:27.550
I'm really not enjoying that juicy peach.

01:37:27.550 --> 01:37:32.820
On the contrary, I'm breaking my teeth on
the pit, and it's not even a real pit.

01:37:32.820 --> 01:37:33.889
That's crazy.

01:37:33.889 --> 01:37:37.840
Tim Ferriss: So —
Arthur Brooks: Not yet, really yet.

01:37:37.840 --> 01:37:38.880
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:37:38.880 --> 01:37:46.119
This I think is a good tie-in to meaning,
and I'm going to get there with a self-indulgent

01:37:46.119 --> 01:37:47.730
reference back to Marcus Aurelius.

01:37:47.730 --> 01:37:49.300
For people who have not read Meditations.

01:37:49.300 --> 01:37:50.300
Read Meditations.

01:37:50.300 --> 01:37:52.200
It was never intended for publication.

01:37:52.200 --> 01:38:00.780
These are effectively the pocket musings of
someone deeply conflicted, but also incredibly

01:38:00.780 --> 01:38:04.010
impressive during war campaigns and otherwise.

01:38:04.010 --> 01:38:05.780
But lots of thoughts on death.

01:38:05.780 --> 01:38:10.440
And also for those interested, there's considered
Stoic practice that exists in a lot of different

01:38:10.440 --> 01:38:16.390
varieties, but memento mori, there are these
meditations, premeditatio malorum.

01:38:16.390 --> 01:38:20.850
Arthur Brooks: It's the Greek Maranasati,
for all intents and purposes, or Roman.

01:38:20.850 --> 01:38:21.850
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

01:38:21.850 --> 01:38:24.830
He also thought very deeply on meaning.

01:38:24.830 --> 01:38:25.830
Let's come to meaning.

01:38:25.830 --> 01:38:30.660
So we identified two, I guess, of the legs
on the —

01:38:30.660 --> 01:38:32.230
Arthur Brooks: Stool, right.

01:38:32.230 --> 01:38:33.400
Tim Ferriss: Meaning.

01:38:33.400 --> 01:38:36.000
How should we think about meaning?

01:38:36.000 --> 01:38:37.929
Arthur Brooks: Meaning's the hard one.

01:38:37.929 --> 01:38:38.929
Meaning's the hard one.

01:38:38.929 --> 01:38:42.770
Look, enjoyment's no joke and satisfaction
takes work, but meaning eludes some people

01:38:42.770 --> 01:38:46.250
their entire lives because they don't know
what they're looking for.

01:38:46.250 --> 01:38:47.909
They're fumbling around for something.

01:38:47.909 --> 01:38:49.280
They don't know exactly what it is.

01:38:49.280 --> 01:38:56.000
And a lot of people, they deeply suspect that
it doesn't exist, that it doesn't actually

01:38:56.000 --> 01:38:57.000
exist.

01:38:57.000 --> 01:39:00.409
You look at a lot of 20th century and 19th
century philosophers and they say it doesn't

01:39:00.409 --> 01:39:01.409
exist.

01:39:01.409 --> 01:39:03.800
I mean, there's three schools of thought about
meaning.

01:39:03.800 --> 01:39:09.599
There's the ancient Greeks and Romans and
the Christians and the Jews and the Muslims,

01:39:09.599 --> 01:39:13.099
and it's all kind of based on meaning in the
following way.

01:39:13.099 --> 01:39:15.580
Essence precedes existence.

01:39:15.580 --> 01:39:20.920
Meaning, you have meaning in life that precedes
your actual life, and your job is to find

01:39:20.920 --> 01:39:22.119
it and live up to it.

01:39:22.119 --> 01:39:24.740
But it's already out there, you just need
to go looking for it.

01:39:24.740 --> 01:39:32.590
In the 19th and 20th centuries, that was relaxed
with two major schools of philosophical thought,

01:39:32.590 --> 01:39:34.449
Nihilism and Existentialism.

01:39:34.449 --> 01:39:39.020
Existentialism, Sartre, and even to a certain
extent, Kierkegaard, would say that essence

01:39:39.020 --> 01:39:41.080
precedes, no, that existence precedes essence.

01:39:41.080 --> 01:39:45.550
In other words, you're born and there is no
meaning until you create it.

01:39:45.550 --> 01:39:46.550
Tim Ferriss: Tabula rasa.

01:39:46.550 --> 01:39:47.550
Good luck, kid.

01:39:47.550 --> 01:39:48.550
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, good luck.

01:39:48.550 --> 01:39:49.550
Go make it.

01:39:49.550 --> 01:39:50.550
If it's no good, it's on you, man.

01:39:50.550 --> 01:39:55.490
By the way, Sartre, he has a very empowering,
a very muscular philosophy, because he says,

01:39:55.490 --> 01:40:00.400
"You have to live up to the responsibility
of creating your essence and living according

01:40:00.400 --> 01:40:01.400
to it."

01:40:01.400 --> 01:40:03.650
It's sort of Freudian in its way.

01:40:03.650 --> 01:40:07.020
And then, of course, there's Nietzsche, our
old pal Friedrich.

01:40:07.020 --> 01:40:11.970
A lot of young men love Nietzsche, because
by the way, it's unbelievably beautiful prose,

01:40:11.970 --> 01:40:14.550
a gorgeous writer, including in English.

01:40:14.550 --> 01:40:18.610
You don't have to go learn German to read
it, but The Gay Science, which is one of his

01:40:18.610 --> 01:40:22.180
most famous texts where he said, "God is dead
and we killed him."

01:40:22.180 --> 01:40:25.469
It's just, it lands, right?

01:40:25.469 --> 01:40:30.760
But his whole point is, existence is real,
but essence is a figment of your imagination.

01:40:30.760 --> 01:40:32.620
So don't even try to find it.

01:40:32.620 --> 01:40:34.510
That's Nihilism, right?

01:40:34.510 --> 01:40:39.380
We're struggling with these schools of thought
and we all suspect it doesn't matter how religious

01:40:39.380 --> 01:40:43.599
you are, it doesn't matter what your wiring
actually happens to be.

01:40:43.599 --> 01:40:47.619
You kind of wonder if maybe Nietzche and Sartre
were right.

01:40:47.619 --> 01:40:51.930
So you go in search of it and it turns out
"I want to go find the meaning of life" is

01:40:51.930 --> 01:40:53.160
too big a question.

01:40:53.160 --> 01:41:00.389
You'll never find it by sitting at the mouth
of the guru's cave or with the ayahuasca shaman

01:41:00.389 --> 01:41:02.540
saying, "I just want to find meaning in life."

01:41:02.540 --> 01:41:06.349
You need to boil it down to really sub-questions,
which are all about coherence.

01:41:06.349 --> 01:41:09.199
"Why do things happen the way they do?

01:41:09.199 --> 01:41:13.449
I need to believe something about why things
happen the way they do."

01:41:13.449 --> 01:41:14.449
It doesn't mean religious.

01:41:14.449 --> 01:41:15.580
It can be completely secular.

01:41:15.580 --> 01:41:17.960
It might even be nihilistic.

01:41:17.960 --> 01:41:21.179
You need to purpose, you need to answer a
purpose question, which is, "What's the purpose

01:41:21.179 --> 01:41:22.590
of my life and what direction am I going?

01:41:22.590 --> 01:41:23.590
What's the goal of my life?

01:41:23.590 --> 01:41:25.080
What's the end point of my life?"

01:41:25.080 --> 01:41:30.070
And the last is significance, which is why
does it matter that I'm alive?

01:41:30.070 --> 01:41:35.290
Now I have a test to see if somebody has a
meaning crisis in their life.

01:41:35.290 --> 01:41:37.190
That's really just two questions.

01:41:37.190 --> 01:41:43.020
And to pass the test, you need incredibly
true, honest, and compelling answers.

01:41:43.020 --> 01:41:44.060
There's no right answers.

01:41:44.060 --> 01:41:47.300
There's only wrong answers or no answers.

01:41:47.300 --> 01:41:50.440
So you want to take the test?

01:41:50.440 --> 01:41:52.840
Tim Ferriss: Sure, why not?

01:41:52.840 --> 01:41:55.239
Arthur Brooks: It’s go time.

01:41:55.239 --> 01:41:56.239
Question number one.

01:41:56.239 --> 01:41:57.239
This is, and again —
Tim Ferriss: This is to punish me for all

01:41:57.239 --> 01:41:58.239
the ayahuasca I've done.

01:41:58.239 --> 01:41:59.239
Go for it.

01:41:59.239 --> 01:42:02.930
Arthur Brooks: I didn't say this just so you're
wrong with it.

01:42:02.930 --> 01:42:05.699
I just think, what do we say?

01:42:05.699 --> 01:42:07.119
It's necessary but not sufficient.

01:42:07.119 --> 01:42:08.119
Tim Ferriss: Where’s my cat o' nine tails.

01:42:08.119 --> 01:42:09.469
I had can opus Dei myself.

01:42:09.469 --> 01:42:12.849
I left it back at the hotel.

01:42:12.849 --> 01:42:14.050
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:42:14.050 --> 01:42:15.250
Tim Ferriss: Damn.

01:42:15.250 --> 01:42:16.250
All right.

01:42:16.250 --> 01:42:18.620
Arthur Brooks: Why are you alive?

01:42:18.620 --> 01:42:21.739
Do you have an answer to the question of why
you're alive?

01:42:21.739 --> 01:42:24.730
Tim Ferriss: I mean, I have a very clinical
answer for it.

01:42:24.730 --> 01:42:25.730
Arthur Brooks: Tell me.

01:42:25.730 --> 01:42:29.170
Well, I mean, there's the physical answer,
but metaphysically, why are you alive?

01:42:29.170 --> 01:42:31.310
Which can be one of two things.

01:42:31.310 --> 01:42:35.239
Either who created you or what you're put
on Earth to do.

01:42:35.239 --> 01:42:36.889
You can answer that in one of two ways.

01:42:36.889 --> 01:42:41.920
Do you have a strong belief in why you're
alive?

01:42:41.920 --> 01:42:47.969
Tim Ferriss: I have a strong driver for taking
advantage of the fact that I am alive, but

01:42:47.969 --> 01:42:54.550
I don't have a story of a creator or something
along those lines.

01:42:54.550 --> 01:42:55.550
I don't.

01:42:55.550 --> 01:42:57.790
Arthur Brooks: Or a strong purpose because
it can be a creator or a purpose.

01:42:57.790 --> 01:43:02.739
Tim Ferriss: I do feel like I have a strong
purpose, but it doesn't relate to my birth.

01:43:02.739 --> 01:43:04.229
Arthur Brooks: That's okay.

01:43:04.229 --> 01:43:06.139
The why of your life.

01:43:06.139 --> 01:43:09.080
Our mutual friend Simon Sinek talks about
this, start with why.

01:43:09.080 --> 01:43:15.400
And the why of your life can be because of
a creation, or it can be because "I exist

01:43:15.400 --> 01:43:18.929
to lift other people up and bring them together
in bonds of happiness and love," which by

01:43:18.929 --> 01:43:21.199
the way is the why of my life.

01:43:21.199 --> 01:43:23.469
So that's number one.

01:43:23.469 --> 01:43:31.429
And if it's not, if it's there, but it's inchoate,
it's not quite clear enough, find out the

01:43:31.429 --> 01:43:34.880
answer to that and write it down and then
perfect it over a six-month period.

01:43:34.880 --> 01:43:35.880
Does that make sense.

01:43:35.880 --> 01:43:39.010
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I would say that for me,
I mean it's looking at and experiencing things

01:43:39.010 --> 01:43:41.159
in unorthodox ways so that I can teach.

01:43:41.159 --> 01:43:42.159
Yes.

01:43:42.159 --> 01:43:43.159
Arthur Brooks: And why do you want to teach?

01:43:43.159 --> 01:43:45.190
Tim Ferriss: Mostly to alleviate suffering,
I would say.

01:43:45.190 --> 01:43:47.719
Arthur Brooks: In other words, you want to
lighten the load for other people.

01:43:47.719 --> 01:43:48.719
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:43:48.719 --> 01:43:52.070
Arthur Brooks: And so in other words, you
want to serve your sisters and brothers.

01:43:52.070 --> 01:43:53.070
Is that fair?

01:43:53.070 --> 01:43:54.070
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, sure.

01:43:54.070 --> 01:43:55.070
Arthur Brooks: Okay.

01:43:55.070 --> 01:43:56.070
That's a great why.

01:43:56.070 --> 01:43:57.070
That's a great why.

01:43:57.070 --> 01:43:58.070
That's a great answer to the first question:
"Why are you alive?"

01:43:58.070 --> 01:43:59.070
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I don't feel conflicted
about that.

01:43:59.070 --> 01:44:00.199
Arthur Brooks: Okay, so the second one's harder.

01:44:00.199 --> 01:44:03.730
For what would you be willing to die today?

01:44:03.730 --> 01:44:11.620
Tim Ferriss: I don't have a ready answer.

01:44:11.620 --> 01:44:13.449
Arthur Brooks: It's a hard one.

01:44:13.449 --> 01:44:14.449
It's a hard one.

01:44:14.449 --> 01:44:15.449
A lot of people don't.

01:44:15.449 --> 01:44:16.449
Tim Ferriss: What's your answer?

01:44:16.449 --> 01:44:18.800
Arthur Brooks: For my faith, for my family,
for my country, and for you.

01:44:18.800 --> 01:44:22.150
I am willing to die for others.

01:44:22.150 --> 01:44:23.150
That's the answer.

01:44:23.150 --> 01:44:26.139
I mean, I probably won't be called to it,
but I'm willing to do it.

01:44:26.139 --> 01:44:27.139
I'm willing to do it.

01:44:27.139 --> 01:44:29.219
I've come to the conclusion that I'm actually
willing to do it.

01:44:29.219 --> 01:44:31.460
And here's how I learned this from my son.

01:44:31.460 --> 01:44:33.320
I learned this actually from my son.

01:44:33.320 --> 01:44:37.449
I mean, I have theoretical answers that are
politically correct in the Catholic sphere.

01:44:37.449 --> 01:44:39.010
I would die for the Catholic Church.

01:44:39.010 --> 01:44:40.300
I would die for my faith.

01:44:40.300 --> 01:44:41.620
These things are true, by the way.

01:44:41.620 --> 01:44:42.850
I really would.

01:44:42.850 --> 01:44:44.660
But it's too pat.

01:44:44.660 --> 01:44:45.750
Here's how I learned this from my son.

01:44:45.750 --> 01:44:49.440
My son was, I have three kids, 25, 23, and
20.

01:44:49.440 --> 01:44:58.740
And my middle son is named Carlos and Carlos
is, he's a kinetic boy.

01:44:58.740 --> 01:44:59.909
He's a fan of yours.

01:44:59.909 --> 01:45:02.699
He's probably watching us right now going,
"Oh.

01:45:02.699 --> 01:45:03.699
My.

01:45:03.699 --> 01:45:04.830
Oh, Dad's going to talk about me right now."

01:45:04.830 --> 01:45:05.830
Sorry.

01:45:05.830 --> 01:45:06.830
Tim Ferriss: Hey, Carlos.

01:45:06.830 --> 01:45:07.830
Arthur Brooks: Sorry, Carlos.

01:45:07.830 --> 01:45:13.389
And Carlos was having a good old time in high
school, and we had substantial grade problems

01:45:13.389 --> 01:45:14.469
and academic issues.

01:45:14.469 --> 01:45:19.570
And my wife's like, "At least we know he's
not cheating."

01:45:19.570 --> 01:45:25.139
But the problem was he wasn't really having
fun, and I think it was a meaning problem.

01:45:25.139 --> 01:45:31.119
And in search of the answers to the questions
after high school, he really became an entrepreneur

01:45:31.119 --> 01:45:32.119
with his life.

01:45:32.119 --> 01:45:35.600
And I asked my kids to do a business plan
when they're in high school because they're

01:45:35.600 --> 01:45:37.599
entrepreneurs and I'm VC.

01:45:37.599 --> 01:45:38.599
I'm an investor.

01:45:38.599 --> 01:45:39.599
I deserve a business plan.

01:45:39.599 --> 01:45:42.780
And when they weren't original, I'd send them
back for revisions.

01:45:42.780 --> 01:45:45.639
,
Tim Ferriss: Bank of Dad?

01:45:45.639 --> 01:45:50.219
Arthur Brooks: And it's really fun to be my
son.

01:45:50.219 --> 01:45:51.420
You can imagine.

01:45:51.420 --> 01:45:56.489
So Carlos's business plan for his life was
by the time it went through several rounds

01:45:56.489 --> 01:45:59.320
of revisions, was appropriately unorthodox.

01:45:59.320 --> 01:46:04.719
He was going to go work on a farm by himself
and find the answers to the questions, work

01:46:04.719 --> 01:46:05.719
hard.

01:46:05.719 --> 01:46:12.409
And so he actually got a job on a wheat farm
in Idaho, a real job, not some sort of hobby

01:46:12.409 --> 01:46:13.409
farm.

01:46:13.409 --> 01:46:14.409
No, no, no, no.

01:46:14.409 --> 01:46:16.070
It was an 8,000-acre working wheat farm.

01:46:16.070 --> 01:46:18.429
He lived in the farmer's basement for the
first year.

01:46:18.429 --> 01:46:20.070
He picked rocks out of the soil.

01:46:20.070 --> 01:46:26.540
He started in the bottom, made minimum wage,
fixed fences, cut down dead trees, ran a combine

01:46:26.540 --> 01:46:27.980
by himself 16 hours a day.

01:46:27.980 --> 01:46:29.110
Tim Ferriss: Why did he choose this?

01:46:29.110 --> 01:46:30.110
How would he explain that?

01:46:30.110 --> 01:46:33.219
Arthur Brooks: Because he needed to see what
he could do.

01:46:33.219 --> 01:46:37.281
He needed to find out what it meant to be
Carlos Brooks, away from his family, away

01:46:37.281 --> 01:46:38.570
from everybody.

01:46:38.570 --> 01:46:39.619
Why?

01:46:39.619 --> 01:46:41.219
Because he was looking for the answers to
the questions.

01:46:41.219 --> 01:46:42.219
They were inchoate.

01:46:42.219 --> 01:46:43.889
They were like, "Why am I alive?

01:46:43.889 --> 01:46:44.889
I don't know.

01:46:44.889 --> 01:46:46.349
Maybe I'll find it in the cab of a combine.

01:46:46.349 --> 01:46:48.159
Maybe I'll find it when I dig rocks out of
the soil.

01:46:48.159 --> 01:46:50.239
Maybe I'll find it by doing something hard
with my hands."

01:46:50.239 --> 01:46:51.710
Then he joined the military.

01:46:51.710 --> 01:46:53.909
He was 19 years old.

01:46:53.909 --> 01:46:59.010
He joined the Marine Corps and bootcamp is
no walk in the park for the US Marine Corps

01:46:59.010 --> 01:47:00.260
as we've all heard.

01:47:00.260 --> 01:47:01.260
But then it got harder from there.

01:47:01.260 --> 01:47:04.920
He did Infantry Training Battalion, and then
the End Dock for the Scout Sniper Platoon,

01:47:04.920 --> 01:47:08.780
which is a branch of the Special Forces and
the Marines.

01:47:08.780 --> 01:47:14.020
Today, he's Corporal Carlos Brooks, Marines
Three-Five Scout Sniper Platoon.

01:47:14.020 --> 01:47:16.389
And he's got answers.

01:47:16.389 --> 01:47:18.219
Now that's a scary job for me and his mom.

01:47:18.219 --> 01:47:19.520
He goes on field trips, right?

01:47:19.520 --> 01:47:20.830
Tim Ferriss: Field trips.

01:47:20.830 --> 01:47:23.260
Arthur Brooks: And thank God nothing's happened
to him.

01:47:23.260 --> 01:47:27.619
He's getting out of the military in December
of this year, but he's got answers.

01:47:27.619 --> 01:47:28.940
Tim Ferriss: What types of answers?

01:47:28.940 --> 01:47:31.250
I mean, I don't want you to —
Arthur Brooks: Here's his answers.

01:47:31.250 --> 01:47:32.250
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:47:32.250 --> 01:47:33.320
Arthur Brooks: I'll tell you his answers.

01:47:33.320 --> 01:47:34.320
"Carlos, why are you alive?"

01:47:34.320 --> 01:47:35.429
"Because God made me."

01:47:35.429 --> 01:47:37.920
"For what are you willing to die?"

01:47:37.920 --> 01:47:42.599
"For my faith and for my family and for my
friends, and for the United States of America."

01:47:42.599 --> 01:47:45.440
Boom.

01:47:45.440 --> 01:47:48.780
These are not the answers that a lot of people
watching us would give, but these are super

01:47:48.780 --> 01:47:49.780
solid answers.

01:47:49.780 --> 01:47:57.830
I'm super proud of my son because he earned
the answers to his meaning questions that

01:47:57.830 --> 01:48:00.340
everybody watching us has got to earn it.

01:48:00.340 --> 01:48:04.110
Everybody's watching us, everybody who's watching
us has got to go on a quest, a vision quest

01:48:04.110 --> 01:48:06.010
for the answers to the meaning questions.

01:48:06.010 --> 01:48:07.980
There's no other way to do it.

01:48:07.980 --> 01:48:09.590
Your dad can't tell you.

01:48:09.590 --> 01:48:11.440
Your priest can't tell you.

01:48:11.440 --> 01:48:14.180
The holy books can give you inklings.

01:48:14.180 --> 01:48:19.659
They can give you shadows on the cave wall,
to get back to the old Platonic metaphor.

01:48:19.659 --> 01:48:24.510
You need to live and to try things and to
go through a process of discernment.

01:48:24.510 --> 01:48:31.330
And the way to do that is to do hard things,
is to challenge yourself and to say to yourself,

01:48:31.330 --> 01:48:35.880
I will not stop until I have answers to these
questions, to my own satisfaction.

01:48:35.880 --> 01:48:44.599
Tim Ferriss: So hearing you describe Carlos's
experience, hi Carlos, and congratulations

01:48:44.599 --> 01:48:45.599
on the trajectory.

01:48:45.599 --> 01:48:46.599
Arthur Brooks: He's fine.

01:48:46.599 --> 01:48:49.139
He's like, "Yeah, my dad embarrassed me, but
Tim Feriss just said hi to me on his podcast,

01:48:49.139 --> 01:48:50.139
so it's all good."

01:48:50.139 --> 01:48:51.139
Tim Ferriss: It is not easy.

01:48:51.139 --> 01:48:54.489
I have some friends who are formerly Marine
force recon.

01:48:54.489 --> 01:48:56.070
That is not —
Arthur Brooks: The recon guys are —

01:48:56.070 --> 01:48:57.070
Tim Ferriss: That is not an easy path.

01:48:57.070 --> 01:48:58.070
Arthur Brooks: No, it isn't.

01:48:58.070 --> 01:48:59.070
Tim Ferriss: None of that is easy.

01:48:59.070 --> 01:49:00.070
Yeah.

01:49:00.070 --> 01:49:05.830
I think I was over processing the for what
would you be willing to die tomorrow?

01:49:05.830 --> 01:49:08.520
I think it was the tomorrow piece that I fixated
on.

01:49:08.520 --> 01:49:09.610
So family, close friends.

01:49:09.610 --> 01:49:11.260
Arthur Brooks: You would die for your family.

01:49:11.260 --> 01:49:12.260
You just would.

01:49:12.260 --> 01:49:13.260
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, family and close friends.

01:49:13.260 --> 01:49:14.330
I can give that answer.

01:49:14.330 --> 01:49:18.730
I was thinking of it more hypothetically as
a for what happening in the world would you

01:49:18.730 --> 01:49:20.710
be willing to basically off yourself tomorrow.

01:49:20.710 --> 01:49:23.400
Arthur Brooks: Is there an idea for which
you'd be willing to die?

01:49:23.400 --> 01:49:24.840
Is there a truth?

01:49:24.840 --> 01:49:27.230
Because this is really where it gets super
intense.

01:49:27.230 --> 01:49:28.230
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:49:28.230 --> 01:49:29.230
That's where it gets intense.

01:49:29.230 --> 01:49:30.230
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:49:30.230 --> 01:49:33.179
When Carlos says, "I am willing to die for
my faith, I am willing to die for the United

01:49:33.179 --> 01:49:37.820
States of America," which for him is an ideal
of liberty.

01:49:37.820 --> 01:49:40.330
By the way, for those of you outside of the
United States, he's willing to die for our

01:49:40.330 --> 01:49:43.300
allies too.

01:49:43.300 --> 01:49:45.750
Dying for an idea, that's super heavy.

01:49:45.750 --> 01:49:52.280
I mean, that's like pure grade meaning, because
people are going to say, "Are you kidding

01:49:52.280 --> 01:49:53.440
me?

01:49:53.440 --> 01:49:55.200
Are you nuts?"

01:49:55.200 --> 01:49:57.710
This is not, "I'm willing to kill for an idea."

01:49:57.710 --> 01:49:58.960
That's kindergarten stuff.

01:49:58.960 --> 01:50:00.820
That's kindergarten stuff.

01:50:00.820 --> 01:50:01.820
No.

01:50:01.820 --> 01:50:03.429
"I'm willing to die for something.

01:50:03.429 --> 01:50:04.880
I'm willing to give my own life.

01:50:04.880 --> 01:50:06.590
I'm willing to take yours.

01:50:06.590 --> 01:50:14.989
You and every other half-baked, dark triad,
malignant narcissist, cancel-culture trait

01:50:14.989 --> 01:50:15.989
psychopath."

01:50:15.989 --> 01:50:17.310
Tim Ferriss: I thought you were talking to
me for a second.

01:50:17.310 --> 01:50:19.690
I was like, "How did we get here all of a
sudden?

01:50:19.690 --> 01:50:21.820
Arthur Brooks got really abusive on my podcast."

01:50:21.820 --> 01:50:23.150
Arthur Brooks: No, no, no.

01:50:23.150 --> 01:50:24.150
No, no.

01:50:24.150 --> 01:50:25.150
Tim, I love you.

01:50:25.150 --> 01:50:26.150
Tim Ferriss: Is this one of your high negative
moments?

01:50:26.150 --> 01:50:28.270
Arthur Brooks: You're a beautiful man.

01:50:28.270 --> 01:50:29.270
But I mean, come on.

01:50:29.270 --> 01:50:33.679
I mean, everybody around the world is willing
to kill for what they think or cancel or hurt

01:50:33.679 --> 01:50:34.679
people what they think.

01:50:34.679 --> 01:50:38.631
But the real question is, are you willing
to sacrifice what you have for an idea?

01:50:38.631 --> 01:50:40.590
And that's really hard.

01:50:40.590 --> 01:50:41.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I'm not sure.

01:50:41.870 --> 01:50:43.139
That's a tough one for me to answer.

01:50:43.139 --> 01:50:45.849
I think also thinking about what you're willing
to die for.

01:50:45.849 --> 01:50:46.920
Let me personalize it.

01:50:46.920 --> 01:50:48.170
Thinking about what I'm willing to die for.

01:50:48.170 --> 01:50:55.670
I also want to be very aware if there are
things I would be willing to die for that

01:50:55.670 --> 01:51:01.639
could be manipulated to make me do things
that I might not currently be morally aligned

01:51:01.639 --> 01:51:02.639
with.

01:51:02.639 --> 01:51:03.639
Arthur Brooks: Totally.

01:51:03.639 --> 01:51:04.639
Totally.

01:51:04.639 --> 01:51:05.639
For sure.

01:51:05.639 --> 01:51:06.639
I get it.

01:51:06.639 --> 01:51:07.639
Tim Ferriss: I think the allegiance —
Arthur Brooks: I get it.

01:51:07.639 --> 01:51:10.680
Tim Ferriss: — it needs to be very, or for
me, I want to be aware if there are things.

01:51:10.680 --> 01:51:13.239
For instance, faith has been manipulated by
politicians.

01:51:13.239 --> 01:51:14.239
Arthur Brooks: So has patriotism.

01:51:14.239 --> 01:51:15.239
Tim Ferriss: Of course.

01:51:15.239 --> 01:51:16.239
Arthur Brooks: My goodness.

01:51:16.239 --> 01:51:18.090
I mean, a lot of people listening say, "You'd
die for the United States of America.

01:51:18.090 --> 01:51:19.090
Are you crazy?"

01:51:19.090 --> 01:51:21.429
Tim Ferriss: Which is not to say that it's
wrong, it's just very context-dependent on

01:51:21.429 --> 01:51:22.429
it.

01:51:22.429 --> 01:51:28.730
Arthur Brooks: Very context-dependent, and
it requires a lot of updating and serious

01:51:28.730 --> 01:51:34.320
thought, and it's not good enough to just
be sort of rah, rah, rah and taking it at

01:51:34.320 --> 01:51:35.320
face value.

01:51:35.320 --> 01:51:36.320
It takes serious discernment.

01:51:36.320 --> 01:51:41.040
Tim Ferriss: So we have the why were you born
or for what —

01:51:41.040 --> 01:51:42.710
Arthur Brooks: Why are you alive?

01:51:42.710 --> 01:51:44.090
Tim Ferriss: — are you alive.

01:51:44.090 --> 01:51:45.170
What would you be willing to die for?

01:51:45.170 --> 01:51:46.170
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:51:46.170 --> 01:51:47.409
Tim Ferriss: Got any more?

01:51:47.409 --> 01:51:49.090
Arthur Brooks: Those are the ones.

01:51:49.090 --> 01:51:54.159
I mean, what those do is that they really
kind of wrap up coherence, purpose, and significance

01:51:54.159 --> 01:51:56.400
into two kind of handy dandy questions.

01:51:56.400 --> 01:52:00.949
And the point is really this, I mean, it's
easy for me to frame that up is once you find

01:52:00.949 --> 01:52:02.040
those, you're all good to go.

01:52:02.040 --> 01:52:03.070
All right.

01:52:03.070 --> 01:52:05.580
But the truth is, you're going to go through
the rest of your life contemplating these

01:52:05.580 --> 01:52:08.630
things, and these are the questions to ask
on your birthday.

01:52:08.630 --> 01:52:11.320
"Are these still the things that I believe?

01:52:11.320 --> 01:52:12.489
Have I updated my knowledge?

01:52:12.489 --> 01:52:15.300
Do I have a better sense of who I am?

01:52:15.300 --> 01:52:16.480
Have I gone backwards a little bit?

01:52:16.480 --> 01:52:18.690
Have I lost this sense of what I'm willing
to die for?

01:52:18.690 --> 01:52:21.469
Do I need to go a little deeper at this point?"

01:52:21.469 --> 01:52:24.610
And touching up on those questions turns out
to be a really good, it's sort of the same

01:52:24.610 --> 01:52:27.610
thing when you go to the doctor and they do
the same test again and again and again and

01:52:27.610 --> 01:52:28.610
again.

01:52:28.610 --> 01:52:32.930
I have a series of tests that I do or these
questions that I ask myself like that about

01:52:32.930 --> 01:52:36.040
the reverse bucket list and the meaning questions.

01:52:36.040 --> 01:52:40.860
And am I pursuing my pleasures socially and
making memory with my prefrontal cortex?

01:52:40.860 --> 01:52:46.710
I also have, by the way, a spreadsheet of
19 micronutrients that feed into my macronutrients.

01:52:46.710 --> 01:52:51.860
And I grade myself on tenths of one percent
on a one to 10 scale weighted with respect

01:52:51.860 --> 01:52:55.320
to what my best estimate of my well-being.

01:52:55.320 --> 01:52:58.840
And when I'm going backwards on those things,
I set a strategic plan for my year.

01:52:58.840 --> 01:53:01.869
So I know I'm getting crazier by the minute,
right?

01:53:01.869 --> 01:53:02.869
Tim Ferriss: I'm into it.

01:53:02.869 --> 01:53:03.869
I encourage it.

01:53:03.869 --> 01:53:05.130
I want to pour gasoline on the fire.

01:53:05.130 --> 01:53:06.370
Arthur Brooks: Let's do it, man.

01:53:06.370 --> 01:53:09.110
Tim Ferriss: So we already talked about death
meditation.

01:53:09.110 --> 01:53:14.310
We talked about your experience in Mexico,
and I think in Build the Life You Want, obviously

01:53:14.310 --> 01:53:21.020
we're not going to go into all the micronutrients
of each of the legs on the stool per se, but

01:53:21.020 --> 01:53:24.960
I'm curious whether it's meaning or one of
the others.

01:53:24.960 --> 01:53:27.700
Maybe meaning, but it doesn't have to be.

01:53:27.700 --> 01:53:34.659
Could you give some examples of some of those,
I don't want to say antecedents, but micronutrients,

01:53:34.659 --> 01:53:39.949
the cast of characters and ingredients that
are important for sort of healthy functioning?

01:53:39.949 --> 01:53:41.300
Arthur Brooks: For sure.

01:53:41.300 --> 01:53:42.300
For sure.

01:53:42.300 --> 01:53:43.989
And you can break them up in as variegated
a way as you want.

01:53:43.989 --> 01:53:48.449
You can make 20,000 of them, but really there's
four that we should be thinking about.

01:53:48.449 --> 01:53:52.780
So there's four fundamental micronutrients,
and I make it more varied than that.

01:53:52.780 --> 01:53:56.469
I've got 19 because they're really about love
and relationships.

01:53:56.469 --> 01:53:57.909
That's really what it's all about.

01:53:57.909 --> 01:54:03.349
And the big four are your search for the divine
or your spiritual journey or your philosophical,

01:54:03.349 --> 01:54:05.050
your faith, whatever that happens to be.

01:54:05.050 --> 01:54:06.230
Religious or not.

01:54:06.230 --> 01:54:09.940
It's your love for something bigger than you
so you can stand in awe.

01:54:09.940 --> 01:54:10.940
It's your family relationships.

01:54:10.940 --> 01:54:14.869
These are the most mystical kinds of love
that we get because we didn't choose the loves.

01:54:14.869 --> 01:54:15.929
They were chosen for us.

01:54:15.929 --> 01:54:19.690
And sometimes we're like, yeah, I wouldn't
have chosen that.

01:54:19.690 --> 01:54:23.280
Friendship, and friendship when I'm talking
about that, I talk a lot about loneliness

01:54:23.280 --> 01:54:27.599
because especially strivers, hardworking people,
a lot of people watching us, they have a lot

01:54:27.599 --> 01:54:31.560
of people around them, but what they have
is deal friends, but not real friends.

01:54:31.560 --> 01:54:34.230
And your deal friends are super useful to
you.

01:54:34.230 --> 01:54:37.810
Your real friends are useless, cosmically,
beautifully useless.

01:54:37.810 --> 01:54:42.320
And so I go into a lot of detail with my students
about how to build useless friendships, not

01:54:42.320 --> 01:54:43.320
worthless.

01:54:43.320 --> 01:54:44.320
That's different.

01:54:44.320 --> 01:54:45.320
I've got those too.

01:54:45.320 --> 01:54:47.960
And last but not least, it's loving everybody's
expressed through your work, and that means

01:54:47.960 --> 01:54:49.510
serving others with your work.

01:54:49.510 --> 01:54:53.050
Your work should be a service profession no
matter what you're doing.

01:54:53.050 --> 01:54:54.841
And so faith, family, friends, and work.

01:54:54.841 --> 01:54:57.040
And then there's, we branch out from there.

01:54:57.040 --> 01:54:58.880
And I'm talking about family.

01:54:58.880 --> 01:55:02.420
I'm talking about different branches of family
that I'm trying to make sure I'm working on

01:55:02.420 --> 01:55:03.460
when I'm talking about.

01:55:03.460 --> 01:55:04.570
My marriage is critically important.

01:55:04.570 --> 01:55:08.440
It gets one of the absolute highest scores
in importance.

01:55:08.440 --> 01:55:13.440
Not always in terms of quality because I'm
not the best husband, but in terms of importance

01:55:13.440 --> 01:55:14.440
for sure.

01:55:14.440 --> 01:55:18.920
And that's because that's the apex of two
of those columns, both family and friendship.

01:55:18.920 --> 01:55:21.800
My best friend and also the adopted member
of my family is my wife.

01:55:21.800 --> 01:55:24.440
And so therefore, my marriage is really at
the top of those two pillars.

01:55:24.440 --> 01:55:25.659
And so I'm thinking about that a lot.

01:55:25.659 --> 01:55:30.239
So I break it up into subcategories, but they
all go into those silos of faith, family,

01:55:30.239 --> 01:55:31.360
friends, and work.

01:55:31.360 --> 01:55:34.840
Work that serves, work that's meaningful,
earn my success.

01:55:34.840 --> 01:55:37.560
And then I say, "Okay, well, what does it
mean to earn my success?

01:55:37.560 --> 01:55:38.560
What does it mean to serve others?

01:55:38.560 --> 01:55:39.560
How do I know?"

01:55:39.560 --> 01:55:40.560
Et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

01:55:40.560 --> 01:55:41.840
And I start getting down to brass tacks.

01:55:41.840 --> 01:55:43.079
I start putting numbers on it.

01:55:43.079 --> 01:55:47.120
And if the numbers aren't, if I'm going backwards
in a particular year — I do it.

01:55:47.120 --> 01:55:49.130
My birthday and my off birthday, by the way.

01:55:49.130 --> 01:55:50.150
Tim Ferriss: Your off birthday?

01:55:50.150 --> 01:55:51.150
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

01:55:51.150 --> 01:55:52.150
Yeah.

01:55:52.150 --> 01:55:53.150
My six-month birthday, November 21st.

01:55:53.150 --> 01:55:54.150
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

01:55:54.150 --> 01:55:56.900
Arthur Brooks: My birthday's May 21st, so
November 21st.

01:55:56.900 --> 01:55:58.830
Tim Ferriss: That's convenient that you got
Thanksgiving week.

01:55:58.830 --> 01:55:59.830
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, I've got Thanksgiving
week.

01:55:59.830 --> 01:56:04.560
And so it gives me a chance to actually think
about these things in some detail to be sure.

01:56:04.560 --> 01:56:09.179
And then I've got the data going back to about
the year 1999.

01:56:09.179 --> 01:56:10.290
I've got it going forward.

01:56:10.290 --> 01:56:12.239
And this system has gotten better along the
way and changed.

01:56:12.239 --> 01:56:15.360
Tim Ferriss: What are the most important changes
you've made to the system, would you say?

01:56:15.360 --> 01:56:18.560
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, the most important change,
the single most important change I made to

01:56:18.560 --> 01:56:24.230
the system was the recognition that I knew
a lot about happiness, but wasn't happy because

01:56:24.230 --> 01:56:27.060
I was studying it, but I wasn't doing it.

01:56:27.060 --> 01:56:31.690
The biggest change to the system was using
my knowledge to change my habits.

01:56:31.690 --> 01:56:34.100
That was number one, far and away.

01:56:34.100 --> 01:56:37.889
It's one thing I was giving people all this
advice about friendships and about having

01:56:37.889 --> 01:56:39.530
a good marriage and all that.

01:56:39.530 --> 01:56:42.830
I was looking at my life and I wasn't living
these things.

01:56:42.830 --> 01:56:47.579
I was just like everybody else, waking up,
going, sure hope I feel happy today.

01:56:47.579 --> 01:56:48.579
It was pathetic.

01:56:48.579 --> 01:56:49.579
It was pathetic.

01:56:49.579 --> 01:56:54.550
It was like a drug and alcohol counselor getting
up and taking a bunch of bong hits and having

01:56:54.550 --> 01:56:55.989
a six-pack.

01:56:55.989 --> 01:56:56.989
That's what it was.

01:56:56.989 --> 01:56:58.489
It was craziness.

01:56:58.489 --> 01:57:00.780
And by the way, it was my wife who finally
clued me.

01:57:00.780 --> 01:57:02.540
She's like, "You have a PhD, right?

01:57:02.540 --> 01:57:04.159
What are you using it for?"

01:57:04.159 --> 01:57:05.159
Exactly.

01:57:05.159 --> 01:57:07.000
Tim Ferriss: And you're like, "Your English
has come a long way."

01:57:07.000 --> 01:57:08.310
Arthur Brooks: "You're killing me, sweetheart.

01:57:08.310 --> 01:57:09.310
You're killing me."

01:57:09.310 --> 01:57:13.179
But I'm writing academic journal articles
that 14 people read so I can get promotion

01:57:13.179 --> 01:57:14.179
and tenure.

01:57:14.179 --> 01:57:15.179
It was ridiculous.

01:57:15.179 --> 01:57:20.409
Tim Ferriss: So how did you, I imagine even
if you're not necessarily walking the walk

01:57:20.409 --> 01:57:24.480
as much as you would you like, you believe
in what you are sharing.

01:57:24.480 --> 01:57:26.949
So it wasn't necessarily a conviction issue.

01:57:26.949 --> 01:57:29.380
So how do you then translate it to action?

01:57:29.380 --> 01:57:30.380
Are you just like, "You know what?

01:57:30.380 --> 01:57:31.989
If it's not in the calendar, it's not real.

01:57:31.989 --> 01:57:37.349
Let me commit to things that I block out so
that they are unavoidable in a sense."

01:57:37.349 --> 01:57:38.349
How did you convert it?

01:57:38.349 --> 01:57:42.429
Arthur Brooks: Well, I started by doing habits
and practices that were very, very specific.

01:57:42.429 --> 01:57:48.880
I mean extremely specific, as specific as
the stuff in The 4-Hour Body specific.

01:57:48.880 --> 01:57:50.260
And I was writing stuff down.

01:57:50.260 --> 01:57:52.920
I held whole notebooks of protocols and things
that I was trying.

01:57:52.920 --> 01:57:54.969
I was trying things on myself based on science.

01:57:54.969 --> 01:57:56.679
And when it worked, I would keep it and I
would write it down.

01:57:56.679 --> 01:57:57.679
I would practice it.

01:57:57.679 --> 01:58:02.060
Turns out that wasn't good enough because
it's very easy to have, because that was too

01:58:02.060 --> 01:58:03.060
hacky.

01:58:03.060 --> 01:58:04.360
That wasn't really habits.

01:58:04.360 --> 01:58:08.880
To ingrain a habit, you've got to do one more
step, which is that you've got to teach it.

01:58:08.880 --> 01:58:13.250
The reason I'm a happiness professor is because
I want to lash myself to the mast, and I want

01:58:13.250 --> 01:58:14.250
to be completely committed.

01:58:14.250 --> 01:58:19.000
Because look, I mean, if I'm doing something
that's clearly at odds with what I'm teaching,

01:58:19.000 --> 01:58:20.920
I'm going to hear about it.

01:58:20.920 --> 01:58:22.110
I'm going to hear about it from my students.

01:58:22.110 --> 01:58:23.230
I'm going to hear about it from my family.

01:58:23.230 --> 01:58:24.500
I'm going to hear about it.

01:58:24.500 --> 01:58:29.530
Good lord, I'm going to hear about it on social
media, not that I'm paying that much attention

01:58:29.530 --> 01:58:32.750
to that because I want to be happy and it's
really, really important.

01:58:32.750 --> 01:58:37.210
So basically, there's a protocol in my life,
which is number one, understand, number two,

01:58:37.210 --> 01:58:39.220
practice, number three, share.

01:58:39.220 --> 01:58:42.230
And that's the protocol that works for everybody
when it comes to happiness.

01:58:42.230 --> 01:58:43.469
You've got to take those three steps.

01:58:43.469 --> 01:58:45.910
You've got to do the work and understand.

01:58:45.910 --> 01:58:49.110
It's funny because I've done all this work
over the years with the Dalai Lama, and he

01:58:49.110 --> 01:58:50.449
always says the same thing.

01:58:50.449 --> 01:58:57.480
"If you want to be a spiritual adept, think
more, feel less, think more, feel less."

01:58:57.480 --> 01:58:58.480
Tim Ferriss: I would not expect.

01:58:58.480 --> 01:58:59.680
Arthur Brooks: No.

01:58:59.680 --> 01:59:00.680
Right?

01:59:00.680 --> 01:59:01.820
Tim Ferriss: That to be the framing.

01:59:01.820 --> 01:59:02.820
Okay.

01:59:02.820 --> 01:59:03.820
What does he mean by that?

01:59:03.820 --> 01:59:04.930
Arthur Brooks: Well, he first wakes up in
the morning, in the first two hours of his

01:59:04.930 --> 01:59:06.551
meditation, he gets up at three or 3:30 in
the morning.

01:59:06.551 --> 01:59:10.170
The first thing that he does after a little
bit on his exercise bike and hanging out with

01:59:10.170 --> 01:59:14.810
his cat, the first thing that he does is two
hours of analytical meditation, which Catholics

01:59:14.810 --> 01:59:16.330
call mental prayer.

01:59:16.330 --> 01:59:21.540
That means you take a couple of lines of sacred
scripture and you analyze it and you think

01:59:21.540 --> 01:59:22.540
about it.

01:59:22.540 --> 01:59:26.530
Most learning doesn't happen when the professor
talks about something.

01:59:26.530 --> 01:59:29.690
If you understand everything the professor
says, it's not a hard enough class, and you

01:59:29.690 --> 01:59:30.830
don't have a very good professor.

01:59:30.830 --> 01:59:34.320
He has to blow your mind with something, and
you've got to go away and think about it.

01:59:34.320 --> 01:59:37.030
And then you learn it through your own thinking.

01:59:37.030 --> 01:59:39.010
That's analytical meditation, or mental prayer.

01:59:39.010 --> 01:59:42.120
Tim Ferriss: And is the Dalai Lama using scripture
in this sense, or what?

01:59:42.120 --> 01:59:43.420
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, Tibetan Buddhist scripture.

01:59:43.420 --> 01:59:48.630
So he's contemplating something, words of
the Buddha or Śāntideva or any of the ancients.

01:59:48.630 --> 01:59:50.530
Tim Ferriss: Tactical questions.

01:59:50.530 --> 01:59:55.770
Is he choosing it or is it flip open the book,
which would not denigrate it.

01:59:55.770 --> 01:59:56.770
I'm just wondering?

01:59:56.770 --> 01:59:57.770
Arthur Brooks: Drop the needle.

01:59:57.770 --> 01:59:59.510
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I mean —
Arthur Brooks: No, it's extraordinary.

01:59:59.510 --> 02:00:01.750
I mean, the holy scriptures for Tibetan Buddhists
are vast.

02:00:01.750 --> 02:00:06.280
There's a library in Dharamshala that's just
stack after stack, after stack after stack.

02:00:06.280 --> 02:00:07.280
It's not a Bible.

02:00:07.280 --> 02:00:10.860
It's not like a collection of books in a little
library called the Bible.

02:00:10.860 --> 02:00:11.860
It's enormous.

02:00:11.860 --> 02:00:14.710
And the monks are going through the scriptures
again and again and again.

02:00:14.710 --> 02:00:19.050
So I'm sure he hasn't explained it to me,
but I'm sure that he has actually a regular

02:00:19.050 --> 02:00:21.599
rotation that he's going through to do his.

02:00:21.599 --> 02:00:22.599
And that's what we need to do.

02:00:22.599 --> 02:00:25.961
Tim Ferriss: He's got the equivalent of these
10 exercises when you go to the gym, skip

02:00:25.961 --> 02:00:27.969
the bench press, like he's got his —
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:00:27.969 --> 02:00:28.969
Kind of.

02:00:28.969 --> 02:00:29.969
Tim Ferriss: — his greatest hits.

02:00:29.969 --> 02:00:30.969
Arthur Brooks: No doubt.

02:00:30.969 --> 02:00:31.969
Yeah, no doubt.

02:00:31.969 --> 02:00:34.940
And that's really important to do in the science
of happiness, is to look at what the best

02:00:34.940 --> 02:00:40.639
protocols, what does it mean to stand up to
your negativity bias by actually practicing

02:00:40.639 --> 02:00:43.429
gratitude when what you're feeling is resentment.

02:00:43.429 --> 02:00:44.429
How do you do that?

02:00:44.429 --> 02:00:49.960
How do you actually achieve a state of metacognition
awareness of your own feelings such that you

02:00:49.960 --> 02:00:52.570
can choose reactions in the face of emotions?

02:00:52.570 --> 02:00:56.920
How can you get into the state of the I self,
which is the state in which you're observing

02:00:56.920 --> 02:00:58.909
the world as opposed to observing yourself?

02:00:58.909 --> 02:01:03.659
Well, it starts with knowledge of what these
concepts are, and then you put it into practices,

02:01:03.659 --> 02:01:04.659
real exercises.

02:01:04.659 --> 02:01:06.719
I have a column in The Atlantic that comes
out every Thursday morning.

02:01:06.719 --> 02:01:08.230
Tim Ferriss: That's next on my list of questions.

02:01:08.230 --> 02:01:13.590
Arthur Brooks: The third part of every column
is do these three things, is taking the science

02:01:13.590 --> 02:01:15.590
and then applying to your life in these three
ways.

02:01:15.590 --> 02:01:19.961
And so it's application and change of habits
and a commitment to that and the best way

02:01:19.961 --> 02:01:22.610
to cement in those ideas and by lashing yourself
to the mast.

02:01:22.610 --> 02:01:24.860
And that's by teaching these things to everybody
else.

02:01:24.860 --> 02:01:28.920
And everybody, look, I have a lab at Harvard
called the Leadership and Happiness Lab.

02:01:28.920 --> 02:01:32.389
The whole point of the lab is not bench science
and pouring stuff into test tubes or doing

02:01:32.389 --> 02:01:33.849
new experiments.

02:01:33.849 --> 02:01:36.820
It's learning how we can all be happiness
teachers.

02:01:36.820 --> 02:01:39.060
How can Tim Feriss be a happiness teacher?

02:01:39.060 --> 02:01:40.060
You already are, by the way.

02:01:40.060 --> 02:01:41.060
Tim Ferriss: Working on it.

02:01:41.060 --> 02:01:42.060
I'm working on it.

02:01:42.060 --> 02:01:43.060
Arthur Brooks: Well, you're working on it.

02:01:43.060 --> 02:01:44.060
And there's no reason that you have to be
happy to be a happiness teacher.

02:01:44.060 --> 02:01:45.060
It's not like playing basketball.

02:01:45.060 --> 02:01:46.060
Tim Ferriss: Thank God.

02:01:46.060 --> 02:01:47.060
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, totally.

02:01:47.060 --> 02:01:49.310
I mean, on the contrary, the people are struggling
the best at it.

02:01:49.310 --> 02:01:50.310
Tim Ferriss: I'm not as miserable as some.

02:01:50.310 --> 02:01:51.310
Arthur Brooks: You're fine.

02:01:51.310 --> 02:01:52.310
Tim Ferriss: Pretending to be.

02:01:52.310 --> 02:01:53.310
Arthur Brooks: I mean, the point is that you're
—

02:01:53.310 --> 02:01:54.570
Tim Ferriss: But I do have struggles.

02:01:54.570 --> 02:01:55.820
I mean, I do have challenges otherwise.

02:01:55.820 --> 02:01:56.820
Arthur Brooks: Course you do.

02:01:56.820 --> 02:01:58.010
So do I. You're self-aware.

02:01:58.010 --> 02:01:59.010
You're self-aware.

02:01:59.010 --> 02:02:02.780
And part of your commitment, because I know
your work, part of your commitment to lifting

02:02:02.780 --> 02:02:05.820
other people up is sharing your journey.

02:02:05.820 --> 02:02:06.820
This is what it comes down to.

02:02:06.820 --> 02:02:11.619
So you joke about, ah, but the truth of the
matter is we're all ugh.

02:02:11.619 --> 02:02:12.619
We all are.

02:02:12.619 --> 02:02:16.489
And sharing that is actually part of the way
that you're practicing these protocols.

02:02:16.489 --> 02:02:17.530
You're not hacking anything.

02:02:17.530 --> 02:02:18.840
You're actually trying to build these habits.

02:02:18.840 --> 02:02:23.530
And then the teaching role, you're a teacher,
by the way, because this is a teaching podcast.

02:02:23.530 --> 02:02:26.409
We're not shooting the breeze and saying,
"What'd you see on TV?

02:02:26.409 --> 02:02:27.760
And I went to a new restaurant."

02:02:27.760 --> 02:02:31.239
No, we're talking about heavy stuff because
we actually want to teach these ideas and

02:02:31.239 --> 02:02:32.239
lift other people up.

02:02:32.239 --> 02:02:35.849
So that's the secret, man.

02:02:35.849 --> 02:02:37.909
Learn more, think more.

02:02:37.909 --> 02:02:39.150
Don't feel.

02:02:39.150 --> 02:02:40.380
Learn, think.

02:02:40.380 --> 02:02:43.630
Second, turn this into habits and practice
this as habits.

02:02:43.630 --> 02:02:47.829
Third, share with others and commit to other
people through your teaching.

02:02:47.829 --> 02:02:49.970
That's the secret of happiness.

02:02:49.970 --> 02:02:51.250
Tim Ferriss: Commit.

02:02:51.250 --> 02:02:52.389
Commit in what sense?

02:02:52.389 --> 02:02:57.480
Just energetically taking the magnifying glass
off of yourself?

02:02:57.480 --> 02:03:00.810
Arthur Brooks: Committing to actually these
practices in your own life.

02:03:00.810 --> 02:03:05.119
And this can often be, "I've been doing this
thing wrong and I don't want to keep doing

02:03:05.119 --> 02:03:06.510
this thing wrong.

02:03:06.510 --> 02:03:09.410
And me telling you it's making yourself accountable
to another person."

02:03:09.410 --> 02:03:11.700
All 12-step programs work this way, right?

02:03:11.700 --> 02:03:16.659
Tim Ferriss: Honestly, 12-step programs, AA,
some of the most important and impressive

02:03:16.659 --> 02:03:23.120
decentralized organizations I've ever seen,
and I've never participated, but I am so impressed.

02:03:23.120 --> 02:03:26.790
Arthur Brooks: But they require accountability
because basically what 12-step programs do

02:03:26.790 --> 02:03:31.630
is knowledge of what's wrong with me, committing
to new habits, and sharing it with others.

02:03:31.630 --> 02:03:34.239
That's why AA works is because of those three
steps as well.

02:03:34.239 --> 02:03:35.369
Tim Ferriss: They do sneak some God in there.

02:03:35.369 --> 02:03:37.639
Arthur Brooks: Well, yeah, the higher power
for sure.

02:03:37.639 --> 02:03:38.639
The higher power.

02:03:38.639 --> 02:03:39.690
Tim Ferriss: Which is helpful.

02:03:39.690 --> 02:03:40.690
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:03:40.690 --> 02:03:41.690
Yeah, yeah.

02:03:41.690 --> 02:03:42.690
Tim Ferriss: I'm not going to deny it.

02:03:42.690 --> 02:03:46.389
One facet that I really appreciate of your
work and your writing is that you straddle

02:03:46.389 --> 02:03:52.099
the very old and the very new, and you mentioned
Aquinas earlier.

02:03:52.099 --> 02:03:59.000
Again, everybody read a few pages, at least
on Wikipedia, about Aquinas and then Aristotle.

02:03:59.000 --> 02:04:05.440
And one of your popular columns is about Aristotle's
10 secrets to happiness.

02:04:05.440 --> 02:04:08.590
I wonder if any, I do have them in front of
me.

02:04:08.590 --> 02:04:09.590
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, because I can't remember
them.

02:04:09.590 --> 02:04:11.052
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I was just going to say,
this is a lot to remember.

02:04:11.052 --> 02:04:12.179
Arthur Brooks: The problem of making a weekly
copy.

02:04:12.179 --> 02:04:14.030
Tim Ferriss: Well, how about this?

02:04:14.030 --> 02:04:20.480
How about I read through and then I would
love for you to pick out one or two perhaps

02:04:20.480 --> 02:04:24.659
that have been particularly impacted for you
or for your readers.

02:04:24.659 --> 02:04:25.659
Arthur Brooks: Right.

02:04:25.659 --> 02:04:26.659
Tim Ferriss: Here we go.

02:04:26.659 --> 02:04:27.659
Name your fears and face them.

02:04:27.659 --> 02:04:29.860
Two, know your appetites and control them.

02:04:29.860 --> 02:04:33.110
Three, be neither a cheapskate nor a spendthrift.

02:04:33.110 --> 02:04:35.530
Four, give as generously as you can.

02:04:35.530 --> 02:04:37.949
Five, focus more on the transcendent.

02:04:37.949 --> 02:04:39.550
Disregard the trivial.

02:04:39.550 --> 02:04:42.780
Six, true strength is a controlled temper.

02:04:42.780 --> 02:04:46.020
Seven, never lie, especially to yourself.

02:04:46.020 --> 02:04:48.710
Eight, stop struggling for your fair share.

02:04:48.710 --> 02:04:51.730
Nine, forgive others and forbear their weaknesses.

02:04:51.730 --> 02:04:53.820
10, define your morality.

02:04:53.820 --> 02:04:56.150
Live up to it, even in private.

02:04:56.150 --> 02:04:57.429
And I want to take one off the table.

02:04:57.429 --> 02:04:58.429
Arthur Brooks: Sure.

02:04:58.429 --> 02:05:02.010
Tim Ferriss: Which is the last one because
there's a recency bias here since we've been

02:05:02.010 --> 02:05:05.559
talking, I think fairly extensively about
that.

02:05:05.559 --> 02:05:06.699
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:05:06.699 --> 02:05:08.610
Those are, here we go.

02:05:08.610 --> 02:05:10.239
So let's start with number seven.

02:05:10.239 --> 02:05:11.550
Tim Ferriss: All right.

02:05:11.550 --> 02:05:16.309
Arthur Brooks: Never lie, be impeccable with
your word, especially with you.

02:05:16.309 --> 02:05:21.570
Now, I know a lot of people who are pretty
honest.

02:05:21.570 --> 02:05:28.719
Frankly, the reason my wife fell in love with
me is not my stunning good looks and my lousy

02:05:28.719 --> 02:05:29.719
accent.

02:05:29.719 --> 02:05:30.719
Tim Ferriss: And the great hair at the time.

02:05:30.719 --> 02:05:31.719
Arthur Brooks: Gray hair?

02:05:31.719 --> 02:05:32.719
Tim Ferriss: No, great hair.

02:05:32.719 --> 02:05:33.719
Arthur Brooks: Great hair.

02:05:33.719 --> 02:05:34.719
I know.

02:05:34.719 --> 02:05:36.820
I mean, it was spectacular if I do say so
myself.

02:05:36.820 --> 02:05:41.510
But the reason she told me later is because
I was the first man that never lied to her.

02:05:41.510 --> 02:05:42.989
I never lied.

02:05:42.989 --> 02:05:46.929
Now, that's part of my upbringing, and that
has to do with your family, et cetera.

02:05:46.929 --> 02:05:52.170
That was a really super important value in
my home.

02:05:52.170 --> 02:05:53.219
You had to be honest.

02:05:53.219 --> 02:06:00.849
The only really dramatic and scary consequences
with my parents were from lying.

02:06:00.849 --> 02:06:03.030
Don't lie, don't lie, don't lie.

02:06:03.030 --> 02:06:06.290
And so the result is, it was kind of in a
way easier for me because of the way that

02:06:06.290 --> 02:06:09.739
I was raised, but I was the first guy she'd
ever met that didn't lie.

02:06:09.739 --> 02:06:12.600
She's like, "What's up with this dude?

02:06:12.600 --> 02:06:13.600
He doesn't lie."

02:06:13.600 --> 02:06:18.420
And I've procured when my oldest son, both
of my sons 25 and 23, they're both married.

02:06:18.420 --> 02:06:21.230
And when my 25-year-old —
Tim Ferriss: 25 and 23, both married.

02:06:21.230 --> 02:06:22.260
Arthur Brooks: They're both married.

02:06:22.260 --> 02:06:23.260
Yeah.

02:06:23.260 --> 02:06:24.260
Tim Ferriss: Wow.

02:06:24.260 --> 02:06:25.260
I need to get relationship advice from your
son.

02:06:25.260 --> 02:06:27.719
Arthur Brooks: Well, my older son, he's a
piece of work.

02:06:27.719 --> 02:06:30.190
I mean, I learned a lot from him.

02:06:30.190 --> 02:06:34.780
He's literally one of the brightest native
intelligence people that I've ever met.

02:06:34.780 --> 02:06:40.290
But he also has this super adroit moral instinct
to him.

02:06:40.290 --> 02:06:48.020
And he asked me, he said, "Tell me one thing
that I should never do because I'm in love

02:06:48.020 --> 02:06:50.120
with this girl, that I should never do."

02:06:50.120 --> 02:06:51.809
He asks good questions like that.

02:06:51.809 --> 02:06:52.809
And I thought about it.

02:06:52.809 --> 02:06:53.809
I said, "It's actually pretty easy.

02:06:53.809 --> 02:06:55.560
Never lie to her ever, ever, ever, ever, ever.

02:06:55.560 --> 02:07:01.210
Until you go to the grave, never lie to her
and ask her never to lie to you.

02:07:01.210 --> 02:07:02.510
No matter what the consequences."

02:07:02.510 --> 02:07:09.780
Now, that's the secret to the pure oxygen
of a true companionate love, the mystical

02:07:09.780 --> 02:07:14.280
love of the romance where you feel truly chosen
for each other.

02:07:14.280 --> 02:07:15.650
It has to be based on honesty.

02:07:15.650 --> 02:07:16.650
It has to be.

02:07:16.650 --> 02:07:20.550
And a lot of people are like, yeah, yeah,
good luck with that.

02:07:20.550 --> 02:07:21.550
And there are lots of things.

02:07:21.550 --> 02:07:24.739
That doesn't mean you have to say, by the
way, "Sweetheart, your butt looks fat in that."

02:07:24.739 --> 02:07:26.270
Tim Ferriss: I was just going to ask.

02:07:26.270 --> 02:07:27.270
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:07:27.270 --> 02:07:28.270
No, no, no.

02:07:28.270 --> 02:07:30.500
It doesn't mean you have to volunteer every
single thing that you're thinking because

02:07:30.500 --> 02:07:32.780
you're not insane.

02:07:32.780 --> 02:07:35.820
But when there's a direct question, there's
a direct answer.

02:07:35.820 --> 02:07:36.860
What I comes down to.

02:07:36.860 --> 02:07:37.860
Okay, that's fine.

02:07:37.860 --> 02:07:40.869
But that's, you say in Spanish, "That's [foreign
language]."

02:07:40.869 --> 02:07:41.869
That's okay.

02:07:41.869 --> 02:07:42.869
Tim Ferriss: Table stakes.

02:07:42.869 --> 02:07:43.869
Arthur Brooks: Table stakes, man.

02:07:43.869 --> 02:07:45.510
You want to really be in the game, never lie
to yourself.

02:07:45.510 --> 02:07:49.360
And we're doing it all the time.

02:07:49.360 --> 02:07:50.360
We're doing it all the time.

02:07:50.360 --> 02:07:52.110
We don't want to face up to the truth.

02:07:52.110 --> 02:07:55.829
We don't want to look really in the mirror
and not so that we can think how great our

02:07:55.829 --> 02:08:01.420
hair looks or whatever, but so that we can
actually see the good and the bad.

02:08:01.420 --> 02:08:04.280
And to say, I'm a true human being.

02:08:04.280 --> 02:08:07.599
That's a hard thing to do when people lie
to themselves constantly.

02:08:07.599 --> 02:08:11.679
And Aristotle talked about that, and Aquinas
and the Buddha, and one of the things that

02:08:11.679 --> 02:08:16.690
they all have in common is that they have
this impeccable idea of self-honesty, which

02:08:16.690 --> 02:08:21.079
is that's taking a draft of the purest liquor
of life.

02:08:21.079 --> 02:08:23.070
It's like, yeah, I can drink that.

02:08:23.070 --> 02:08:24.480
I'm going to drink that.

02:08:24.480 --> 02:08:25.480
Really?

02:08:25.480 --> 02:08:26.510
You tough enough to drink that?

02:08:26.510 --> 02:08:29.400
And once you start doing that, I mean, it's
hard.

02:08:29.400 --> 02:08:31.179
It's really hard, but it's life changing.

02:08:31.179 --> 02:08:32.179
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:08:32.179 --> 02:08:34.989
So I have a question about that, and it also
makes me think about some of the scientists

02:08:34.989 --> 02:08:40.780
I respect most, like Richard Feynman who says,
"You must not fool yourself, and you're the

02:08:40.780 --> 02:08:42.219
easiest person to fool."

02:08:42.219 --> 02:08:43.219
Arthur Brooks: Well, for sure.

02:08:43.219 --> 02:08:45.550
And a lot of people say, because I'm religious,
that I'm fooling myself.

02:08:45.550 --> 02:08:46.550
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:08:46.550 --> 02:08:52.030
Well, this leads not to a religion-specific
question, but rather just to a fine slicing,

02:08:52.030 --> 02:08:56.699
which is sometimes, and I'm going off the
cuff, so this isn't going to be polished,

02:08:56.699 --> 02:09:01.880
but we lie to ourselves in the sense that
we have a story about ourselves or the world

02:09:01.880 --> 02:09:06.429
that we know isn't quite true, but we repeat
it enough because we want it to be true.

02:09:06.429 --> 02:09:11.610
Then there are times when we lie to ourselves,
but we are in the delusion.

02:09:11.610 --> 02:09:20.619
It is because we have a lack of awareness
or who knows, there are any number of factors

02:09:20.619 --> 02:09:26.929
that have fed into this belief, which is simply
not accurate, for whatever reason.

02:09:26.929 --> 02:09:30.710
So how do you catch yourself?

02:09:30.710 --> 02:09:36.250
Could you give examples, hypothetical or otherwise
of, because I think most people would say,

02:09:36.250 --> 02:09:38.460
"I don't want to lie to myself."

02:09:38.460 --> 02:09:47.770
But when it comes down to actually catching
those, catching those self-lies, that self-deception

02:09:47.770 --> 02:09:51.320
in the butterfly net, so you can do something
with them, how do you do that?

02:09:51.320 --> 02:09:55.849
Arthur Brooks: Well, you commit yourself to
being uncomfortable, for one thing.

02:09:55.849 --> 02:09:57.370
So there's a couple of different ways to do
that.

02:09:57.370 --> 02:10:00.180
Number one is you seek outside counsel.

02:10:00.180 --> 02:10:02.100
You ask people who really know you well.

02:10:02.100 --> 02:10:05.599
You ask them to be committed to telling you
the absolute truth, and then you ask them

02:10:05.599 --> 02:10:11.561
incredibly hard questions about yourself,
and you should have friends that can do that

02:10:11.561 --> 02:10:12.561
for you.

02:10:12.561 --> 02:10:15.219
This is one of the ways that you can do it,
but your friends really have to be able to

02:10:15.219 --> 02:10:16.219
tell you the truth.

02:10:16.219 --> 02:10:19.220
Most, being a good friend usually means telling
you convenient lies.

02:10:19.220 --> 02:10:23.040
That's not the right criterion for your closest
friends, your real friends.

02:10:23.040 --> 02:10:27.489
Your real friends, who should be like, "Buddy,
I don't think you're being straightforward

02:10:27.489 --> 02:10:28.489
with that right now."

02:10:28.489 --> 02:10:33.309
See, this is, I'm doing a fake Atlanta accent
because my best friend's a guy named Frank,

02:10:33.309 --> 02:10:35.880
and he lives in Atlanta, and we are committed
to telling each other the truth.

02:10:35.880 --> 02:10:39.780
And boy, does he ever give it to me, both
barrels, when he thinks I'm being full of

02:10:39.780 --> 02:10:40.780
it.

02:10:40.780 --> 02:10:41.780
"Buddy, I don't know.

02:10:41.780 --> 02:10:43.300
I don't know.

02:10:43.300 --> 02:10:47.260
I know you're saying that thing, but let's
drill down on that a little bit, shall we?"

02:10:47.260 --> 02:10:49.909
I talk to him every week.

02:10:49.909 --> 02:10:50.909
That's super important.

02:10:50.909 --> 02:10:51.909
Tim Ferriss: Every week.

02:10:51.909 --> 02:10:52.909
Arthur Brooks: Sure.

02:10:52.909 --> 02:10:53.909
"Can I talk to you?" because he's a real friend
and that takes work.

02:10:53.909 --> 02:10:54.909
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:10:54.909 --> 02:10:55.909
No, I understand.

02:10:55.909 --> 02:10:56.909
But is that like you have a standing?

02:10:56.909 --> 02:10:57.909
Arthur Brooks: No, it's not.

02:10:57.909 --> 02:10:58.909
No.

02:10:58.909 --> 02:11:02.190
But he's somebody where we're committed to
when the call comes in and says "Frank," I'd

02:11:02.190 --> 02:11:03.190
take the call.

02:11:03.190 --> 02:11:05.309
Even if it's during work time, I take the
call.

02:11:05.309 --> 02:11:09.719
But otherwise, you can't take every single
call that comes in.

02:11:09.719 --> 02:11:14.530
There are certain colleagues, certain family
members, and Frank.

02:11:14.530 --> 02:11:20.239
Tim Ferriss: And have you guys explicitly
made some type of mutual commitment?

02:11:20.239 --> 02:11:25.340
And the reason I ask is there are environments
also that I suppose I could join, but I haven't

02:11:25.340 --> 02:11:27.639
up to this point.

02:11:27.639 --> 02:11:28.910
For instance, YPO forums, these small groups.

02:11:28.910 --> 02:11:30.139
Arthur Brooks: Those are quite good.

02:11:30.139 --> 02:11:31.260
Tim Ferriss: They are very good.

02:11:31.260 --> 02:11:32.260
Arthur Brooks: Six, seven, eight guys.

02:11:32.260 --> 02:11:34.949
Tim Ferriss: And I know people who have benefited
from it tremendously.

02:11:34.949 --> 02:11:39.949
And there, as I understand it, based on my
friend's stories, there are many hot seat

02:11:39.949 --> 02:11:45.980
moments where you get your beliefs and stories
and approaches and strategies interrogated.

02:11:45.980 --> 02:11:51.030
Arthur Brooks: And you are committed in a
YPO forum to have your BS called out.

02:11:51.030 --> 02:11:52.030
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

02:11:52.030 --> 02:11:53.550
Arthur Brooks: So if you're saying something
they know is not true about you —

02:11:53.550 --> 02:11:54.909
Tim Ferriss: There's some great rules.

02:11:54.909 --> 02:11:59.550
People can study it also, if you wanted to
try to mimic or replicate it.

02:11:59.550 --> 02:12:04.070
I guess for a lot of folks, and I hate to
say it, but I think especially men, they may

02:12:04.070 --> 02:12:08.150
not have sort of a codified setting like that.

02:12:08.150 --> 02:12:12.929
Arthur Brooks: Especially successful men tend
to get lonelier and lonelier as they get older.

02:12:12.929 --> 02:12:16.530
Women tend to get less and less lonely, but
men tend to get lonelier and lonelier.

02:12:16.530 --> 02:12:20.119
So about 60 percent of 60-year-old men say
their best friend is their wife.

02:12:20.119 --> 02:12:22.580
About 30 percent of their wives say their
best friend is their husband.

02:12:22.580 --> 02:12:23.580
Tim Ferriss: No.

02:12:23.580 --> 02:12:27.750
Arthur Brooks: This is the story of, sad story
of unrequited friendship, unrequited companion

02:12:27.750 --> 02:12:30.200
love at home, for sure.

02:12:30.200 --> 02:12:33.199
But you know what that means is that a lot
of women have very, very close friends that

02:12:33.199 --> 02:12:36.820
they've cultivated, usually through family
life and community life, and guys, it's like,

02:12:36.820 --> 02:12:41.469
"Hey, man, I worked so hard over the years
and hanging out with my friends is stealing

02:12:41.469 --> 02:12:42.469
from my family.

02:12:42.469 --> 02:12:44.570
I'm not going to go golfing for five hours
on Saturday.

02:12:44.570 --> 02:12:46.770
When the kids are little, it'll be terrible
at home.

02:12:46.770 --> 02:12:49.650
I'll get yelled at and yada, yada, yada yada,"
all kinds of excuses, and pretty soon they're

02:12:49.650 --> 02:12:50.650
lonely.

02:12:50.650 --> 02:12:53.489
Tim Ferriss: And we might come back to this,
I mean, the loneliness is a big topic and

02:12:53.489 --> 02:12:57.530
the bonding and these types of communal relationships.

02:12:57.530 --> 02:13:06.320
But as far as cultivating a relationship with
the friends that you have, that unvarnished

02:13:06.320 --> 02:13:08.699
feedback and interrogation, is there a suggestion?

02:13:08.699 --> 02:13:11.430
Might you have any suggestion for how to approach
that?

02:13:11.430 --> 02:13:13.550
Arthur Brooks: Well, there's two ways to do
it.

02:13:13.550 --> 02:13:16.020
There's the organic way and there's the manual
way.

02:13:16.020 --> 02:13:20.270
The organic way is that you don't lose those
true friends that you've had, usually since

02:13:20.270 --> 02:13:22.060
you were a young adult.

02:13:22.060 --> 02:13:23.060
And a lot of people do.

02:13:23.060 --> 02:13:26.620
I mean, we move around a lot and we're really
ambitious and our friends wind up being our

02:13:26.620 --> 02:13:28.850
deal friends.

02:13:28.850 --> 02:13:32.829
And our real friends, people will say — I
mean, I'll do an exercise where I'll say,

02:13:32.829 --> 02:13:35.219
"Tell me the 10 people that are closest to
you.

02:13:35.219 --> 02:13:36.219
Write them down real quick."

02:13:36.219 --> 02:13:37.440
Tim Ferriss: I can do it easily.

02:13:37.440 --> 02:13:38.440
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, yeah.

02:13:38.440 --> 02:13:40.159
Tim Ferriss: I literally have it in a spreadsheet.

02:13:40.159 --> 02:13:41.210
Arthur Brooks: That's great.

02:13:41.210 --> 02:13:44.739
And then put real or deal after each one of
those names based on the fact that — and

02:13:44.739 --> 02:13:45.800
for you, it's all real, right?

02:13:45.800 --> 02:13:46.800
Because you have close friends, right?

02:13:46.800 --> 02:13:47.800
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I do.

02:13:47.800 --> 02:13:48.949
A high percentage from a long time ago.

02:13:48.949 --> 02:13:51.530
Arthur Brooks: And you travel with them and
you do stuff with them, right?

02:13:51.530 --> 02:13:55.199
Tim Ferriss: I put a lot of energy and time
into ensuring that we spend time together,

02:13:55.199 --> 02:13:58.570
which gets harder as people have wives and
kids and everything else.

02:13:58.570 --> 02:14:00.300
Arthur Brooks: And children, for sure.

02:14:00.300 --> 02:14:02.219
Family life tends to get in the way of that.

02:14:02.219 --> 02:14:03.830
But it's like anything else, you've got to
do the work.

02:14:03.830 --> 02:14:07.440
Tim Ferriss: I'm unfettered by my direct family
life.

02:14:07.440 --> 02:14:13.550
Arthur Brooks: But the data say that in 10
years you'll most likely be married and have

02:14:13.550 --> 02:14:14.890
kids and it's going to be harder.

02:14:14.890 --> 02:14:17.650
But you'll have to put in —
Tim Ferriss: Inshallah!

02:14:17.650 --> 02:14:20.100
Arthur Brooks: Folks, let's work on this.

02:14:20.100 --> 02:14:21.440
Shall we?

02:14:21.440 --> 02:14:23.829
Write in the comments your best —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

02:14:23.829 --> 02:14:24.829
All right.

02:14:24.829 --> 02:14:25.829
So there's the organic way.

02:14:25.829 --> 02:14:28.340
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, there's organic way,
which is make sure that you don't lose track

02:14:28.340 --> 02:14:33.219
of your real friends and you have a commitment
with your real friends to hold you to a standard

02:14:33.219 --> 02:14:37.420
of honesty in the friendship and with yourself,
because that's what true friendship really

02:14:37.420 --> 02:14:38.920
entails.

02:14:38.920 --> 02:14:43.570
The manual way is the YPO forum way where
there's a bunch of guys that you know that

02:14:43.570 --> 02:14:47.630
are usually deal friends and you make a deal
to make them into real friends.

02:14:47.630 --> 02:14:48.630
You make a deal.

02:14:48.630 --> 02:14:53.909
It's like an arrangement where part of the
friendship is actually going to be to go deeper,

02:14:53.909 --> 02:14:58.179
to hold each other's secrets, to be honest
with each other.

02:14:58.179 --> 02:15:02.390
And that can actually be incredibly effective
because you've actually decided to do that

02:15:02.390 --> 02:15:05.760
and people will owe up to those particular
promises.

02:15:05.760 --> 02:15:08.370
Psychological safety is really important in
that too, because it's one thing to say, "I

02:15:08.370 --> 02:15:10.900
want you to be completely honest with me,"
and say, "Yeah, you're a jerk."

02:15:10.900 --> 02:15:15.120
It's like not that honest, or don't express
it in that particular way.

02:15:15.120 --> 02:15:19.860
You have to have enough psychological safety
where the rules of the road are clear that

02:15:19.860 --> 02:15:25.460
honesty is always wielded as a gift and never
as a weapon.

02:15:25.460 --> 02:15:27.829
That's also true in marriages, by the way,
Tim.

02:15:27.829 --> 02:15:31.929
The best marriages are completely honest,
but the honesty is a gift and never a weapon.

02:15:31.929 --> 02:15:34.059
Tim Ferriss: Yep.

02:15:34.059 --> 02:15:39.980
One of the mantras at Facebook before it was
Meta was "Feedback is a gift.

02:15:39.980 --> 02:15:42.059
Feedback is a gift.

02:15:42.059 --> 02:15:43.059
Feedback is a gift."

02:15:43.059 --> 02:15:44.550
It was repeated over and over again.

02:15:44.550 --> 02:15:49.719
It was put all over the place to cultivate
a culture of feedback.

02:15:49.719 --> 02:15:54.679
And I've thought about that, just grabbing
these little snippets.

02:15:54.679 --> 02:15:56.150
"Make yourself small."

02:15:56.150 --> 02:16:02.559
I mean these very pithy reminders that will
have some stickiness in the mind and also

02:16:02.559 --> 02:16:08.980
doubling down on having your friends act as
the best mirrors available.

02:16:08.980 --> 02:16:13.139
Very brief side note, just to give people
a Scooby snack, mirrors, reflections.

02:16:13.139 --> 02:16:15.320
What are your thoughts?

02:16:15.320 --> 02:16:17.090
Arthur Brooks: Get rid of them.

02:16:17.090 --> 02:16:18.090
Tim Ferriss: Okay, tell me more.

02:16:18.090 --> 02:16:20.380
Arthur Brooks: So I work with a guy, he's
fantastic.

02:16:20.380 --> 02:16:21.380
He actually is.

02:16:21.380 --> 02:16:23.900
He's really, really helpful for me.

02:16:23.900 --> 02:16:27.710
I've recommended him to you because he does
incredible body work.

02:16:27.710 --> 02:16:33.620
Physical therapy is fantastic, and he was
a fitness model.

02:16:33.620 --> 02:16:38.030
There's almost nothing you can do that's unhealthier,
worse for your mental health, than being a

02:16:38.030 --> 02:16:39.240
fitness model or fitness influencer.

02:16:39.240 --> 02:16:40.240
Tim Ferriss: Why?

02:16:40.240 --> 02:16:42.920
Arthur Brooks: Because you're just looking
in the mirror all day long and your physical

02:16:42.920 --> 02:16:47.350
appearance is that on which you'll be judged
is very emotionally warping.

02:16:47.350 --> 02:16:49.050
And he hated it.

02:16:49.050 --> 02:16:50.050
He hated it.

02:16:50.050 --> 02:16:51.050
He was unhappy.

02:16:51.050 --> 02:16:53.300
He never ate what he wanted for 10 years.

02:16:53.300 --> 02:16:57.590
And the truth of the matter is, as we all
know, you look great if your body fat is under

02:16:57.590 --> 02:17:03.260
10 percent, but you feel crummy and you want
everybody in the world to die.

02:17:03.260 --> 02:17:05.380
It can be really, really hard on you.

02:17:05.380 --> 02:17:10.469
And he said, but after a while, there's a
reason that 35 percent of people who lose

02:17:10.469 --> 02:17:15.840
a lot of weight, when they get to their goal,
they keep going and a quarter of them develop

02:17:15.840 --> 02:17:17.260
an eating disorder.

02:17:17.260 --> 02:17:21.139
The reason is because you can't stop when
you're looking in the mirror all the time.

02:17:21.139 --> 02:17:22.139
You can't stop.

02:17:22.139 --> 02:17:23.139
You can't stop.

02:17:23.139 --> 02:17:24.380
You don't know how to stop.

02:17:24.380 --> 02:17:25.599
So he had the presence of mind.

02:17:25.599 --> 02:17:28.260
He's an adept, he's a spiritual adept.

02:17:28.260 --> 02:17:33.160
He got rid of all of his mirrors and showered
in the dark for a year.

02:17:33.160 --> 02:17:36.240
He got rid of all of the mirrors in his house.

02:17:36.240 --> 02:17:38.260
Tim Ferriss: The showering in the dark, I
need — maybe I —

02:17:38.260 --> 02:17:40.000
Arthur Brooks: Well, because you can't see
your abs.

02:17:40.000 --> 02:17:44.502
Tim Ferriss: I don't have that problem, but
yeah, I get it.

02:17:44.502 --> 02:17:47.780
You've got washboard abs and you're looking
at you going, "Yeah, man.

02:17:47.780 --> 02:17:48.780
Yeah, yeah."

02:17:48.780 --> 02:17:51.950
And bye-bye watching the water cascade off
your 12-pack like rocks in a waterfall.

02:17:51.950 --> 02:17:53.930
Arthur Brooks: I know, and the truth of the
matter is that —

02:17:53.930 --> 02:17:55.979
Tim Ferriss: I hate when that happens.

02:17:55.979 --> 02:17:58.920
Arthur Brooks: Extreme —
Tim Ferriss: But yes, I get it.

02:17:58.920 --> 02:18:02.590
Arthur Brooks: — physical attractiveness
ordinarily is something that you do because

02:18:02.590 --> 02:18:07.620
you want to become more lovable and you make
that judgment on the basis of what you're

02:18:07.620 --> 02:18:12.030
seeing in the mirror and not the relationship
that you're projecting to others in real life.

02:18:12.030 --> 02:18:13.179
It's really weird.

02:18:13.179 --> 02:18:17.889
You talk to dudes who are trying to get to
six percent body fat and get super jacked

02:18:17.889 --> 02:18:21.040
and the whole thing, and they have this — they
imagine that women are going to be just super

02:18:21.040 --> 02:18:23.340
attracted to them, and the only people who
even say anything are dudes.

02:18:23.340 --> 02:18:25.170
Tim Ferriss: Oh, it's always dudes.

02:18:25.170 --> 02:18:31.309
It's like I have friends who're single guys
and they have incredible beard game or mustache

02:18:31.309 --> 02:18:35.880
game and nonstop it's just dudes coming up
and complimenting them.

02:18:35.880 --> 02:18:39.090
I mean, I'm sure there are women who like
it too, but it's mostly guys.

02:18:39.090 --> 02:18:40.090
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, for sure.

02:18:40.090 --> 02:18:41.090
Tim Ferriss: It seems to be similar.

02:18:41.090 --> 02:18:43.780
Arthur Brooks: No woman has ever said, "Nice
car."

02:18:43.780 --> 02:18:47.680
I mean, it's just, no, it's other guys is
the whole thing.

02:18:47.680 --> 02:18:49.860
And what that is the other guys are just a
mirror.

02:18:49.860 --> 02:18:50.929
They're just a mirror.

02:18:50.929 --> 02:18:54.679
The other guys are saying the thing that you
think, but it's profoundly unsatisfying if

02:18:54.679 --> 02:18:58.929
you're actually in the heterosexual dating
market to have other guys saying, "God, how’d

02:18:58.929 --> 02:18:59.929
you get those abs?"

02:18:59.929 --> 02:19:00.929
Tim Ferriss: Who cares?

02:19:00.929 --> 02:19:04.439
Now to provide a counter example also, and
this is just something I've seen on dating

02:19:04.439 --> 02:19:11.679
apps that we could talk about, but I have
yet to meet a single guy I like and respect

02:19:11.679 --> 02:19:18.439
who's like, "I love super intense lip fillers
and all of this Frankenbotox situation."

02:19:18.439 --> 02:19:21.280
I have not met a single one yet.

02:19:21.280 --> 02:19:22.280
Arthur Brooks: I know.

02:19:22.280 --> 02:19:26.979
And have you ever said — I've never said
to my wife one time, "That's such a cute little

02:19:26.979 --> 02:19:27.979
dress.

02:19:27.979 --> 02:19:28.979
Is it new?"

02:19:28.979 --> 02:19:30.050
I've never said that.

02:19:30.050 --> 02:19:35.319
All her friends say that, and she's always
like, "I have a cute little dress, and everybody

02:19:35.319 --> 02:19:36.870
noticed it, but you didn't."

02:19:36.870 --> 02:19:40.080
Tim Ferriss: For the record, I'm into cute
little dresses.

02:19:40.080 --> 02:19:44.870
I don't want people to mix up voices here.

02:19:44.870 --> 02:19:48.630
Arthur Brooks: The show notes just went "BRRRRR!"

02:19:48.630 --> 02:19:52.729
Tim Ferriss: So why did you write this book
and how did it come to be?

02:19:52.729 --> 02:19:59.600
I know you have this long history with thinking
about these catalyzing events and tracking,

02:19:59.600 --> 02:20:03.790
and I don't want to say pursuing, but analyzing,
thinking deeply about happiness.

02:20:03.790 --> 02:20:08.430
So why now and what do you hope that it will
accomplish?

02:20:08.430 --> 02:20:12.870
Arthur Brooks: So this new book is the most
bread and butter book I've ever written about

02:20:12.870 --> 02:20:14.450
the science of happiness.

02:20:14.450 --> 02:20:15.490
It's two parts.

02:20:15.490 --> 02:20:19.899
Part one is how to manage your emotions so
they don't manage you.

02:20:19.899 --> 02:20:23.050
Part two is once you've got that down, now
you can actually build your life and not be

02:20:23.050 --> 02:20:25.890
distracted and frittering away your time with
stupid things.

02:20:25.890 --> 02:20:29.609
Those are the two parts of the book, and it's
as deep a dive as I've ever actually written

02:20:29.609 --> 02:20:31.189
publicly about neuroscience.

02:20:31.189 --> 02:20:34.670
It was actually vetted by my colleague at
Harvard, Josh Greene, who's one of the most

02:20:34.670 --> 02:20:37.561
distinguished neuroscientists in the world,
just to make sure, because I'm a social scientist,

02:20:37.561 --> 02:20:41.120
so I've got to be careful getting into the
biology side of this thing.

02:20:41.120 --> 02:20:46.300
I know enough to be dangerous to be sure,
but I have to be very careful about that.

02:20:46.300 --> 02:20:50.689
And by the way, this project was instigated
to do a bread and butter owner's manual on

02:20:50.689 --> 02:20:51.790
you and your happiness.

02:20:51.790 --> 02:20:52.790
That's what it is.

02:20:52.790 --> 02:20:55.399
It's an owner's manual on your happiness.

02:20:55.399 --> 02:20:58.010
It wasn't my idea, it was Oprah Winfrey's
idea.

02:20:58.010 --> 02:21:01.399
Tim Ferriss: So you occupy some rarefied air.

02:21:01.399 --> 02:21:05.290
So from the Dalai Lama, you just bump into
her on the subway?

02:21:05.290 --> 02:21:06.290
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, Her holiness Dalai Lama.

02:21:06.290 --> 02:21:07.920
I mean, His Holiness Dalai Lama and Her Holiness
Oprah Winfrey, right?

02:21:07.920 --> 02:21:08.920
Tim Ferriss: Yes, exactly.

02:21:08.920 --> 02:21:09.920
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:21:09.920 --> 02:21:10.920
I mean, when Oprah Winfrey calls, it's like
—

02:21:10.920 --> 02:21:11.920
Tim Ferriss: Right.

02:21:11.920 --> 02:21:14.830
But I'm guessing she didn't get your phone
number on Zoom info.

02:21:14.830 --> 02:21:15.920
How did this happen?

02:21:15.920 --> 02:21:18.500
Arthur Brooks: She calls, I'm like, "Yeah,
I'm Batman.

02:21:18.500 --> 02:21:19.500
No, really?

02:21:19.500 --> 02:21:20.500
Who is this?"

02:21:20.500 --> 02:21:21.500
No.

02:21:21.500 --> 02:21:22.500
Tim Ferriss: "Hi, it's Oprah calling again.

02:21:22.500 --> 02:21:24.990
Your company has been verified by Dunn and
Bradstreet."

02:21:24.990 --> 02:21:26.470
Oh, wait a minute.

02:21:26.470 --> 02:21:30.170
Anyway —
Arthur Brooks: So we got connected because

02:21:30.170 --> 02:21:34.149
she is a regular reader of my column in The
Atlantic, and she was during the coronavirus

02:21:34.149 --> 02:21:38.140
epidemic when people were trying to use the
time to learn new things, et cetera.

02:21:38.140 --> 02:21:41.160
And she was actually a serious reader of the
column.

02:21:41.160 --> 02:21:42.160
And she's a serious reader.

02:21:42.160 --> 02:21:43.160
Tim Ferriss: She's a serious reader.

02:21:43.160 --> 02:21:46.880
Arthur Brooks: When she has a book on her
podcast, she reads the book.

02:21:46.880 --> 02:21:50.740
It's just amazing how exhaustive her knowledge
is.

02:21:50.740 --> 02:21:55.170
And then when my book came out, From Strength
to Strength in February 2022, she got it literally

02:21:55.170 --> 02:21:58.340
in the first week, she read it, and that's
when she called and she said, "Would you come

02:21:58.340 --> 02:21:59.359
on my podcast?"

02:21:59.359 --> 02:22:02.450
Her Super Soul, which is this book podcast,
phenomenal.

02:22:02.450 --> 02:22:07.450
And we were like a house on fire from the
very beginning because she's — it's funny

02:22:07.450 --> 02:22:13.820
because you've met a lot of famous people
and I've met some famous people, and they're

02:22:13.820 --> 02:22:17.689
usually not exactly like they appear in public.

02:22:17.689 --> 02:22:18.729
She's like she appears in public.

02:22:18.729 --> 02:22:25.000
I mean, she's calm, she's smart, she's nice,
she's funny, she's awesome.

02:22:25.000 --> 02:22:27.939
She's actually what people think she is.

02:22:27.939 --> 02:22:29.180
She's truly an authentic person.

02:22:29.180 --> 02:22:32.601
And so we really got along and we have the
synchronicity of mission, which is to lift

02:22:32.601 --> 02:22:36.220
people up and bring them together in bonds
of happiness and love, but we have different

02:22:36.220 --> 02:22:37.939
platforms for doing it.

02:22:37.939 --> 02:22:40.939
I'm teaching this class on the science of
happiness at Harvard University and writing

02:22:40.939 --> 02:22:42.680
in The Atlantic.

02:22:42.680 --> 02:22:48.930
She has been in mass media forever, and whenever
she weighs in, she has millions and millions

02:22:48.930 --> 02:22:51.630
and millions of people around the world that
trust her and want to hear what she has to

02:22:51.630 --> 02:22:52.630
say.

02:22:52.630 --> 02:22:56.271
And so her suggestion was, "Let's take that
class that you teach at Harvard and that you're

02:22:56.271 --> 02:22:59.810
writing about in The Atlantic, let's present
that to a big audience.

02:22:59.810 --> 02:23:02.540
You want a big audience?"

02:23:02.540 --> 02:23:03.540
I said, "Yeah.

02:23:03.540 --> 02:23:04.540
Yes, yes, yes.

02:23:04.540 --> 02:23:06.080
Oprah, can I have a big audience, please?"

02:23:06.080 --> 02:23:10.460
And she says, "Let's write a book together
and we'll present this book together."

02:23:10.460 --> 02:23:15.050
And so we did, and we got together at her
home and we framed it up.

02:23:15.050 --> 02:23:20.990
We framed up the book over a three- or four-day
period last year in her teahouse in Montecito,

02:23:20.990 --> 02:23:21.990
California.

02:23:21.990 --> 02:23:26.802
And I was looking around going, I'm just like
this small town college professor who fell

02:23:26.802 --> 02:23:28.930
off the turnip truck in front of Oprah's teahouse.

02:23:28.930 --> 02:23:29.930
You never know.

02:23:29.930 --> 02:23:31.010
He's like, God bless America.

02:23:31.010 --> 02:23:32.670
You never know what's going to happen.

02:23:32.670 --> 02:23:33.850
And it was super fun.

02:23:33.850 --> 02:23:34.850
It was super interesting.

02:23:34.850 --> 02:23:35.920
Tim Ferriss: It's the best country for people
who want to work hard.

02:23:35.920 --> 02:23:36.920
Arthur Brooks: Oh, my goodness.

02:23:36.920 --> 02:23:37.920
Oh, my goodness.

02:23:37.920 --> 02:23:42.590
And she's the best case study in American
success and working hard and believing in

02:23:42.590 --> 02:23:45.010
others and paying it forward possible.

02:23:45.010 --> 02:23:48.930
And then we went and we started working on
our respective parts of the book and passing

02:23:48.930 --> 02:23:49.930
it back and forth.

02:23:49.930 --> 02:23:53.231
And I took a house in San Clemente in California
for six weeks in the winter largely to look

02:23:53.231 --> 02:23:56.450
at the Pacific Ocean to write the book, and
she was writing her parts.

02:23:56.450 --> 02:24:00.800
And then we got to this impasse at one point,
not between us, but because the title didn't

02:24:00.800 --> 02:24:02.170
fit.

02:24:02.170 --> 02:24:06.500
The title now is How to Build the Life You
Want: The Art and Science of Getting Happier.

02:24:06.500 --> 02:24:10.870
And it was called Fully Alive and it wasn't
fitting, and I didn't know.

02:24:10.870 --> 02:24:13.770
And finally Oprah calls me and she says, "Got
the wrong title."

02:24:13.770 --> 02:24:15.000
Tim Ferriss: She's a genius.

02:24:15.000 --> 02:24:17.080
Arthur Brooks: "We've got the wrong title.

02:24:17.080 --> 02:24:18.390
This is a how to book.

02:24:18.390 --> 02:24:20.750
This is an owner's manual book.

02:24:20.750 --> 02:24:23.580
This is really a how to book on your own happiness.

02:24:23.580 --> 02:24:25.840
This should be called How to Build a Life
You Want."

02:24:25.840 --> 02:24:27.399
And ching, the whole thing fell into place.

02:24:27.399 --> 02:24:31.040
Because that's what happens as you know, in
the course of writing a book, you think you

02:24:31.040 --> 02:24:32.040
have it, but you don't.

02:24:32.040 --> 02:24:35.680
And then when the title actually completes
the book and allows you to finish the book

02:24:35.680 --> 02:24:36.680
and make it all cohere.

02:24:36.680 --> 02:24:41.560
Then we finished it up and it was this amazing
collaborative experience, a joy, actually,

02:24:41.560 --> 02:24:42.850
it was the most fun.

02:24:42.850 --> 02:24:48.350
It was the most positive experience I've ever
had writing a book because I got to write

02:24:48.350 --> 02:24:50.470
it with her, and she's enriched my life.

02:24:50.470 --> 02:24:53.430
Tim Ferriss: What a wild, incredible experience.

02:24:53.430 --> 02:24:54.550
Arthur Brooks: It's nuts.

02:24:54.550 --> 02:24:55.680
It's nuts, man.

02:24:55.680 --> 02:25:01.520
I told you my death fear is losing my mind.

02:25:01.520 --> 02:25:04.390
It's actually possible that I am and that's
just a hallucination.

02:25:04.390 --> 02:25:09.310
Tim Ferriss: "So then we had tea, and we had
crumpets, and we played bridge."

02:25:09.310 --> 02:25:11.700
Arthur Brooks: Had tea in her teahouse and
all that.

02:25:11.700 --> 02:25:14.262
It's like, "But Tim, I know I want the pure
truth.

02:25:14.262 --> 02:25:15.770
But if that's not true, don't tell me."

02:25:15.770 --> 02:25:17.450
Tim Ferriss: "Let me keep that one.

02:25:17.450 --> 02:25:18.450
Let me keep one."

02:25:18.450 --> 02:25:21.080
Arthur Brooks: "Lie to me, baby."

02:25:21.080 --> 02:25:26.630
Tim Ferriss: So in this book, are there areas
— because I know in my book I can point

02:25:26.630 --> 02:25:30.510
to specific chapters where I'm like, "Man,
I really wish people had paid more attention

02:25:30.510 --> 02:25:31.510
to X."

02:25:31.510 --> 02:25:38.720
Maybe it didn't quite get the emphasis so
I can own that responsibility, but maybe they,

02:25:38.720 --> 02:25:43.760
for whatever reason, didn't pay enough attention
to this one component.

02:25:43.760 --> 02:25:44.760
Is there something that comes to mind?

02:25:44.760 --> 02:25:46.220
Arthur Brooks: I don't know yet 'cause it
hasn't come out.

02:25:46.220 --> 02:25:47.450
Tim Ferriss: Oh, I know, but I'm just saying
—

02:25:47.450 --> 02:25:48.450
Arthur Brooks: So what do I suspect?

02:25:48.450 --> 02:25:50.730
Tim Ferriss: What would hurt you the most
if people missed?

02:25:50.730 --> 02:25:58.510
Arthur Brooks: I think what would bother me
the most is the amount that Oprah and I emphasize

02:25:58.510 --> 02:26:01.359
the role of unhappiness in living a full life.

02:26:01.359 --> 02:26:04.690
See, one of the biggest mistakes that people
make as we talked about before, is that people

02:26:04.690 --> 02:26:07.000
say, "I want to be happy, but..."

02:26:07.000 --> 02:26:10.330
And then they talk about some source of unhappiness
in their life that they think blocks their

02:26:10.330 --> 02:26:11.460
happiness.

02:26:11.460 --> 02:26:15.220
And that's the wrong way of thinking because
you can get happier even if you're unhappy,

02:26:15.220 --> 02:26:18.560
absolutely 100 percent all day long, because
these are existing in different parts of your

02:26:18.560 --> 02:26:19.560
brain, number one.

02:26:19.560 --> 02:26:24.899
But number two, happiness is not the goal
and unhappiness is not the enemy.

02:26:24.899 --> 02:26:26.510
Getting happier is the goal.

02:26:26.510 --> 02:26:28.460
Oprah coined this term in the book.

02:26:28.460 --> 02:26:30.729
She said, "We've got to stop talking about
happiness because that's actually not the

02:26:30.729 --> 02:26:31.729
goal.

02:26:31.729 --> 02:26:33.580
The goal is happierness."

02:26:33.580 --> 02:26:35.490
That's really what we're going for is happierness.

02:26:35.490 --> 02:26:39.010
And to get happierness, you need unhappiness
in your life.

02:26:39.010 --> 02:26:43.240
Look, you need negative emotions to keep you
alive, but you also need the deferral of gratification

02:26:43.240 --> 02:26:44.380
to get your satisfaction.

02:26:44.380 --> 02:26:48.450
And you need to understand the nature of the
frustration that comes such that you can start

02:26:48.450 --> 02:26:49.870
to manage your wants.

02:26:49.870 --> 02:26:53.800
You need serious, full-on suffering to find
the answers to the questions of meaning that

02:26:53.800 --> 02:26:54.800
we talked about.

02:26:54.800 --> 02:26:55.800
You do.

02:26:55.800 --> 02:26:56.939
My son needed a bootcamp.

02:26:56.939 --> 02:27:01.899
You and I need substantial problems with mood,
to put it euphemistically.

02:27:01.899 --> 02:27:05.630
No, look, we need sadness.

02:27:05.630 --> 02:27:11.390
Jung, Freud's greatest student, Carl Jung,
so much greater in so many ways, said that

02:27:11.390 --> 02:27:14.750
you don't really understand happiness until
you've experienced unhappiness because of

02:27:14.750 --> 02:27:15.750
the contrast.

02:27:15.750 --> 02:27:20.530
But more to the point, you've not been fully
alive because unhappiness is what actually

02:27:20.530 --> 02:27:24.340
— the suffering is what helps you understand
what you're made of and what you can bear.

02:27:24.340 --> 02:27:27.170
And only then will you find the answers to
the "Why am I alive?"

02:27:27.170 --> 02:27:28.720
or "For what I'd be willing to die?"

02:27:28.720 --> 02:27:29.720
questions.

02:27:29.720 --> 02:27:35.800
You don't find the meaning questions, the
answers at that week at the beach in Ibiza.

02:27:35.800 --> 02:27:41.751
You find it in the depths when somebody you
love dies, when you're afraid of what your

02:27:41.751 --> 02:27:45.740
future holds, when you feel hopeless.

02:27:45.740 --> 02:27:49.970
That's when those moments become real, and
that suffering turns out to be an integral

02:27:49.970 --> 02:27:51.260
part in your journey to happiness.

02:27:51.260 --> 02:27:55.029
So the number one thing that Oprah and I will
be very disappointed about is that people

02:27:55.029 --> 02:28:01.029
don't actually become more fully alive through
the transcendent passage of both happierness

02:28:01.029 --> 02:28:05.180
and the unhappiness that is a part of what
it means to be a real person.

02:28:05.180 --> 02:28:11.260
Tim Ferriss: It is a diverse and ever-changing
tapestry, that is for sure.

02:28:11.260 --> 02:28:12.260
Arthur Brooks: It is.

02:28:12.260 --> 02:28:15.520
And I think about it, one of the biggest mistakes
that I think that my students make — by

02:28:15.520 --> 02:28:22.100
the way, right now, if I were to go back to
1968 or 1969 Woodstock and the hippies said,

02:28:22.100 --> 02:28:23.460
"If it feels good, do it."

02:28:23.460 --> 02:28:26.609
I remember my dad heard that for the first
time, and he's like, "That's the end of America."

02:28:26.609 --> 02:28:29.410
He was kind of right.

02:28:29.410 --> 02:28:36.500
Anyway, if we had a Woodstock today, it might
be, "If it feels bad, make it stop.

02:28:36.500 --> 02:28:39.490
If I'm suffering, treat it.

02:28:39.490 --> 02:28:44.300
If there's pain, it's evidence that I'm defective,
that I'm broken.

02:28:44.300 --> 02:28:45.300
Something's got to change."

02:28:45.300 --> 02:28:46.300
That's wrong.

02:28:46.300 --> 02:28:47.300
That's wrong thinking.

02:28:47.300 --> 02:28:51.050
Look, the Tim Ferriss I'm talking to right
now had to suffer.

02:28:51.050 --> 02:28:55.460
I mean, these messages that you're giving
are dramatically different than what you were

02:28:55.460 --> 02:28:56.950
writing 15 and 20 years ago.

02:28:56.950 --> 02:28:57.950
They're dramatically different.

02:28:57.950 --> 02:29:01.420
It doesn't mean it was wrong 15 and 20 years
ago, but it was incomplete.

02:29:01.420 --> 02:29:03.130
It wasn't deep in the same way.

02:29:03.130 --> 02:29:06.149
And the depth actually comes from the — not
just from the good.

02:29:06.149 --> 02:29:09.210
It also comes from the bad.

02:29:09.210 --> 02:29:11.479
Andrew Solomon, who wrote The Noonday Demon,
have you read that book?

02:29:11.479 --> 02:29:12.479
Tim Ferriss: No.

02:29:12.479 --> 02:29:17.130
Arthur Brooks: It's the best book I've ever
read about anxiety and depression.

02:29:17.130 --> 02:29:18.500
Andrew Solomon's a phenomenal writer.

02:29:18.500 --> 02:29:20.050
Tim Ferriss: All right, so what is the title
again?

02:29:20.050 --> 02:29:25.340
Arthur Brooks: The Noonday Demon, which was
an ancient way of talking about depression,

02:29:25.340 --> 02:29:26.840
which comes over you like a noonday demon.

02:29:26.840 --> 02:29:27.840
Tim Ferriss: It's like the black dog.

02:29:27.840 --> 02:29:30.430
Arthur Brooks: It's like Winston Churchill's
black dog for sure.

02:29:30.430 --> 02:29:32.090
The Noonday Demon, in the end, it's an incredible
book.

02:29:32.090 --> 02:29:35.880
It's a total page-turner for anybody who's
actually had anything close to —

02:29:35.880 --> 02:29:38.029
Tim Ferriss: At face value, does not sound
like a page-turner.

02:29:38.029 --> 02:29:39.140
Arthur Brooks: I know.

02:29:39.140 --> 02:29:40.140
It's phenomenal, though.

02:29:40.140 --> 02:29:41.140
It's so interesting.

02:29:41.140 --> 02:29:42.430
It's just beautiful writing.

02:29:42.430 --> 02:29:47.600
But in the end, he said, "In the sum and final
balance, I have to conclude that I love my

02:29:47.600 --> 02:29:52.770
depression because it's part of who I am as
a person, and it's allowed me to learn what

02:29:52.770 --> 02:29:54.760
my life is all about.

02:29:54.760 --> 02:29:56.399
I don't wish it on anybody.

02:29:56.399 --> 02:30:02.431
I don't want it to come back, but it is who
I am, and so therefore, I have no choice but

02:30:02.431 --> 02:30:03.580
to love it."

02:30:03.580 --> 02:30:08.609
I'm paraphrasing, but his words are more beautiful
than anything I could remember.

02:30:08.609 --> 02:30:10.550
But it's really an important thing for us
to remember.

02:30:10.550 --> 02:30:13.340
And anybody who's watching you and who follows
you and who admires you and gets a lot of

02:30:13.340 --> 02:30:17.399
sustenance from the knowledge that you bring
to this podcast, what they have to realize

02:30:17.399 --> 02:30:20.880
is that they are beneficiaries of Tim Ferriss's
suffering.

02:30:20.880 --> 02:30:26.069
And if they want to lift up the world, they
have to suffer too.

02:30:26.069 --> 02:30:31.640
Tim Ferriss: It's food for thought.

02:30:31.640 --> 02:30:32.640
Yep.

02:30:32.640 --> 02:30:33.640
Food for thought.

02:30:33.640 --> 02:30:38.910
May not be candy bars, might be high fiber,
but it is important food for thought.

02:30:38.910 --> 02:30:41.850
Arthur Brooks: It's a philosophical Clif bar.

02:30:41.850 --> 02:30:43.910
Sorry, people love those.

02:30:43.910 --> 02:30:46.010
Not to cast aspersions, my friends at Clif
—

02:30:46.010 --> 02:30:52.640
Tim Ferriss: But I have thought about this
very deliberately, and I don't wish suffering

02:30:52.640 --> 02:31:00.470
upon anyone, but I had someone give me very
good advice at one point, and the words can

02:31:00.470 --> 02:31:02.050
be substituted, of course.

02:31:02.050 --> 02:31:10.140
But she said to me after I'd gone through
— very gifted therapist with a lot of experience,

02:31:10.140 --> 02:31:18.080
a lot of mileage with different types of patients,
including some very tragic and difficult cases.

02:31:18.080 --> 02:31:22.689
And she said, "Take your pain, make it part
of your medicine."

02:31:22.689 --> 02:31:29.069
And I was like, "Okay," meaning the medicine
in my case, the way I think of that is what

02:31:29.069 --> 02:31:37.529
I can teach or provide or just the perspective
through which I can speak and explore, given

02:31:37.529 --> 02:31:39.780
that I have the history that I have and understand.

02:31:39.780 --> 02:31:42.580
Arthur Brooks: This is what the best therapists
do.

02:31:42.580 --> 02:31:44.710
They teach you about yourself.

02:31:44.710 --> 02:31:48.899
They help you to learn and grow from your
pain, and they help you to treat yourself

02:31:48.899 --> 02:31:50.529
and serve others.

02:31:50.529 --> 02:31:51.970
That's what the great therapists do.

02:31:51.970 --> 02:31:54.590
The worst therapist is like, "Yeah, I'll help
you take care of that.

02:31:54.590 --> 02:31:56.410
We'll make that problem go away."

02:31:56.410 --> 02:31:58.620
No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

02:31:58.620 --> 02:31:59.979
I want to learn.

02:31:59.979 --> 02:32:03.160
I want to grow and I want to surf.

02:32:03.160 --> 02:32:04.160
Yeah.

02:32:04.160 --> 02:32:12.420
Surf part has been very critical to the tether
of meaning that has given the suffering meaning.

02:32:12.420 --> 02:32:13.950
Tim Ferriss: Exactly.

02:32:13.950 --> 02:32:20.780
Tim Ferriss: Meaning made it — I don't say
irrelevant, but the greater the potency of

02:32:20.780 --> 02:32:26.149
the meaning, the less the suffering incapacitates
you.

02:32:26.149 --> 02:32:31.140
Let me ask you, is it possible you're not
afraid to suffer more now?

02:32:31.140 --> 02:32:36.410
Arthur Brooks: Because you said pleasure and
as a defense, et cetera, et cetera.

02:32:36.410 --> 02:32:40.140
But I don't feel like I'm sitting with somebody
who's afraid of suffering.

02:32:40.140 --> 02:32:43.950
Tim Ferriss: It's certainly less than it was.

02:32:43.950 --> 02:32:47.061
I also think that they're just pleasurable
things that I really like.

02:32:47.061 --> 02:32:48.930
Arthur Brooks: Well, there's that.

02:32:48.930 --> 02:32:50.790
Tim Ferriss: There's that.

02:32:50.790 --> 02:32:52.180
Weird, how bizarre.

02:32:52.180 --> 02:32:54.521
There is that —
Arthur Brooks: So I remember reading about

02:32:54.521 --> 02:33:00.030
one of your sexual experiences in The 4-Hour
Body where it was like, "To get more testosterone,

02:33:00.030 --> 02:33:02.500
and then I ate a single Brazil nut."

02:33:02.500 --> 02:33:03.850
And you're —
Tim Ferriss: Oh.

02:33:03.850 --> 02:33:05.930
Yeah, it was lots of Brazil nuts, cholesterol
loading.

02:33:05.930 --> 02:33:07.750
I did lots of crazy stuff in The 4-Hour Body.

02:33:07.750 --> 02:33:10.730
Arthur Brooks: But it was somebody who knew
how to have fun.

02:33:10.730 --> 02:33:12.110
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, that chapter.

02:33:12.110 --> 02:33:13.490
Arthur Brooks: How old were you?

02:33:13.490 --> 02:33:15.810
What, 34 or something when you —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, early thirties.

02:33:15.810 --> 02:33:16.810
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:33:16.810 --> 02:33:17.810
Yeah, yeah.

02:33:17.810 --> 02:33:20.520
So anyway, no, I respect the fact that you
like to have fun and you like to feel good.

02:33:20.520 --> 02:33:23.120
I get that, but I don't sense fear from you.

02:33:23.120 --> 02:33:24.120
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:33:24.120 --> 02:33:32.760
I would also say what I have done is I've
built into my life a lot of premeditated,

02:33:32.760 --> 02:33:40.510
deliberate discomfort to inoculate myself
against the fear of unpredictable discomfort.

02:33:40.510 --> 02:33:43.410
And it's not a cure-all, but I have found
that —

02:33:43.410 --> 02:33:44.840
Arthur Brooks: It's exposure therapy.

02:33:44.840 --> 02:33:51.800
Tim Ferriss: It's exposure therapy, also just
wadding into some of the deep waters psychologically

02:33:51.800 --> 02:33:56.040
and psycho-emotionally that I would be prone
to fearing.

02:33:56.040 --> 02:34:00.601
Learning how to take swimming lessons in some
of those deep waters, I would say is also

02:34:00.601 --> 02:34:02.061
an approach.

02:34:02.061 --> 02:34:12.840
So, it resembles exposure therapy, but there's
also a skill development piece on top of that,

02:34:12.840 --> 02:34:15.940
which is combined with the exposure I would
say, which I know is a bit nebulous.

02:34:15.940 --> 02:34:19.420
Arthur Brooks: No, no, but it makes perfect
sense and there's actually a way that we can

02:34:19.420 --> 02:34:21.689
all get better at that, because I know a lot
of people are watching this like, "Yeah.

02:34:21.689 --> 02:34:22.840
How do we get better at that?"

02:34:22.840 --> 02:34:26.729
Here's one way to do that that actually is
a very practical way to do it, start each

02:34:26.729 --> 02:34:30.149
day with a statement of fact and then an aspiration.

02:34:30.149 --> 02:34:33.120
The statement of fact is, "I don't know what's
going to happen this day.

02:34:33.120 --> 02:34:34.300
I don't know."

02:34:34.300 --> 02:34:39.770
I learned this from a pediatric oncologist,
by the way, somebody who gives bad cancer

02:34:39.770 --> 02:34:42.350
diagnoses to kids.

02:34:42.350 --> 02:34:48.180
He says he tells the parents to every day
start the day saying, "I don't know what's

02:34:48.180 --> 02:34:53.830
going to happen in my future, but I do know
I am alive this day and I'm deeply grateful

02:34:53.830 --> 02:34:56.950
for everything that happens for good and for
bad."

02:34:56.950 --> 02:35:02.170
It takes cojones, man.

02:35:02.170 --> 02:35:07.850
It's hard because you're like, you catch your
breath a little bit.

02:35:07.850 --> 02:35:11.050
Because what Mother Nature wants you to do
is to look for the good feelings and to avoid

02:35:11.050 --> 02:35:15.439
the bad feelings, because that's the evolutionary
imperative, to avoid the disgust and the anger

02:35:15.439 --> 02:35:19.760
and the sadness and the grief and the loneliness,
and to avoid those things, not to embrace

02:35:19.760 --> 02:35:24.720
those things to say it's inevitable, bring
it on.

02:35:24.720 --> 02:35:31.251
But if you can actually do that to steal yourself,
to steal — as they say in Isaiah, "I steel

02:35:31.251 --> 02:35:35.550
my face like flint."

02:35:35.550 --> 02:35:36.550
And that's how to do it.

02:35:36.550 --> 02:35:39.890
"Look, I don't know what's going to happen
today, but I do want to know I'm alive right

02:35:39.890 --> 02:35:40.890
now.

02:35:40.890 --> 02:35:41.890
I'm not going to waste this day.

02:35:41.890 --> 02:35:46.220
And the way I don't waste this day is by being
grateful for every single thing that happens,

02:35:46.220 --> 02:35:47.220
good and bad.

02:35:47.220 --> 02:35:48.220
Bring it on.

02:35:48.220 --> 02:35:49.220
Here we go."

02:35:49.220 --> 02:35:56.910
Tim Ferriss: It's a gift I received quite
a while ago, but actually — steel my face

02:35:56.910 --> 02:35:57.910
like flint.

02:35:57.910 --> 02:36:02.301
It is a piece of, I think it is steel, and
it's engraved with a quote from, I think it

02:36:02.301 --> 02:36:04.750
is Neale Donald Walsch.

02:36:04.750 --> 02:36:06.990
I may be getting that off.

02:36:06.990 --> 02:36:07.990
Someone can fact check.

02:36:07.990 --> 02:36:13.490
But the quote is, "The struggle ends when
the gratitude begins."

02:36:13.490 --> 02:36:16.390
And it's such good advice.

02:36:16.390 --> 02:36:17.390
Yeah.

02:36:17.390 --> 02:36:21.180
And you don't have to be grateful only for
the obviously good things.

02:36:21.180 --> 02:36:22.180
Arthur Brooks: No, no, no, no.

02:36:22.180 --> 02:36:23.180
On the contrary.

02:36:23.180 --> 02:36:29.710
And that's really, that's what separates first-course
gratitude from PhD-level stuff is saying — and

02:36:29.710 --> 02:36:34.210
by the way, here's a practical way to do that,
to be grateful for bad things, a very practical

02:36:34.210 --> 02:36:35.210
way to do it.

02:36:35.210 --> 02:36:37.490
I ask my students to make a failure journal.

02:36:37.490 --> 02:36:41.510
And so what that is that every time bad thing
happens, something bad happens frequently.

02:36:41.510 --> 02:36:44.620
When you're 28 years old, somebody breaks
your heart today and tomorrow you’re going

02:36:44.620 --> 02:36:45.630
to be on a test.

02:36:45.630 --> 02:36:48.850
And the next day after that, you don't get
the interview for that job you hoped for.

02:36:48.850 --> 02:36:54.290
And it's just a constant string of disappointments
and thrills because it's like when you're

02:36:54.290 --> 02:36:55.510
28 years old.

02:36:55.510 --> 02:37:00.610
So, every time something happens that really
frustrates and disappoints you or you screw

02:37:00.610 --> 02:37:05.149
up or whatever, you take out your failure
journal and you write in your failure journal

02:37:05.149 --> 02:37:06.149
what happened.

02:37:06.149 --> 02:37:10.740
And then you leave two lines open behind it,
and you put reminders on your phone, ding,

02:37:10.740 --> 02:37:13.680
one month from now you've got to go back and
six months from now.

02:37:13.680 --> 02:37:17.970
One month from now, you've got to go back
and say what you learned from that thing that

02:37:17.970 --> 02:37:19.160
you would forget otherwise.

02:37:19.160 --> 02:37:22.359
And six months from now, you've got to go
back and say a good thing that happened because

02:37:22.359 --> 02:37:23.500
of that thing.

02:37:23.500 --> 02:37:26.290
And there's always entries, always, always,
always.

02:37:26.290 --> 02:37:31.010
And so it's like, "I went into my performance
review and I thought I was doing a really

02:37:31.010 --> 02:37:32.010
good job.

02:37:32.010 --> 02:37:36.080
My boss basically told me that I'm a B player
at best."

02:37:36.080 --> 02:37:38.090
This happens constantly.

02:37:38.090 --> 02:37:41.670
"That bums me out and I just want to put it
behind me, and I want to go hang out with

02:37:41.670 --> 02:37:45.590
my friends and have a couple of beers and
complain about my boss and move on."

02:37:45.590 --> 02:37:46.819
No, no, no, no.

02:37:46.819 --> 02:37:49.790
Write it on your failure journal one month
from now, remember it and go back.

02:37:49.790 --> 02:37:50.790
"Huh?

02:37:50.790 --> 02:37:55.271
You know what I learned from that is I thought
I was going to be super bummed about that

02:37:55.271 --> 02:37:58.740
for a long time, and it bothered me for a
week."

02:37:58.740 --> 02:38:00.120
That's interesting.

02:38:00.120 --> 02:38:01.120
Now that's homeostasis.

02:38:01.120 --> 02:38:02.810
There's a lot of brain science in that.

02:38:02.810 --> 02:38:07.170
Six months later, you come back and you say,
"When I thought about that, I realized that

02:38:07.170 --> 02:38:10.150
that probably wasn't the perfect fit for my
career.

02:38:10.150 --> 02:38:14.750
And I went on the market and I have a better
job now.

02:38:14.750 --> 02:38:17.430
I think that the job I have now is a better
fit."

02:38:17.430 --> 02:38:21.171
And every single thing that happens that you
put into your failure journal, you will realize

02:38:21.171 --> 02:38:24.680
that there's something generative that happens
from this in terms of learning and in terms

02:38:24.680 --> 02:38:25.810
of gratitude.

02:38:25.810 --> 02:38:29.260
And you will turn that thing into something
— sooner or later when something really

02:38:29.260 --> 02:38:33.300
bad happens to you, you're going to be like,
"Oh, God, I get to put it in the failure journal."

02:38:33.300 --> 02:38:37.609
It'll really change your perspective on it,
because failure and disappointment and frustration

02:38:37.609 --> 02:38:42.040
and sacrifice and pain will take on their
proper perspective, which is part of your

02:38:42.040 --> 02:38:43.140
full life.

02:38:43.140 --> 02:38:45.770
Tim Ferriss: We shall see.

02:38:45.770 --> 02:38:46.770
That's great.

02:38:46.770 --> 02:38:47.770
We shall see.

02:38:47.770 --> 02:38:48.770
That's terrible.

02:38:48.770 --> 02:38:49.770
Arthur Brooks: I know.

02:38:49.770 --> 02:38:50.770
The famous parallel.

02:38:50.770 --> 02:38:51.770
Tim Ferriss: We shall see.

02:38:51.770 --> 02:38:52.770
Arthur Brooks: We shall see that famous parallel.

02:38:52.770 --> 02:38:53.770
Tim Ferriss: We shall see.

02:38:53.770 --> 02:38:54.770
So true.

02:38:54.770 --> 02:38:55.770
Arthur Brooks: Yeah.

02:38:55.770 --> 02:38:56.770
Tim Ferriss: Well, Arthur, this has been so
much fun.

02:38:56.770 --> 02:39:00.149
Now, the title of the book is —
Arthur Brooks: Build the Life You Want: The

02:39:00.149 --> 02:39:01.950
Art and Science of Getting Happier.

02:39:01.950 --> 02:39:05.950
Tim Ferriss: And by the time this comes up,
people will be able to certainly find it and

02:39:05.950 --> 02:39:06.950
purchase it.

02:39:06.950 --> 02:39:07.950
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, yeah.

02:39:07.950 --> 02:39:08.950
"Where all fine books are sold" is the saying.

02:39:08.950 --> 02:39:10.130
Tim Ferriss: Where all fine books —
Arthur Brooks: And also, if people like, Oprah

02:39:10.130 --> 02:39:12.060
and I will read it to you.

02:39:12.060 --> 02:39:17.521
Tim Ferriss: You mean, I'm guessing, in audiobook
form, not on Cameo.

02:39:17.521 --> 02:39:24.120
Arthur Brooks: Look, no, we'll come to your
house and with dulcet tones of Oprah's voice,

02:39:24.120 --> 02:39:25.500
we'll lull you to sleep now.

02:39:25.500 --> 02:39:26.500
No, I mean, we read it.

02:39:26.500 --> 02:39:27.840
Tim Ferriss: You could raise a lot of money
that way.

02:39:27.840 --> 02:39:29.240
Arthur Brooks: Yeah, that's right.

02:39:29.240 --> 02:39:33.910
"Sorry, Oprah, I committed us to a thing.

02:39:33.910 --> 02:39:36.720
Let's just say the book tour is taking on
new dimensions."

02:39:36.720 --> 02:39:41.810
But we read the book for Penguin Random House,
and I'm really super happy with the way that

02:39:41.810 --> 02:39:46.471
it turned out, mostly because my voice is
interspersed with her beautiful voice, one

02:39:46.471 --> 02:39:47.720
of the most famous voices in America.

02:39:47.720 --> 02:39:49.180
Tim Ferriss: What an incredible experience.

02:39:49.180 --> 02:39:50.180
Congratulations.

02:39:50.180 --> 02:39:51.180
Arthur Brooks: Thank you.

02:39:51.180 --> 02:39:55.140
Tim Ferriss: So people can find the book where
all fine books are sold.

02:39:55.140 --> 02:39:57.180
I just love saying that.

02:39:57.180 --> 02:40:01.210
And is there anything else you'd like to point
people to, whether it's a social profile or

02:40:01.210 --> 02:40:06.220
a request of the audience, an ask of the audience,
something you'd like to close with, anything

02:40:06.220 --> 02:40:08.120
at all that you'd like to add before we wind
to a close?

02:40:08.120 --> 02:40:13.180
Arthur Brooks: This is really a teaching experience,
both for me and Oprah and just part of my

02:40:13.180 --> 02:40:16.660
life, which is dedicated to writing, speaking,
and teaching about love and happiness, to

02:40:16.660 --> 02:40:20.370
bring people together in bonds of love, using
the science and ideas.

02:40:20.370 --> 02:40:22.000
That's really my mission statement for the
rest of my life.

02:40:22.000 --> 02:40:24.850
And that really is a teaching mission, just
like yours is a teaching mission.

02:40:24.850 --> 02:40:29.689
So there's a lot of stuff that I'm doing ancillary
to books and columns, et cetera.

02:40:29.689 --> 02:40:33.590
I have classes like video classes and things
that people can watch.

02:40:33.590 --> 02:40:37.600
And my goal in doing those things — this
all on my website, arthurbrooks.com, but when

02:40:37.600 --> 02:40:42.340
people do those things, my goal is training
them to be happiness teachers.

02:40:42.340 --> 02:40:47.370
This is really what it's all about because
remember, it's understand, change your habits,

02:40:47.370 --> 02:40:48.430
share with others.

02:40:48.430 --> 02:40:52.640
And so to learn more about exactly how to
do that, we're developing a lot of resources

02:40:52.640 --> 02:40:56.170
that make that possible in, I think, a pretty
effective way.

02:40:56.170 --> 02:41:02.140
And I would love people to do that for the
single reason that I want a movement.

02:41:02.140 --> 02:41:07.561
I want to be part of a movement of people
whose hobby is the science of happiness and

02:41:07.561 --> 02:41:10.149
bringing it to others.

02:41:10.149 --> 02:41:13.853
There's a lot of people who are broken in
this world and who are sad and who are suffering.

02:41:13.853 --> 02:41:18.189
And if we had people who are warriors for
greater happiness for themselves through others,

02:41:18.189 --> 02:41:24.600
through real knowledge and a commitment to
change, I'm truly convinced that this is the

02:41:24.600 --> 02:41:28.370
one thing that I can do that could have an
impact on the world that needs to be a lot

02:41:28.370 --> 02:41:29.370
happier.

02:41:29.370 --> 02:41:35.630
Tim Ferriss: Arthur, so glad that we were
able to find the time, have this conversation,

02:41:35.630 --> 02:41:36.630
and I admire the work you do.

02:41:36.630 --> 02:41:37.780
I respect the work you do.

02:41:37.780 --> 02:41:39.630
I value the work that you put out in the world.

02:41:39.630 --> 02:41:40.750
It does help people.

02:41:40.750 --> 02:41:46.380
So I wanted to also just simply thank you
for putting out what you put out and spending

02:41:46.380 --> 02:41:48.500
time on the things you spend time on.

02:41:48.500 --> 02:41:49.670
Arthur Brooks: Likewise, Tim.

02:41:49.670 --> 02:41:54.000
I've only met you today in person, but I feel
like I've known you for a long time because

02:41:54.000 --> 02:41:57.680
I've been consuming your work like so many
millions of other people, and you've enriched

02:41:57.680 --> 02:41:58.680
my life a lot.

02:41:58.680 --> 02:41:59.680
Thank you for that.

02:41:59.680 --> 02:42:00.680
Tim Ferriss: Thanks, man.

02:42:00.680 --> 02:42:01.680
I really appreciate it.

02:42:01.680 --> 02:42:02.850
This has really sparked a lot in me.

02:42:02.850 --> 02:42:08.540
I've taken copious notes, so I have a number
of things that I'll be focusing on: getting

02:42:08.540 --> 02:42:13.859
small, Brothers K, muscular philosophies,
I just love the phrasing, so I wrote it down,

02:42:13.859 --> 02:42:20.029
Aristotle, Aquinas, all things that can lead
you to build the life that you want.

02:42:20.029 --> 02:42:24.210
And for everybody listening, we'll have extensive
show notes, links to everything as usual at

02:42:24.210 --> 02:42:26.109
tim.blog/podcast.

02:42:26.109 --> 02:42:31.229
And until next time, be just a bit kinder
than is necessary to others and to yourself,

02:42:31.229 --> 02:42:32.920
and thanks for tuning in.

